        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Royal Plate.—All the stories that are told about the supposed solid gold dinner service at Windsor Castle are mere fiction. There is no such thing in existence as the solid gold service, and the pieces of solid gold plate at the Royal palaces, and in private possession probably do not number altogether as many as three figures. The so-called gold service, which is used on State occasions at Windsor or Buckingham Palace is silver gilt, but at the same time, most of it is probably worthmore than its weight in gold. It never of course pays probate duty, as the King does not pay the King's taxes, and there is no official record of its value. But if a valuation was made, there is little doubt that it would appraise the Royal plate far in</p>
        <p>a sweepstake and may be the means of encouraging gambling amongst women. Sensational Fiction.—A well-known Scottish divine has been grieving over the feminine taste for unwhole- some reading, and no one can deny that the demand for sensational fiction has grown to an extent undreamt of years ago. The sensational element in novels that were popular 20 or 30 years ago. would now appear tame and commonplace. That however only makes it the more surprising that Dr. Story should associate the works of the late Mrs. Henry Wood, and another distinguished authoress who is living with what he calls "sensational rubbish'," and injurious to the minds of young women. Mrs. Henry Wood was one of the pioneers of popular literature, and she was regarded as one of the best writers of wholesome reading for the home circle. Mrs. Burnett Smith (Annie S. Swan) has taken up the cudgels on her own behalf, and for the late Mrs. Henry Wood, and most readers of the works of these two gifted authoresses will agree that Dr. Story made an unhappy selection when he wished to point the moral of her protest. MADGE.</p>
        <p>excess of a million pods. A Slack Season.—The Royal entertainments at Windsor, and the visit of the King and Queen of Italy, gave a much-needed fillip to what the dressmakers one and all describe as an unusually slack season. The modistes were disappointed in their hope that a State ball would form part of the Royal entertaining of the King and Queen of Italy, but at least some orders for smart evening toilettes have resulted from the enter- tainments at Windsor, and those to be given at Sandring- ham in celebration of Queen Alexandra's birthday. Very frequently sheep leave the tops of moors when Early in December,the Court will return to Buckingham snow is not far distant. Poultry are most noisy the Palace for the Christmas shopping. Tradespeople are night before rain. Horses often shudder a few seconds hoping that this may come to the rescue of a bad season before a flash of lightning. So with man. People who but it is common to hear them remark that "there is no I have rheumatism can often tell when rain is approach- money in London, or in the country." One reason for i ing. If, however, a course of 'Charles Forde's bile this complaint is undoubtedly the large shrinkage in beans be taken, the bones and joints cease Stock Exchange values of financial securities, and Ito ache. Ellis Hughes, who lives in Castletown another which affects the dressmakers and general em- 1 Road, Moss (Wrexham), always suffered severe poriums, is, that so many people are spending their 1 rheumatic pains along the back and in the loins . and spare money on motor cars and motor cycles, thighs whenever any damp weather was approaching, The Victorian Christmas.—An interesting book of the , and often when it was not damp. He says :—" I con- season is a collection of some of the:writings of Dickens: suited two medical men, well known in this district, on the subject of Christmas. Thackeray, and many , but they could do nothing for me. My condition other authors, have wiitten "Chris