        <p>, this subject. I a new man of me." The Christmas Pantomime.—lt seemed a few years i This is no isolated instance of their wonderful power. ago that the Christmas pantomime would gradually die All chemists now stock this great family medicine, the out, at least in London, but the large number of theatres prices being one-and-three-halfpence and two-and-nine that have sprung up in the suburbs of the metropolis, per box. appear to have revived the popularity of this class of entertainment. Al no less than 27 of these suburban theatres will Christmas pantomimes be produced, and; there will be four at the Central London theatres. That I popular favourite Mr. Dan Leno will not, for the first ; time for many years, appear in the pantomime at Drury- I lane; which is relying upon " Humpty Dumpty "as the SACRED HEART CATHOLIC CLUB v. THE subject for its production. But it matters not as to I LEAGUE OF' THE CROSS. mho or what provides the story. One always expects i This return match was played at the Sacred Heart the" book "to be a farrago of nonsense, but it must bring out the chief events of the year, and catch the Club, and ended in a victory for the home club by 144. Score : humour of the moment. It is that and the spectacular ,- attractions, upon which the success of the pantomime ,' P. George SACRED IFIEART. LEA.GUE OF THE CROSS. depends. 100 S. Millington 42 The Pioneer of the Blouse.—The visit of the King J. Gibbin% D. Noon lOO J. Wilkinson and Queen of Italy recalls the fact that the blouse was, 100 G. Green 73 in the first instance, the result of the visit to this coun_ H. Paton lOO J. Richardson try of the great patriot, General Garibaldi. His T. Corless 74 D. Darby lOO picturesque appearance led at once to the adoption of A. Corless 100 .1. Mather the flannel ' shirt-waist " or "Garibaldi," which was P. Whelan lOO H Walsh BB really a loose and simple make of blouse. After some A. Jones 99 T. McCormack years of public favour, these garibaldis disappeare