      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>SHEEP AS WEATHER PROPHETS.</head>
        <p>SHEEP AS WEATHER PROPHETS.</p>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, November 28, 1903.</p>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>" Imitation is sincerest flattery,". we are told and if this be so then may we with good grace adorn ourselves with some of the won- derfully clever cc unterparts of the most fashionable furs which are now to be got at less than half the price of the real. peltry. The plush which takes the place of the vel- vety moleskin, or curly caracul makes most charming coats and pelerines, and is also used in the form of bands on skirts and coats, while it is also often employed for the crowns of smart hats or for the stylish toques so modishly worn this season. These latter require very little additional trimming and a wing or an aigrette is all that is necessary,</p>
        <p>held in place by an ornamental cabuchon. These imitation furs do not weigh nearly so heaviy as the real articless and are therefore more suited for millinery purposes as they are more comfortably worn. The prettiest toques just now have a brim which fits the head dos* where it rests on the hair, and slope thence outwards to the top. The brim also is -so fashioned as to project considerably over the face in front while it is rather slanted away at the back. An edging of real or imitation fur is a great improvement to a brim of this style. The smart hat depicted is of dark red felt trimmed with a grey and white bird which rests in a swathing of spotted chiffon. The veil is crossed at the back and brought round the throat to the side, where it is coquettishly tied in a careless bow with flowing ends. A veil is not easily arranged successfully in this style, but when it is done the effect is excellent and will give a wonder- fully smart look to the tout ensemble. Th veil, which hangs loosely from the brim of the hat, has not as yet taken on on this side of the Channel, and, indeed, is hardly suited to the</p>
        <p>THE FR</p>
        <p>A kIOPEFUL SIGN.</p>
        <p>The letter of the Archbishop of Canterbury to Dr. 'Horton and the latter's reply have excited a great deal of comment. In Free Church circles it is felt that the Archbishop's communication is a sign that the Church party have opened their eyes to the fact that the "settlement "of the Education Question by the Acts of 1902 and 1903 cannot be regarded as final. Indeed, many loyal Churchmen have long since recognised that the recent legislation has done more harm than good to the Church. The Archbishop, as a prudent man, no doubt, was right in trying to come to some compromise with the Nonconformists now that the latter are on the eve of almost certain victory. Dr. Horton's action in the matter is to be highly commended. If he had accepted the Arch- bishop's proposals and agreed to a compromise without reservation, there might have been caused a split in thp Free Church ranks. As it was, Dr. Horton met nif,ny of the leaders of the Free Church I‘l3vement, and the action which he took had the approval of those who have to a large extent conducted the campaign. -</p>
