      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>LATEST FUR STOLE:</head>
        <p>THE LATEST FUR STOLE:</p>
        <p>English taste in dress, which aims at a certain neatness and trimness. There are some very pretty veilings this season, most of which show large or graduated sizes cf velvet spots. One has, however, to be careful as to where these spots come on the face as otherwise the [effect is ugly and a pretty mouth made to look large or a delicately arched eyebrow irregular. 'There is a decided leaning towards all that is military, both in dress and millinery, as illustrated by the Guards coat, or in the other instance, by the Hussar aigrette, or the smart zebeline toques with their clusters of black tassels round the brim.</p>
        <p>The charming fur wrap shown in the illus- tration is a marked feature of dress this winter. It spreads on the shoulders to the dimensions of a deep collar or in some in- stances to that of a cape, while a group of tails, completed by a little animal's head, is arranged at the point where the collar or cape joins the long stole ends. These in turn are finished at the lower ends by a fringe of fur tails. One can obtain a stole of this style in almost any fur, real or imitation, and at prices to suit all purses and tastes. Fur tails are also much employed onthe large muffs which are worn this winter, and certainly look very smart. They are also sometimes used to trim</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>PRETTY BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>the lower edge of a fur cape or even on the skirts of smart toilettes. Of course in this case, as in that of many other fashionable novelties, manufactured fur tails are em- ployed and are shaped and dyed so as to be difficult to be distinguished from the real. Each ensuing year finds our fursmore beauti- ful than the one before, and one cannot but marvel at the lovely articles of attire in various skins which can be obtained for a very reasonable amount.</p>
        <p>The pretty evening blouse sketched is of pale blue accordion-pleated silk over a tight- fitting lining. The style is a simple one, and an old evening bodice could always be utilised for the lining as the simulated coat conceals it from view. Silk pleated in this style' can be bought for rather less than wo shillings a yard, but it must be borne in mind that it is measured when the silk is stretched plainly and not pleated, so that a considerable quan- tity is required. It, however, is more bulky than accordion - pleated chiffon and likewise wears very much longer than the more fragile material. A little ribbon, and a dainty touch here rind there of tulle or chiffon, is all that is required as ,trimming, so that an old bodice could easily be entirely renovated by a home worker of average ability. Vapourine is a material which is used a good deal this season for evening dresses and is more durable than the more diaphanous materials such as tulle or chiffon. Sequins are also still much in evidence and sparkle and glitter on many dainty and lovely gowns.</p>
        <p>CoguErrsi</p>
        <p>, k \ . !5,.. 'w ' le6s' II -4) /)0 lio</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>FREE CHURCH CAMPAIGN FUND.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH CAMPAIGN FUND.</p>
        <p>It is apparent •tkit a great deal will have to be done to insure such a victory at the next General Election as to once and for all put Education upon a National basis. Already the need is being felt for the Campaign Fund which the National Free Church Council is raising. The Press of Mr. Chamberlain and the leagues with which he is connected are pouring out tons of literature on the Fiscal Question. The Free fooders and the Liberal party are replying in quantities equally voluminous. Under these circumstances it is absolutely necessary that the Education Question be brought before the attention of the electors, and this is being done by the circulation of millions of leaflets, &amp;c. This, of course, is very costly work, as the National Free Church Council supplies the leaflets gratis. The ordinary work of the Council has to be carried forward simultaneously with the Education Campaign, so that the funds for the latter must be additional to the general revenue. The extraordinary situation has demanded the organised action of a body with purely spiritual aims, and it is a sign of the in- tensely earnest spirit with which Free Churchmen regard the matter that contributions are being sent in in such generous numbers. Not nearly the sum required has yet been obtained, how- ever, and an urgent appeal is being addressed to Free Churchmen to send donations to the Rev. Thomas Law, Memorial Hall, Farringdon- street, E.C. "NO NONCONFORAI ST NEED APPLY?'</p>
        <p>An illustration of the principle that "No Nonconformist need apply" to many of our State-maintained schools appears every week in the advertisement columns of the scholastic papers. Under the heading of various Educa- tion Committees we read of situations being vacant, the applicant for which having to be "Churchman," "Churchwoman," &amp;c. Even in cases where this is not definitely stated one can guess how much chance a Nonconformist appli- cant would stand. For instance, in the "School- master" for November 14 there are no less 140 advertisements where applicatinim to be made to the vicar. 'On, 1. une heading, "West Sussex E4,icati 011 Committee," we read that an assistant mistress is required at Heyshott (Na- tional) Voluntary School. She must be "an earnest Churchwoman." What "earnest "means can be understood from the boast of some of the clergy that they are turning the children of Dissent into good Catholics. Some advertise- ments go farther and point to the work which must be done in connection with the Church by the teachers in the school. It was said in the scholastic profession that the Education Act would have the effect of doing away with the extraneous duties which teachers in Sun- day Schools in rural parts have to perform, such as playing the organ, &amp;c. It would not seem, however, that the Act has resulted thus. For instance, a mixed school (St. Stephen's, St. Albans) advertised in the same issue of the " Schoolmaster " for a mistress. We have the comforting assurance there are no big boys. and that a mistress is kept. Further, it is added, "One hour Sunday School, except holidays." The salary to be paid is a year, this, pre- sumably, to include the Sunday School. No one could object to these things if the schools were privately supported; but, under the Education Act, the whole of the salaries of these teachers are paid out of rates and taxes, and they are therefore public servants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>UNIQUE EDUCATIONAL EXPERIMENT.</head>
        <p>A UNIQUE EDUCATIONAL EXPERIMENT.</p>
        <p>This month's Free Church Chronicle contains an article on the new settlement at Wood- brooke, Birmingham, which is being instituted under the auspices of the Friends' Summer School Committee. Mr. George Cadbury, with characteristic genlorosity, has placed his former residence near Selly Oak at the disposal of the Committee, and arrangements have been made for a permanent educational settlement where those desiring to have more perfect knowledge for Christian work may reside. No limit 19 placed on age, denomination, or sex. SOME FORTHCOMING PUBLICATIONS.</p>
        <p>The National Free church Council's Publica- tion Department have a promising list of fo?th- coming publications, which include two series of handsome little books issued at the popular price of is. 6cl. The first will be entitled "Eras of Nonconformity," and will be under the general editorship of the Rev. C. Silvester Horne, the other —" Helps to the Devout Life," the Rev. F. B. Meyer is editing. It is expected that a full list of the titles and authors will shortly be published. The latter, it is understood, include the most brilliant writers among the Free Churches. The Rev. J. H. Jowett is publishing through the National Free Church Council a volume containing prayers for every day of the year. It will be entitled "Yet Another Day," and will be issued in a very dainty form.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>GIRLS' GUILD.</head>
        <p>THE GIRLS' GUILD.</p>
        <p>The work of the Free Church Girls' Guild goes on in a steady but very progressive manner. In several large towns Guild homes have recently been taken and furnished for the purpose of the meetings and social intercourse of the members of the Guilds. A handsome home has been established, for instance' at Sheffield, and was opened recently by the Lord Mayor of that city. It has just been decided to acquire a house for a similar purpose at Bath. A very interesting article appears in this month's Free Churchman, entitled "An Evening with the Girls' Guild." The article describes the varied forms of work and entertainments which are employed in connection with the Girls' Guild. Mrs. Charlotte Benham, the Organising Secretary of the Movement, has been visiting many parts of the country of late in connection with the work, and it is probable that many additional Guilds will be formed in s ‘llll5ll Nam</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>(Copyright.) THE AMERICAN DUEL.</head>
        <p>(Copyright.) THE AMERICAN DUEL.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN K. LEYS,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Maiden's Victory," "An Emanci- pated Woman," "In the Grey of the Dawning," "In the Toils," "A Man of Millions," "A Broken Fetter," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Pour men sat round a ttible in a small private room in an immense New York hotel. Two of them were Spanish-Americans, the third a Californian, and the fourth an Englishman. The American was slowly dealing out a pack of cards, the other three were watching him in silence. The silence was profound. The dealer was not distributing the cards all round, but dealing them into two packets, he for the Englishman on his right, the other' for the younger of the two Spanish-Americans, irho sat on his left. And he dealt them face upwards. There was little wonder that all three held their peace, and stared at the cards as if fascinated by them, for a human life was trembling in the balance. The cards fell with mechanical precision: Left—right— left—right—black—red—red—red—black. The man, to whom the ace of spades fell was to die! Of course, a woman was at the bottom of it. Maud Guilfoyle was the belle of New York that winter, and she was the orphan' daughter of a millionaire. In spite of her wealth Maud was simple-mannered, light-hearted creature, who took the world as she found it, and found it delightful. Suitors she had by the score, and , it was notorious that she .had at one time seemed to prefer.. Henriquez Be Quelfa to any other of her admirers, but since Fred Sefton, with his frank, easy-going ways .appeared on the -scene, the half- breed's star had waned. Jealousy, bitter and keen as the hatred of brothers, had' been the result.</p>
        <p>One by one the cards fell: Left—right—left— right: and still the dreaded ace did not come. The restraints of civilisation had long prevented an outbreak, but some chance .word had fired the gunpowder, and in a moment there was an explo- sion. Insult followed insult, till at last the half- breed struck the Englishman on the cheek, and Sefton promptly knocked him down. The blood was still on his chin.</p>
        <p>Left—right—left—right—left. Would that card never come?</p>
        <p>Of course, there had been talk .of a meeting, but there were difficulties, the laws of the State of New York amongst others; and at length it has been settled that the matter should be left to the arbitra- ment of what has been called "The American Duel." The seconds were -both men to whom gambling was the very breath of life, and it seemed to them the fairest way of settling the dispute. Sefton was secretly horrified at the idea, but he lacked the courage to decline the ordeal. It was indeed shocking to his English sense of what was right and decent.. He had pledged his honour that if the fatal card were dealt to him he would take away his own life within six hours of receiving notice from his antagonist that he called on him to fulfil his pledge, and, of course, the Spanish- American had made a similar promise. Further- more, both parties had bound themselves that they would not appeal to the law in any case; and it was understood that in the impossible event of either party refusing to kill himself if fate and the laws of honour called upon him to do so, the other was to be at liberty to shoot him at sight, or put him to death in any other way that might be convenient. There was little wonder that Sefton's breath came in great labouring gasps, and that a clammy sweat gathered on his forehead, as he saw one red card fall here, and jpne there, and a black one here and a red one there. Would the ace never come?</p>
        <p>Of course, if he were the lucky one he would tell the blackguard that he might go to the deuce his own way, without helping or prompting from him. He was not going to be a sort of murderer. But if the ace should fall to him?-</p>
        <p>The suspense was beginning to tell on the nerves of the man who dealt the cards. He paused an instant to rest the side of his left hand against the edge of the table, and drew another card, dealing it to Sefton.</p>
        <p>It was the ace of 'spades t "I guess that's final," said the Californian, leaning back in his chair. Sefton sat and looked at the card without speaking. His face was very white. He was thinking of the mother that awaited him in England.</p>
        <p>He looked up, and saw that the eyes of his enemy were fixed on him, and that they wore a smile of triumph. "You needn't be afraid that I will not keep my word," he said, hoarsely. He had been brought up in that false school of morals that puts faith to a man's pledged word above all other considera- tions, above every law, human or divine, and it never occurred to him that he might save his life by breaking his promise. "I shall be dead within the time named—six hours, I think." "Excuse me," said De Quelfa's second. "The arrangement was that you were to await the signal from my principal before becoming your own executioner. lam right, am I not?" he added, turning to the American. "You are right," said Colonel Coleworthy, with a grave nod. "At the same time, if your man is a gentleman, as I take him to be, he will not pile up the agony too high, but will give notice in a polite and gentlemanly style at an early date." The young Englishman gave his friend a grate- ful look, and the party broke up, the t‘v Spanish- Americans bowing with punctilious politeness as they left the room. Young Sef: on spent half the-night in writing letters and makinc, his will; he had not very much to leave, poor fellow. Then he went to bed and tried to sleep, but could not. He fully expected that a- summons to end his life would come up with his other letters in the morning. But there was no such missive, and after hanging about the hotel in uncertainty for an hour or two Sefton went off to a sailing-match in which he took some interest. In the afternoon he did not go back to his hotel, though he had little doubt that the fatal message was awaiting him there. He went and called on Maud Guilfoyle. To his delight Maud allowed him to take her out driving that afternoon. He dined with her and her aunt, and went back to his hotel feeling that if he was to die that night he had, at all events, had a pleasant day. But there was no letter for him in the office at his hotel. And none arrived for him next day. When a week had gone by, bringing no word from his rival, Sefton came to the conclusion that the Spanish fellow had thought better of it. When it came to the point he could not write the cold- blooded words that were to kill a fellow-creature, body and soul. And this view of the matter was confirmed by the fact that De Quelfa and his friend had apparently left the city. They were not to be heard of anywhere. So Sefton thought the best thing he could do was to forget the unpleasant episode. He went on witit his wooing, and to all appearance his wooing prospered. 'At length the time came when Fred thought he might venture to put the all- important question. He did so, and almost as much to his surprise as his delight got for answer a faintly whispered "Yes."</p>
        <p>For the next few weeks the world was an enchanted garden. Maud was the sweetest as well as the prettiest of sweethearts, and for the first time in his life Fred began to understand what it must be to be the recipient of a princely income. The sums dealt with by way of settlement were immense—those of Fred's side, though respectable in themselves, shewed quite paltry by comparison; and the bridal - presents that arrived every hour seemed more fit ifor a :royal palace than for the home of a plain citizen. The happy clay drew near; and after long deliberation it was settled that the first part of the honeymoon should be spent in visiting Niagara and the Thousand islands, after which would Come the inevitable voyage to the Mersey. By this time the American duel had faded into the dimness of the remote past. It was not yet twelve months since the might when the cards were dealt out one by one to Fred and to his rival, but so much had happened since then that already the episcrde seemed to belong to the' times of his boyish and irresponsible days. When he thought of it it was with a smile of amusement, but he was glad to think that he had seen the last of De Quelfa .04 his companion.</p>
        <p>About a week before the day fixed for the wedding, Fred met in the street the gentleman froth California who had .acted as his second that night in the hotel, and though the young English- man did not yearn after his society, he thought himself bound to treat him as a friend and ask him to the reception which was to be held after the ceremony. "Seems to me that Spanish half=breed ain't doing the square thing by you," said Colonel Cole- worthy, after accepting the invitation. "Really? On the contrary, I feel indebted to him for forgetting—as I am sure he has done—a piece of folly which only two lunatics could have had anything to do with."</p>
        <p>"That's your opinion, is it ? " said the western man, slowly turning his gaze upon the outspoken Britisher. "If I were you, I wouldn't make too sure about that Spanish fellow forgetting anything in the way of blood-letting. Likely as ,not ho'll be down on ye when ye least expect it." "In that case I Shotild pay no .attention to his summons, or whatever he may call it," said Fred, with more heat than he cared to shew. "Of course, it would be out of the question to carry out that absurd agreement." . "And how long do you think it would be, stranger, before that Don had his knife in you, if you played the mean skunk like that? But you can't be in earnest? .By the rolling thunder, if I thought you were meaning to go back on your word like that, I would sight a bead on you myself You were in fun, jest now, weren't you?. Say you were speaking in joke! " The wild dark eyes of the American looked into those' of the youno. Englishman—he was very young—with such a arange, masterful expression, they threatened such measureless contempt for the man who should "go back on his word," that he hastened to assure the Californian that be would be ready to carry out the compact if he were duly summoned to do so; and the two men parted. They did not see one another again till they met in the drawing-room of 3glaud's aunt, Xiss Nora Guilfoyle, on the occasion of the wedding. In accordance with American custom, the ceremony had been performed in the afternoon, and the newly-married pair were re- ceiving the congratulations of the hosts of friends that crowded the spacious rooms. There was nothing in the way of a feast, but refreshments were being dispensed in one of the rooms, and ices and tumblers of champagne and cups of coffee were being carried about in all directions. The bride, who looked a marvel of beduty in her white satin and lace, was chatting with some of her friends close by her husband, when a sudden thought seemed to occur to her, and calling her maid she whispered something in her ear. The girl left the room, and in a few moments returned, bearing in her hand a large square envelope, which she gave to her mistress, who, in turn, handed it to her husband.</p>
        <p>"This was brought to me this morning, Fred," she said, "along with a message to the effect that I must give it to you myself this afternoon, after the ceremony. Is anything wrong? What is making you so pale? " No wonder he was pale. The wretched man knew at once what it was his wife was handing to him. There was a smooth, hard thing, something like a stump of a lead pencil, inside the envelope. As he fingered it he glanced round the room, and his eye fell upon Colonel Coleworthy, who was looking at him in a strange, meaning fashion. And a little behind him were the other two—De Quelfa and his second.</p>
        <p>The young man opened the envelope. It con- tained a sheet of paper and a small glass tube, which held three round 'white globules of the size of small peas. On the sheet of paper was written "The time has come for you to redeem your word, and I beg to remind you that the six hours will begin to run from the time when this note is put into your hands. In order that there may be no difficulty with respect to the means for carrying out your promise, I enclose some carefully prepared pellets, which cannot fail to have the desired effect."</p>
        <p>So this was the 'fiendish revenge which the Spaniard had prepared At the moment of his supreme joy and triumph, he was to fall, like a self-convicted criminal, by his own hand!</p>
        <p>the two half-breeds saw the irresolution in his face, and looked at each other with a world of contempt in their eyes. Fred saw the look and turned to the American Colonel. There was not a vestige of sympathy in his face, nothing but a stern desire that the barbarous compact should be kept. And as Fred stood hesitating something of a wondering disgust came into the Colonel's eyes, as thoug he would say: "Is it possible that the Britisher is going to turn out a coward after all? "</p>
        <p>In an access of fury Fred seized on a cup of coffee that stood near him, and striding up to the Californian, tossed towards him the note he had just received. .The Colonel read it and nodded gravely. Fred crushed the top of the glass tube with his finger and thumb, dropped the three globules into the coffee, stirred it for a moment, and swallowed it. He set the cup down. The two Spanish-Americans, with an expressive look of gratified malice in their faces, bowed and turned to leave the house. Fred took no notice of them. The Colonel came up and held out his hand saying: "You're true grit, Britisher, and that's a fact."</p>
        <p>• Fred turned his back en him. As soon as he could he got out of the crowded room and tried to think what he should do. The horrid taste of the drug was in his mouth, and already his head felt dizzy. Surely it wo ild be best, he said to himself, that he should not take Maud away from her friends. It was terrible to think of her, a bride of a few ,hours, in a strange city, with her husband dead ' before her eyes. It would be doing her a great wrong to go away with her at all. But the carriage would be at the door .in a few minutes. It was impossible, unless he were to tell the whole story (which he had pledged himself not to do), to account for his change of plans. He would have to go to the station at any sate, and then perhaps he would find courage to tell Maud that she must go home again. She could seek a shelter with some friends and go home when the guests had left. But when he and Maud were alone together in the carriage driving to the station, it seemed to him that to insult the girl in that fashion would be worse than any other course. He must at all events set off with her, if it were only to keep gossiping tongues quiet. He took the tickets, therefore, and followed his bride into the private saloon that had been reserved for them, as if nothing were the matter. But his heala was throbbing so that was with difficulty that he could hear what his wifo said to him, or make an intelligible reply. The train started, and Fred made anotlier effort to pull himself together and consider what was best to be done. Who; if he were to become seriously ill there, in the railway carriage? What if he were to die before the train stopped? It was cruel to have risked placinc, an innocent girl in such a position. Plainly she must be told; she must be warned.</p>
        <p>He raised his eyes and looked at her. She was bending over towards him, and by degrees she Blipped from her seat, knelt on the floor of the carriage, and looked up in his face with a curious expression on her face. Dimly Sefton began to understand that some- thing was wrong. Maud wanted to tell him some- thing. At least he got her to speak. "Please forgive me, Fred. 'I will never, never do such a thing again. But it was so tempting ! I opened the letter that came for you this morning. And as I was taking the .letter—what did it mean, Fred ?—out of the envelope, the little glass thing slipped out, and fell down into the courtyard—l was sitting out on the balcony—and was smashed into a thousand bits. I had just had time to see that it held three little white things like pills, so I got three camphor globules about the same size out of auntie's Medicine chest and a glass tube—and, oh, Fred! What is the matter? You are hurting me, dear! What have I done? How glad you look, but—oh ! "</p>
        <p>"The fact is, my darling," said Fred when he found himself able to speak. "I swallowed all your white pills—camphor, did you say they were once, and they have proved a little too strong for me. I feel ill—a strange giddiness. But I think that if we sit very quiet for a few minutes, it may pass off." Maud set as still as a mouse for a quarter of an hour, and by that time the giddiness had gone. It shewed a tendency to return, however, whenever Maud began to ask questions about the mysterious letter; and by the time they reached their destina- tion she was so intent upon performing properly her part of "an old married woman,' that the incident of the letter and its lost globules passed out of her Mind and was forgotten. Fred speedily decided that he had done all that honour required of him. When.,the honeymoon was over he took his bride to her Yorkshire home I mad he, never sew Be Quelfa or his fr_Op4s_ isssin. [Tan IND.] 4</p>
      </div>
