        <p>I , _ . --.-- --.-- - 'l, i. 7 1 r---- -••—..., ...er —4-- -r . . . fa-.--- zr• . , .. , . ;.!</p>
        <p>LIBERAL DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>THE EARL OF CREWE AND MR. H. BROADHURST, M P., AT WARRINGTON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Speeches oil the Fiscal Question. MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S POLICY CONDEMNED.</head>
        <p>Speeches oil the Fiscal Question. MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S POLICY CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>Parr Hall was filled to its utmost capacity on Tues- day evening when a very successful naeeting,prornoted by the Warrington Liberal Association, was held. The all absorbing fiscal .proposals and the discussion they have arou,sed. were no doubt in a great measure responsible for the gratifyingly large attendance, .which was an evidence of the healthy state of local Liberalism. The Earl of Crewe, and Mr. H. Broadhurst, M.P. (who is well known in Warrington) were the principal speakers, and their able addresses on Mr. Chamber- lain's policy were followed with keen interest. Mr. Charles E. Parker (president of the Association) pre- sided and was supported on the platform by Council- lors Tinnion, Brough, Burton, Barton, Ellispn, Pea- cock, James Evans and Wilkinson, the Revs. J. F. Matthews, J. S. Hughes, J. L. Milne, and H. 'Manic°, Captain G. R. Crosfield, Dr. J. Dore Liston, and Messrs. A. H. Crosfield, T. Birch.all, E. J. Guest. J. W. Hayward, W. Furness, Gordon Roberts, Philip ,Garnett, E.A. Sanders, J. H. Hill, John Harrison, G. Wallington T.Locker,S. Wright,Joseph Smith(Knuts- ford). Critchley, Hill (Buttermarket-street), Winskill, George Weaver, E. V. Walker, S. Dunbobbin, J. Fieldhouse, T. Pemberton, W. Crosskill, F. Dixon, W. H. Sherburn, Mounfield, Godfrey Armitage, J. G. Withinshaw, Longland, J. L Ewart, A. Rathbone, N. )ok, P. O'Brien, W. Jeffrey, J. Povey, 'Thomas Grime, W. J. Newton, W. Clegg, J. E. Worrall, J. W. Bouchier, Francis Brown,Thornas Mather, Felix Maginn,William Winstantly,J. Hughes, John Griffiths, Walter B. Ashton, Andrew Scriven, William Hayes, H. Fear, G. Colley, C. E. W. Nurse, J. Podmore, John Haley, J. W. Jeffery,Thomas Woods, Alf Downes, J. Wolfenden, F. Highley, E. Plinston, Thomas Knowles, Thomas Barnes, Charles Cotton,W. Beswick, J. Bailey, E. Lloyd,William Drewett, James Woods, W. Ford, H, White, J. Cowdell, F. E. Dean, P. Carter,J. W.Bibby, John Clayton(Liberal agent),and C. Jackson Holmes (hon.see.),aud the followingladies ; —Mesdames Armitage Parker, Pierpoint, C. Jackson Holmes, Ewart, Osborne, Simpson and O'Brien, Miss Bessie Dutton and Miss Birchall. While the audi- ence assembled Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac. played a number of national and patriotic airs on the organ. LETTERS OF' APOLOGY. 4</p>
        <p>Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMRS announced that letters apologizing for inability to be present had been received from Alderman Pierpoint, Councillor Maginn, Mr. Ziba .Armitage, the Rev. John Yonge, Major Roome, the Rev. G. H. Carnson, Miss Crosfield, and Messrs. F. W. Monks, A. G. Symonds (National Reform Union), W. Forahaw, G. W. Rawlins (Rainhill), G. L. Welford (Lymm), and Joseph Elwell. Alderman Pierpoint wrote :—"I regret that in conse- quence of being obliged to leave home to-day lam unable to attend your meeting." Mr. Ziba Armitage, in his letter, said: "I regret that am under an interdict, and may notattend the meeting to-night. The operation of flogging a dead horse is an inglorious one, and I should be glad to see more of the time of the Liberal party taken up in denouncing the education policy of the Prime Minister,and the Fandals of the War Office, and in advocating a great construc- tive policy tending to peace, retrenchment and reform. I trust the meeting may serve to bind our party together in prosecuting the good cause; with the result of returning a progressive Liberal to represent us in the House of Commons." Councillor Maoinn wrote :—" I would like you to tell Mr. Crosfielethat he can depend upon me for all that T am worth to support him at the next election, as T am opposed to any alteration in our Free Trade policy, and would like to have the opportunity of publicly explaining to the working men of Warrington what my personal knowledge of the conditions under which their brothers (working men) of Continental nations, where there is no such thing as Free Trade, are forced to work and live under, and to compare their social conditions with the work- ing men of Great Britain under Free Trade. Many people are asking why the working people of England are worse off to-day than' they were 50 years ago. Well, the answer is 'easy. mmely, the late war, and if the Liberals of England had been true to themselves and their principles of Liberalism and freedom over that affair, they would have saved the working people of this country from a great deal of the misery they are now suffering, and are likely to suffer for some time to come. I wish you a successful and crowded meeting." Mr. Welford, of Lymtn, wrote: lam delighted to see that you are to have two such true and able repre- sentatives of Liberalism and Labour as Earl Crewe and Mr. Broadhurst. They and Mr. Parker are all able exponents of Free Trade, and will, I am sure, place before the working men of Warrington some of the real facts of the case different from the silly stuff which has been recently spokenin Warrington. THE CHAIRMAN'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The CFIAIRMAN, who had a cordial reception said he would best serve the interest of that meeting by con- fining what he had to say to small compass. An this he all the more readily did because through the courtesy of the Warrington press, he had been already privileged to take a somewhat large share in the dis- cussion of the fiscal problem. (Hear, hear.) There was one point, and only one, he wanted to press home. No doubt they had in that hall a'considerable number who like himself were engaged in direct manufacturing prdcesses, but probably two-thirds of those present were not directly connected with manufacturing con- cerns. The number of people engaged in this country in producing was only one third of the whole. To the representation of the other two-thirds he wished to say a word. There might be at the end of one row a shop- keeper, on his left a tramcar man, the next might be a school-master, the next a railway man. Representatives of these classes were apt to say this fiscal question was more one for those who were engaged in manufacturing that they did not understand it, let those whom it most concerned fight it out amongst themselves.. And , THE GREAT DANGER TO FREE TRADE —the only danger—was the apathy of the consumer. (1-I€!ar, hear.) He would point out that a change in ' their fiscal policy would affect this class far more than ..any other, and for this reason their remuneration, their profit, was of a more stable character than the wage ..of those engaged in manufatcturing, and there was nothing in a change of fiscal policy which was likely to directly increase, or decrease, their remuneration. But let it be clearly understood that any return to Protec- tion under whatever disguise it appeared, would undoubtedly raise the price of the articles which they :and their families required to buy. This the protec- tionists could not deny, for if manufacturers did not get more for their goods, then what good would Protection do them? If a workman getting a pound a week forma that his pound in consequence of the rise in price of all he bought would only go as far as formerly 18s. would, then he (the Chairman) asked that man what he and his wife were going to cut off in the -weekly outlay of their sovereign. The 20s. might de- preciate in purchasing power until it was only as valu- able as 18s. now is. Like Mr. Chamberlain "I only use • these figures as illustrations, I do not pretend they are •proofs, the proof will be found in the argument and not in the figures. I use figures as illuStrations to shox what the argument is." (Laughter.) He fancied, how- -ever, that his figures would be found to be a great deal more accurate than Mr. Chamberlain's for they were bused on the experience of protected countries. So far Mr. Chamberlain's track of the last six weeks was -.strewn with the wreck of his own inaccurate figures. He wanted the non-producers to think this thine out, -he wanted them to take an active interest in this ques- tion. (Hear, hear.) He belonged to the industry which hoped by a tariff to get more for its leather, which -would oblige everybody to pay more for their boots. He -belonged to the class which was expected to benefit by the proposed change, but he asked himself this ques- tion: "If two thirds of the wearers of boots have a 'fixed amount to spend, and if all things are dearer, what if they exhaust their spending power on such articles as food, house rent &amp;c., which they 'cannot well reduce, ,and they have empty pockets when they come to the •question of buying boots, and the boots with gaping -holes and worn soles have to be put up with for months longer than they ought to be,then Task what will be the effect on the tanning trade?" (Hear, hear.) The REDUCED SPENDING POWER OF VIE PEOPLE would re-act upon the manufacturers and lessen the demand. He could not therefore see in • what way the manufacturers or their workmen would be benefited, and certainly those not engaged in 'manufacture would be very much worse off. If there- 'fore they allowed this policy to be"dtimped"upon them, if they allowed the great national verdict -to go by default, if they accepted this policy "lying down" they would only have them;elves to blame. But some mimight" This is very well as far as it goes,. but i it s this question of " dtimping ' that troubles us." He would not go into this except to say: That the country that received dumped goods always got the best of the deal, and gained on-the national exchange, although at times he fully admitted that it caused sectional hardship. But the alternative of Protection would result in a far greater national loss. Once upon a time there was a 'vendor of *paten ills that medicines that mired all the lls that flesh is subject to, including freckles. A good lady who objected to the freckles on her face bought and used a bottle of the medicine and wrote the follow- ing testimonial : " have used your medicine. the 'freckles have entirely disappeared, and now I have got vonvillsions." (Laughter.) It was best to endure the ills we have than fly to those we know not of. Better to endure our industrial freckles, than to invite protec- tionist industrial ,00nvalsiorts. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE RESOLUTION. SPEECH BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. eft.3,WIELD who had a very hearty reception said the resolution he had the honour to move began with a paragraph on the War Commission:—" That this meeting in the light of the revelations contained in the report of the South African War Comlnission, and in view of the enormous increase in the National expenditure,con- siders the continued conduct of public affairs by the present Government to be a grave danger to the state.", (Applause). It was no exaggeration to say that the whole country without distinction of party had been absolutely staggered by the revelations of this Com- mission. (Hear, hear). As regarded the events that preceded the war some of them had held that by wise and statesmanlike diplomacy that frightful war might have been averted, and they had condemned the ex- Colonial Secretary's methods as reckless and provoca- tive even if the country had been well prepared for war. But what were we to say of such a game of bluff • as he played when we were not prepared? Was not that a gamble with the nation's interests just as we are now asked to gamble with the nation's food? (Applause). The second paragraph in the resolution was as follows— "In particular this meeting condemns the Education act of 1902-3, and expresses th,, conviction that the education question can never be settled, save by legislation, which will place under genuine POVULAR CONTROL ALL ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS, . _ . . . .</p>
        <p>„. maintained out of public funds.” And on that principle the Liberal party was united to a man. They intended to enforce that principle, and that was one of the first things they had got to see to when they had cleared the present awkward squad out of office. He believed an inform tl meeting was held some time since in the House of Commons where men representing various political views were present. Starting with the principle of genuine popular control, it was suggested that during one day in the week ministers of different denominations should have the opportunity to come and give I:religions instruction on a requisition from the parents of the children that they wished the instruc- tion to be given. He was told that this plant met with very general assent. He hoped Mr. Broadhurst would not be presently pitching into him for telling tales out of school. (Laughter.) He did nut know that that was the best plan that could be devised, but at any rate it was infinitely better than the pernicious measure which had stirred up such strong and bitter feeling amongst the most law-abiding and conscientious people in the country. His informant was a Member of Parliament, who was one of the strongest supporters of Liberalism in that part of the country. He argued theta measure on these lines would satisfy, and ought to satisfy this Protestant nation as a whole; and as regarded their Roman Catholic friends, he thought it might be necessary to legislate esp ,c'fally in their case. (Hear, hear.) He ventured to think his remarks were well w )rth consideration. One thing was certain—thepresent miserable fiasco was doomed. (Applause.) The next little item in his small bill of fare did not appear in the resolution. He hoped the Chairman would forgive him making that small hit off his own bat, because it was a matter on which he knew thousands of men in this town were thinking about deeply and anxiously. He meant THE REJECTION OF MR. SHACKLETON'S BILL —(applause)—a measure as moderate and as absolutely ' necessary in the interests of justice and social peace as any measure that was ever introduced into the House of Commons. That was a matter of supreme import- ance to every workman in the country, and he hoped they would all bear in mind the part that Warrington played in the defeat of Mr. Shackleton by the Tory Party. It would be easy to go on piling up the agony, because that was what the Tories had been doing all these years. Perhaps it might remind them that the gods first rendered crazy those whom they wished to destroy. (Laughter.) It really had looked at times as if the Tory party were bent on destruction. A certain very astute member of the Government, with ideas of his own, set to work to galvanize the Government, and that brought him to the last paragraph in the resolu- tion. "This meeting also condemns the reversal of our Fiscal policy proposed by Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamber- lain, and calls upon all who are interested in our com- mercial prosperity and the social condition of the people to resist by every means in their power proposals which, if adopted, will raise the price of food and all manufactured goods, reduce trade by increasing the costlof production, F.endanger our relations with our colonies and threaten the stability of the Empire." (Applause). They would notice the reso- lution referred to both Mr. Chamberlain and Mr. Balfour, but he did not think they need trouble their heads about the Prime Minister's policy. His half-way house was built on the sands, and the founda- tions were tottering already. (Hear, hear.) Neither had they to consider any inquiry as to whether there were a few rough corners in the Free Trade policy to be rounded off, and whether it was feasible and wise to deal with them. Not a bit of it. Mr. Chamberlain's last speeches had made the position clear at last, and they saw that they were face to face with a demand for all round, full fledged</p>
        <p>FULL BLOWN PROTECTION. That simplifies matters a good deal, though even now Mr. Chamberlain's economic arguments were a good deal more worthy or" Alice in Wonderland" than anything else he could think of. He dodged about from one posi- tion to another, like a hare chased by a greyhound, and that made him nearly as hard to hit as a snipe in a gale of wind. (Laughter.) Not so long since they had the picturesque interview between Mr. Chamberlain and the workingman, in which he was to prove that dear food and dear clothes d d not matter because old age pensions and higher wages would be more than a set off. (Laughter.) Well, old age pensions had again dis- appeared, not for the first time, nor did they hear any more of higher wages. (Laughter.) The next, be thong it it was the next thing they were told, was that bread would be no dearer in the long run cheaper because of the enormous increase of the wheat area in Canada which would follow. Well, fortunately, wheat growing in Canada was increasing and without, any of this artificial stimulus. But the prophecy of cheap bread (lid not please the agricultural interest, and so now they were told it must be pro- tected, which of course meant dearer prices and more rent for the landlord. (Hear, hear.) It was bound to be so. The landed interest was the "</p>
        <p>BACKBONE OF THE TORY PARTY and they w ould never have tolerated • dear , goods unless they had dear food at the same time. Nobody had put that very obvious fact more forcibly : and pithily than Mr. Chamberlain himself in a speech he had made eighteen years ago, and of course their agricultural friends knew very well that 2s. a quarter was but the thin end of the wedge, and that the corn- bined power of vested interests would soon enable ' them to raise these duties higher. Mr. Chamberlain himself had, in his Glasgow speech, hinted that he would not object to the average duty of 10 per cent on manu- factured goods being raised, and with higher prices for every mortal thin. in the country, and the purchasing power of the whole nation reduced, how was he going to give the promised extra employment? Could he tell , them? No. No. • They had his promise, it was true. But how could he make out, when the purchasing power of a so mreign was reduced to perhaps 17s. Gd. and the cost of manufactured goods went up whilst the demand for labour went down, that there would be increased employment? The question had been asked many times but Mr. Chamberlain had vouchsafed no answer. He had given them his personal assurance, but were they going to accept a personal assurance and fiscal legerde- main of this kind as sufficient grounds for revolutionis- ing and upsetting the whole trade of the country? There was :a great danger in accepting statements of this kind. They had almost every statesman of weight ; and experience in the country warning them against the reckless folly of these proposals, and they were proud ' that evening to welcome lone of these distinguished statesmen. (Cheers.) They had the same emphatic warning from the labour members of Parliament to a man. Were the artisans of our town going to ignore and reject the advise of their trusted counsellors? One of the ablest, most sagacious and most deeply respected of them was with them here:to-n ght—(loud cheers) —and glad indeed they were to welcome him again amongst them. (Loud cheers.) He did not know, though one might pretty well guess what Lord Crewe and Mr. Broadhurst had in storolfor them,but he was quite certain that he could prorniSe them an attentive and apprecia- tive audience. (Cheers.) THE CHAIRSIAN INTRODUCES THE .EARL OF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 28, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 28, 1903.</p>
        <p>he might be allowed to say that in his opinion the resolution was one of the most admirably drawn up he had ever seen. He did not know to whom the credit was due, but it appeared to him to so fully describe the political position at that mompit that it hardly needed any speech from him that evening to enforce it. He might be allowed to say what pleasure it gave him to stand on the same platform as Mr. Broadhurk,— whom they all regarded as one of the most substantial pillars of the Liberal party. (Applause.) His only regret was that in speaking to that vast audience he had to stand between that audience and his admirable elo- quence. It seemed hard to /reflect that only a little more than six months ago they did not know that there was a fiscal question at all, and it might not be uninteresting just to reflect for one moment what at the beginning of last May was a view of the Goveinment under which they were living, and what would have been their view of it at that' moment supposing the fiscal question had never been startedat all. He thought that all agreed, not merely the opponents of the Government, but also many of its supporters, that the record of the Government was one of</p>
        <p>ADMINISTRATIVE INCAPACITY AND EXTRAVAGANCE. (Hear, hear.) The Government had saddled the country with an enormous war debt, but what mattered even more than that was that it had added by millions every year to the common expenditure of the country. (Hear, hear.) They looked wita positive amazement at that document, to which Mr. Crosfield had alluded, namely the report of the War Commission, and what- ever might have been' said for or against Mr. Chamberlain's South African diplomacy when he had overwhelming forces at his coya- mand, what could be said of that diplomacy when the forces were nbt at hand and were not avail- able? Then there were also the two Education Acts. Both of them were outrages on the sense of fairness of the community of this country. (Hear, hear.) He did not propose to enter into the question of these Educa- tion Acts, further than to repeat what Mr. Crosfield had already said, that it would be undoubtedly one of the first duties of any Liberal administration to deal with the Education question from an absolutely differ- ent standpoint from that from which it had been treated by those in power at present. (Applause.) He was bound, however, to say in reference to the Education question that it! was not a question of what a minister of one denomination said,Or what a minister of another denomination said, but those who wee really effected whose voices should be heard and whose wishes should be final, were the parents of the children—(applause)— and it was with deference to the feelings of those parents and to their sense of what was fair, that they must look for the final solution of this question. (Applause.) After.lthis brief preface he came to</p>
        <p>_ _ THE INEVITABLE FISCAL QUESTION, a question which its author' Mr. Chamberlain, com- plained hul been treated in a somewhat exces- sively party spirit. (Laughter). Mr. Chamberlain had said many strange things already in the course of this controversy, but that remark coming from Mr. Cham- berlain seemed to him to be one of ,the strangest of all (Hear, hear and laughter.) If this question was not to be treated in a party spirit why was it born on a party platform? (Applause.) If Mr. Chamberlain had pro- posed an examination by a Commission or otherwise into the commerce of this country, though he (the speaker) did not say that such an inquiry was necessary it might have been conducted with a very complete ab- straction from party feeling. (Hear, hear.) After all however, party discussion meant an unprejudiced dis- cussion and they had only to read any one of Mr. Chamberlain's speeches, which were increasing in violence us they were uttered, to see that he in his con- duct in the controversy did not attempt even to appear unprejudiced, but conducted his part in it in the most absolutely party spirit possible. Of course in one sense this was not a party controversy, for at least 99 per cent, of the Liberals were Free Traders and he was happy to think that therewasa considerable percentage of Unionists who were not Protectionists. (Applause.) If that was what Mr. Chamberlain wanted by a non- party controversy then he ventured to think that he had got it. (Laughter and hear, hear.) A meeting of the greatest possible importance was being held in London that night—(hear, hear)—which would be attended, as they knew, by men whom the country respected for their loyalty to their convictions—such men as the Duke of Devonshire, Mr. Ritchie, Lord Hamilton and Lord Balfour of Burleigh. (Cheers.) Those were only a few of the names, but he could not help thinking that when they saw the account of that meeting they would find that the cause or Free Trade had received very material assistance from them. (Ap- plause.) What was</p>
        <p>THE POSITION OF THE GOVERNMENT and of the Prime Minister? .It was a very hazy posi- tion. (Laughter.) When Mr. Chamberlain spoke he presented to thema draught that was exceedingly potent and that was also, they thought, unwholesome, varying in colour and strength according to the place at which the speech was delivered. Whenthey heard Mr. Balfour however, they were in the position of the old farmer who, having finished a bottle of light claret com- plained that he "was no forrarder." (Laughter.) Mr. Balfour, in his two speeches, at She Meld and Bristol, had exhibited a triumph of dialectical ingenuity and he had succeeded, as he intended,perhaps, in keeping both his opponents and his supporters in the dark as to what his intentions were. (Hear, heir). Mr. Balfour spoke of what was known as retaliation, but he spoke of it by the polite name of" freedom to negotiate.' This seemed to him (the speaker) to be a hardly candid way to advance it, since every government had freedom to negotiate, and there was nothing whatever to 'prevent Mr. Balfour from notifying any foreign power that the Chancellor of the Exchequer would propose at the earliest opportunity that a duty be imposed nponcertain commodities subject, of course, to the consent of Mr. Balfthlr's majority in Parliament. So far as freedom to negotiate was concerned, Mr. Balfour, with his majority had it absolutely. What Mr. Balfour wanted was not freedom, to negotiate but freedom to act, to claim for the Government of the day)power to clap a duty on any foreign commodity that might seem to them offensive regarded as an import, without leave of Parliament for that particular instance. (Hear, hear). He (the speaker) for one would never believe that in this country a public would be found that would so absolutely bind its hands as to hand over the power of taxing the inhabitants of the country to the Govern- ment of the moment, or to some irresponsible com- mittee composed of Heaven knew who. (Applause.) In connection with</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OP RETALIATION they heard a great deal about 10 per cent. duties. If retaliatory duties were to do anything to stop the kind of imports of which so much complaint was made, it must not be 10 per cent., but must in some cases be 100 per cent. That was an unquestioned fact that was admitted by economists who favoured the proposals of •Mr. Chamberlain, but they never heard anything about these high duties on Protection platforms. They heard rather of these mild ten per cent. duties. One other point in imposing retaliatory duties was that we as a country in our constitution were at a distinct disad- vantage when compared with countries where the Government was more arbitraryand autocratic. If they engaged in a tariff ivar,. for instance with Rnssia or, to a less extent with Germany, it would • involve the imposition of high duties, which would have damaging effects on certain trades in this country, and no doubt also on Russian tmdes. In Russia not much would bejheard about the damage. There was not much public opinion, and when there was it generally got into trouble when it talked too loud. (Laughter.) In this country the representatives in Parliament of the damaged trades would go to the party whips and shake their heads and say that they were in an exceedingly difficult position, and that if something were not done they would have to abstain from spch and such a division. The party whip would go to the Government and inform them of what he had been told. That sort of thing would place England in an inferior position to carry on a sanguinary tariff war with Russia or even to a very considerable extent with Germany. Another point which so far Mr. Chamberlain.and his friends had not condescended to answer WILS that so far as experience went there was no knawn instance where the imposition of a retaliatory duty had led to other duties being taken off. Instead the result had been that other duties were put on. Germany and Russia had a tariff war which lasted ' three years. Russia understood all about Protection I and imposed duties to the amount of about 170 per Icent. on pretty nearly everything. At the end of that war the Russian duties against Germany were as high as they were at the %beginning, and in some cases higher. What reason was there to suppose that by placing duties upon foreign commodities imported into this countty we should induce other countries to lower their duties against us. To take</p>
        <p>berlain's speeches consisted of rows of figures, chiefiy remarkable for their unvarying inaccuracy, varied with rather blatant appeals to nationll feeling to be directed against foreigners, and to what he (the speaker) ven- tured to call a very sham and spurious imperialism. (Applause.) Mr. Chamberlain made one remarkable admission at Cardiff. He was obliged to make it there, because Cardiff was a town of which the prosperity was undoubted. Being at Cardiff Mr. Chamberlain was not able to deny the</p>
        <p>tpopulation gave a sort of stability to the country That was an argument which might have some weight, and it did carry weight in countries where the land was in very many hands and where there was a large peasant proprietary, but it would carry less weight here because the land was in very few hands. The direct action of Protection was bound to help the land- lord more than anybody else, although in the long run he believed it was a fact that when agricultural pro- ducts were highly protected in England the lanDord had to pay very nearly as much for increased pauperism as he got out of increased rent. (Laughter.) He didnot know whether Mr. Chamberlain was affected by the ' agricultural arguments but at any rate his proposals would not help to restore the agricultural prosperity of this country. (Applause.) The kind of duties pro- posed by Mr. Chamberlain would be absolutely useloss as regarded restoring the prosperity of the agricultural interest, and it appeared to him that his other proposals might operate in a directly contrary direction. (Applause). The great outcry at the present moment was in] regard to the depopulation of the country and the flocking of people to the towns in search of employment. If they bolstered up certain manu- facturing indnstries there would be a still greater rush from the country to the towns and that would be damaging to the agricultural interest. As regarded manufacturers, why had foreign countries thought it well to protect their manufacturers? One reason he had seen suggested—a curious and ingenious reason—was that they had.done so from misreading the cause of the prosperity of England. They hadseen that this country was very prosperous and that it was a great manufacturing country, and said "Let us have manufacturers and we must necessarily be prosperous too." But that did not follow. A country might mann- ' facture enormously and export gigantically awl yet be anything but prosperous. The suggestion,therefore,was, that those who were responsible for the arrangements abroad were misled by the prosperity of this country in not ascribing it to the varied causes to which it was due but to the manufactures which we made. Another argument was that if they protected industries they would get a great number of different trades—instead of only getting a few—which was considered by se to be exceedingly advantageous. He did not exaMy see why, Lecause if they had a few industries instead of a great number, they would divide themselves up into sub-industries, and they would end by getting as great a diversity as if they had started with a number of small pottering protected trades of different kinds. (Applause.) That led lam to the curious classification of certain trades which had been made by Mr. Chamberlain. Accord- ing to him certain trades were to be looked upon with great respect and regard while others were to be treated with contempt as only inferior industries. In his, Bir- mingham speech Mr. Chamberlain said contemptuously that if they could not make iron they could make mouse- traps. He did not know what put that into Mr. Chamberlain's mind, but Birmingham used to be a Liberal town and in a political sense there were a good many rats there he had no doubt. (Laughter.) But seri- ously speaking, why was a man who made mousetraps inferior to a matt who made steel bars?. No matter whether a man rolled bars or made mousetraps, watches, or anything else, if that man under proper conditions earned fair wages by honest labour, he (the speaker) did not see why he should not engage in any form of employment which was at Rand and which seemed to him desirable. (Applause.) The speaker pointed out how syndicates and trusts had been a very large factor in maintaining Protection in Germany. Last of all Protection appealed no doubt to some extent to what was called national feeling—not the right but the wrong kind of national feeling—that sort of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CASE IN POINT.</head>
        <p>A CASE IN POINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain at Newport spoke of sugar, and said the exceedingly useful arrangement that we Li. entered into with foreign powers had not raised the price of sugar at all. They had been told that the great object of the Sugar Convention was to benefit the West Indian producer, but how the West Indian producer would be able to send more sugar than before with advantage if the price were not raised, he could not conceive. In consequence of that convention the price of sugar had risen to some extent, and he thought it would rise con- siderably more. In that case the West Indian might get some slight benefit; though not enough, he was afraid, to be of much service to him, but as all the poor people in the country would have to pay dearer for the sugar dire3tly and indirectly, a number of important trades would be seriously effected. They all knew Mr. Cham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Original Cocoa, and a Speciality.</head>
        <p>The Original Cocoa, and a Speciality.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>being distinguished from all others by its invigorating nutritious qualities and its delicious flavour. This Cocoa, con- taining as it does all the substance of the Cocoa Nib, maintains its leading position</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>after three-quarters of a Century as</head>
        <p>after three-quarters of a Century as</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>the best form of Cocoa for every-day 1::,e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GENERAL PROSPERITY OF THE COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>GENERAL PROSPERITY OF THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>They all knew that the country, speaking generally, was prosperous although of course, there were depressed industries in various places, and there was a great amount of poverty that did not depend very much upon industries at all. (Hear, hear.) Theyall knew what the signs of prosperity were :—savings banks, the income tax and Board of Trade returns. In spite of this pros- perity, Mr. Chamberlain was in a most desperate hurry to settle this question or otherwise the Empire was go- ing to crash into atoms while we looked on. (Laugh- ter.) The hurry was so immensethat the question was to be settled some time after the next election after the next one. (Laughter.) The next election was, according to Mr. Balfour, to be given up to consider- ing the question of retaliation, and after that Mr. Chamberlain who would, he supposed,have been making a speech a week until then, would be allowed to bring forward his proposals, and the country would then give its opinion upon them. (Laughter.) It was a most ridiculous position. Mr, Chamber- lain's proposals were the ones really before the coun- ' try, and they hada right to ask that on those proposals, within a reasonable time, the opinion of the country should be taken. (Hear, hear, and applause.) It was not fair to the trade interests of this country to leave these matters in this state of hazy uncertainty, and he thought it would be found that if Mr. Balfour and his friends got into the House of Commons and had to speak face to face with people like Mr. Broad- hurst and others who sat in the front benches, they would find it no easy matter to main- tain their lofty and abstracted position. (Applause.) They would be brought into very close quarters indeed, with the result that this question would be submitted to the country before a very long time had elapsed. According to Mr. Chamberlain the only thing a country had to do to be rich was to export and apparently he thought the volume of exports from a country was the sole criterion of its wealth. We imported very much more than we exported and yet we could not be called other than a tolerably prosperous country. The United States was a prosperous country but its exports were as a rule rather more than the imports. India, whom no one would call a rich country, exported infinitely more than was imported, and to come nearer home, the same was the case with Ireland. That country had always asked for Protection, and would ask for it against us if she could get it, on the ground that her exports had exceeded her imports. The deduction to be drawn was that there was no necessary connection between the prosperity of a coun- try and the excess of exports or imports. Prosperity, of course, depended to a certain extent upon the trade but it also depended upon a great many other things. Mr. Chamberlain had proceeded by very rapid stages indeed from his idea of colonial preference to</p>
        <p>OUT AND OUT, DOWNRIGHT PROTECTION. What evidence was there that the Colonies really desired this preference, or that they were prepared to engage in the complicated transactions which preference would mean? After all, we did a great deal of business with the colonies, and the colonies did a great deal with us. There was a. letter in the newspapers only that day in which a gentleman pointed out that when they came to talk of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>DUMPING STEEL AND IRON</head>
        <p>DUMPING STEEL AND IRON</p>
        <p>they found that more was dumped upon us from Canada than anywhere else. The 'reason was that the Canadian Government gave a bounty of 12s. on every ton of pig iron produced in Canada, and that bounty had enabled them within the past three years to export to England 103,000 tons. During the same period the whole quantity that we imported from Germany, Holland,and Belgium, and the United States was only 124,000 tons. If the import of pig iron had such a monstrously-damaging effect upon our industries, it would not be a very long step from asking Protection against the foreigner to asking Protection against our own colonies. That would apply not merely to iron, but to agriculture and many other industries mentioned by Mr. Chamberlain. It was his firm belief that if Mr. Chamberlain's proposals ever;came into operation'and the country was turned into a Protectionist country, we should see a duty put upon everything against the foreigners and a minor duty against the Colonies. Whether that would tend towards the ultimate evolution of the great imperial idea Mr. Chamberlain had in view, he thought they might reasonably be permitted to doubt. (Applause.) At Cardiff and Newport Mr. Chamberlain talked a great deal about tin plates. Those tin plates had become an object of. interest to thousands who previously believed that tin plates were plates of tin. (Laughter.) The</p>
        <p>TIN PLATE TRADE used to be practically in the hands of South Wales until the McKinley tariff was put on in 1890, which imposed a duty of upwards of 100 per cent. upon tin plates sent from South Wales to America. The effect of that at the moment was immensely damaging to the South Wales producer. But, besides being damaging .to the Welsh producers, it was exceedingly damaging ton great many people in the United States. Tin plates, as they knew, were used in that now enormous industry which we called tinned food,and which Americanscalled canned food, and for a great many other purposes. The effect of the tariff was exceedingly disastrous to. the people who wished to can fruit and other commodities, but if the Americans chose to undergo it it was their look out. The Welsh producers, having been seriously damaged, began to look about them for new markets. They looked at home, and the malt was that in 1902 there were more tin plates produced in Wales than had ever been made before, even when the United States was an open market.(Applause.)Mr.Chamberlain said," But you might have had both the enormous increase in the home trade, and have kept the United States market as well. (Laughter.) The United States, if we had asked them at that time, would have left tin plates to us and agreed to make something else." As a matter of fact the United States had every possible reason to make tin plates. They had very good coal and iron, and tins were required in enormous quantities, both for the canning industry and the oil industry and all they had to do was to set up manufac- tories for "tinning "steel, and the thing was done. What possible reason as there to suppose that the United States, with such good opportunities as they had, would have refrained from doing such a thing. simply because Mr. Chamberlain or anybody else asked them? (Applause.) The tin-plate industry was a per- fectly natural one for them to take up, and they might have been sure that they would engage in it. I n the meantime the South Wales tin-plate industry was no doubt very much helped by the "dumped "steel and iron, and it also helped a great many industries in this country—Such as confectionery and jam making—by enabling them to obtain cheap tins. Mr. Chamberlain said, however, that the dumped steel which had been brought in to make the tin plates had only produced £12.5,000 of profit—supposing it was brought mat 10 per cent less than it should have been —whereas there was a loss of £500,000 in wages during that time. That ttotal, however, was arrived at by counting all the wages in the iron-working industry and leaving outall wages in the finished iron industry. To the £125,000 profit must also be added the profits made in the confectionery, jam and other trades using the tins, and he thought they would find that the £500,000 loss in iron-makers' wages was swallowed up by a very much larger total of wages and employment cau.sed brthe introduction of cheap steel. Of course, they all knew that</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN WOULD NOT WITHDRAW any statement ht made either on that or upon any other subject. He made it a rule never to apologise, with- draw, or retract. (Laughter.) There used to be—he did not know .whether it still existed—a harmless method of punishing yo4ng children when they were naughty by placing them in a corner with their faces to the wall and leaving them there until they said they were sorry. He was sure that Mr. Chamberlain had never been subjected to that punishment in his youth— (laughter)—becausp if he had he would have been in that corner still. IRenewed laughter.) There was only i one other question to which he wished to'speak upon that evening, because it affected people who were not otherwise inclined to the charms of Protection. There were so many other countries protected that they asked "Is it likely that they swill be:all wrong while we (in England) are right?" He did not know whether it was necessary to argue whether the United States, France, Germany, and Italy ! were right or wrong in being protected countries, but we had our own opinions upon that point. We could leave them to manage their own affairs. It might be conceivable to be quite right for them to he Prote9- tionists,while it would beequally right for us to remain Free-Traders. Still, it might be interesting to examine one or two of the reasons which tended to make a country Protectionist, and which no doubt operated very largely in the countries he had mentioned. In the first place there xi4l.-S no doubt a considerable superficial attraction about Protections and the loose phrases used about taxing the foreigners and increasing employment seemed to have somewhat of a charm for the great mass of the people who were, he supposed, ignorant of the elementary facts of political 'economy. It was not a very attractive science, and no doubt a very large majority of the people who attended Mr. Chamberlain's meetings were ignorant of the elementary truths of economics. (Laughter.) Then there was the agricul- tural argument—the argument that it was a great thing for a country to' have a strong agricultural industry for the health of the nation was better maintained by a large proportion of its children being brought up in the country than by being brought up in towns; and it was generally believed that a strong agricultural</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Why</head>
        <p>Why</p>
        <p>does Fels-Naptha return your money if you don't like it? You like it.</p>
        <p>39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>NATIONAL ANIMOSITY</head>
        <p>NATIONAL ANIMOSITY</p>
        <p>_ which regarded every foreigner as an enemy, and which was willing to lose money itself if it could only make the foreigner lose more. That did not seemlto him an honourable or a sensible feeling—(applause)—but it no doubt did exist. He should not be surprised, however, if some members of the Government, in spite of their desire to encourage home industries, occasionally rode in foreign-made motor cars—(laughter and applause)— if some ladies of their families occasionally bought dresses in Paris—(laughter)—and if they drank wines of Germany and Prance. (Laughter.) After all was said and done, it really came to this: that all restrictions must lessen the] volume of trade. If they decreased their imports they decreased the exports as well. They might think they would be only affecting one trade whereas they might be affecting half a dozen. In giving a benefit to one trade they might be damaging three or four others, and it was that great complexity which ought to make them exceedingly careful in touching the question at all, and which made the conduct of those who went about delivering speeches without having studied it something which seemed to him not far short of politically criminal. (Applause.) lie appealed to them all to study the question and not lightly to depart from a system which had greatly enhanced the prosper- ity of this country and which had made our Empire the greatest which had ever followed one flag and honoured one throne, (Loud cheers). "MR BROADHURST'S SPEECH. VIGOROUS DENUNCIATION OF MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S POLICY.</p>
        <p>Mr. BIIO.B.DHURST, M.P., who had a hearty reception said they had had a very great treat that night in hear- ing from Lord Crewe a thorough, and almost exhaustive argument against the policy which the country was at the present moment highly excited with. He trusted his appeals to reason would have weight in the town of Warrington, and should awaken in the minds of the people an interest in the question that would go deeper and further that reading the speeches of orators. It should, in fact, lead them to examine into the past days of:Protection,and the results the freedom of trade.had had upon our commercial system A great danger of the subject was that it might lead us to neglect the study of other questions. (Hear, hear.) He understood, if he gathered corrgctly from Chamber- lain's speeches,that he was after increasing tho revenue of the country in order to put Austen— (laughter and applause) —in possession of larger means. (Laughter.) There were other channels of increase, which Mr: Chamberlain might have recommended instead of that of the taxa- tion of the raw material—the greatest of all the raw materials of this country, namely, full and free and cheap fooe for the.building up of the men and women of our nation. (Loud applause). Mr. Chamberlain us-d to be very great on</p>
        <p>LAND LAWS.</p>
        <p>(Laughter.) Why had he neglected that great ques- tion ? They had been exciting the interest of the people, and properly directing their attention to the escape from its proper burden of taxation of the income of landowners from land in the neighbourhood of our great towns and'what was called the "unearned incre- ment." It was the people, the industries, the manufac- turers, the inventors, the builders up of our own elm- merce, who increased the value of the land—(loud applause)—and not the owners. (Hear, hear.) There- fore, that increase ought to bear its full share of taxa- tion, as well as the results of the labour engaged upon its surface. (Applause.) That was the direction In which they had bent their minds. Mr. Chamberlain, his dukes, marquises, and all his great friends of foreign origin whose names he could not pronounce—(laughter) —thought the necessities :of the people and their very existence was the direction, and not the direction of wealth, for the further increase of the revenues of the country. But then the revenues would not have necessitated such increases had it not beenjfor the policy of Mr. Chamberlain. (Hear, hear.) The Government, as he thought Lord Crewe had pointed out, had increased their annual expenditure by nearly fifty millions of money, They had run up a debt of over a hundred millions, but they had taxed- them to the tune of a hundred of millions of moneyjfor</p>
        <p>THE LITTLE WAR which no one wanted—(laughter)—only a few mine owners. (Cheers.) No one knew better than Mr. Chamberlain how utterly dead was King Jingo— (laughter)—and now h 3 started another agitation. Well, if it had not been for his policy, and for the utter in- competence of the Government to control the finances of the nation that vast expenditure would never have been incurred, and, therefore, the revenues of the coun- try would not have stood in such need of replenishment as they did at the present moment. (Hear, hear.) %Veil, there were other questions that the Chairman and Lord Crewe had referred to that had been to some extent neglected, certainly altogether neglected by Mr. Chamberlain—the sins of commission and omission of the Government, and their active work in destroying the popularly controlled education of the country. Now Mr. Chamberlain wanted them to try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>PREFERENTI AL TARI FFS.</head>
        <p>PREFERENTI AL TARI FFS.</p>
        <p>They were trying them now. (Laughter and applause.) The Church of England teachers had the pre- ferential tariff. (Laughter.) None but Church of England communicants could be head masters or head teachers although the schocils a"'were paid as much for by Nonconformists as they were by Churchmen. (Hear, hear.) How did they like that sample of preferential tariffs? (Cheers.) They did not care much for that. They would like the other fax less if they got it. (Laughter.) He (Mr. Broadhurst) was born six years before the corn laws were abolished and nine years before the abolition law came into force. He thought he had referred in that hall before for other purposes to a similar reminiscence, but not in the sense to which he would now refer to it. They were a family HAD TO GIVE UP WORK.</p>
        <p>Brickmaking is hard work and requires a man to be well and strong in order to keep it up. Mr. E. Jarvis, of Deuce Green, Gamlingay, near Sandy, Cambs, who is employed at the Belle Vue Brick Works, says : "My work is very laborious and fonr years ago I was stricken down with Kidney Disease. My blood became thinand poor, mybreath was foul. T could not sleep at night ; I suffered with pains in my back and I could not stoop or straighten myself up. "I had to give up my employment and place myself under a doctor, who treated me for a long while with- out the slightest benefit to me. I resolved to try Dodd's Kidney Pills and soon discovered that they were doing me good. I took six boxes in all and they com- pletely cured me. lam happy to say that I now enjoy the beat of health, my old trouble is gone, and I am veil and strong again and able to take my place in The Brick Works once more. My fellow workers are astounded at the wonderful cure Dodd's Kidney Pills , have wade in met'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>"WRITE FOR MEDICAL LEAFLET,</p>
        <p>of 16 all told. (Lighter.) Wages averaged through- out the year about 18s. a week. He remembered sugar at Bd. a lb., tho nominal but reputed 41b. loaf at Bd. and 9d., and it was made of a material which the poorest would reject to-day. He remembered that the agricultural labourers in the neighbourhood where he was born never had what was called by Scotch people "flesh meat," seldom had they bacon, rarely tea; turnips and swedes with pepper and salt were a common food for the workers on the land. Did they want their agricultural fellow workers to go back to those—(cries of 'No, no ")—at the bidding of Mr. Chamberlain? Of course they did not, and they would also have a measure of the punishment if this</p>
        <p>WILD CAT; SCHEME of Mr. Chamberlain's was sanctioned by the nation: He was sure that Lord Crewe would not for a moment charge Mr. Chamberlaia with intentional neglect. and he agreesi with his lordship that he (Mr. Chamberlain) was badly served by his clerks. They got terribly mixed up in their facts, and the blame fell, as in the case of a solicitor, upon the head clerk. (Laughter.) Neither could it be Mr; Chamberlain's fault ; it was that of his unconvincing clerks. (Renewed laughter.) Referring to</p>
        <p>Are the best remedy known to Medical Science for INDIGESTION, CONSTIPATION, LIVER Lir:Vl Li EL FILS COMPLAINTS, and BAD COMPLEXION. Sold at all Branches of Boots' Cash Chemists, and by all Chemists, &amp;c., at 111 i and 219 pei boA.</p>
        <p>The ILINEEL CO., W., 16, Bevis Marks, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MR. CFIAMBERLAIN'S INACCURACIES</head>
        <p>MR. CFIAMBERLAIN'S INACCURACIES</p>
        <p>with reference to our shipping, MI:. Broadhurst pointed out that the American fi i gures ncluded the river and lake trade which did not compete with us in. any sense whatever. Another element which was not taken into account was that most of our competitors counted their tonnage in the gross whilst our returns contained only the net tonnage. That made an enormous differ- ence, equal to one-third in the tonnage of ordinary cargo boats and nearly one-half in the tonnage of passenger boats. Furthermore Germany registeted her carrying boats down to 17 tons and Russia dawn to 25 tons in capacity. In Great Britain we did not register boats of anycarrylng -capacity under 100 tons. If we calculated the whole of our carrying capacity, includ- ing our barge traffic, the returns would be enormous. A more outrageous mis-statement of the case could not be conceived than that which Mr. Chamberlain made with reference to theshipping industry of thiscountry. (Ilear,hear.) Proceeding, he said if the two shilling duty on corn were imposed, it would not remain at two shillings for more than one year. (Hear,hear). Then how were they to defend the proposed taxon manufactured goods imported into this country? Re had recently paid a visit to the woollen manufacturing districts of Yorkshire, and he found the manufacturers in a wealthy, and in some cases affluent position. Some of these manufacturers had risen from the ranks, and all honour to them. But how came they to be in such a position if trade was in such a parlous state as Mr. Chamberlain imagined? We had</p>
        <p>PROGRESSED WITHIN THE LIST 50 YEARS at a pace unknown before in the history of the world. (Applause.) He would mention the conditions of life which existed when he was a boy. He could not very well remember where it was that he commenced work. He worked on the land, in gardens, and as a blacksmith and then he went to his trade as a mason, and when he commenced in that trade he worked 60 hours a week and the best men got 2.15. a week wages. All the men, if they were. worth anything, now got 38s a week for about six: hours a week on the average during the year less work-. ing time. They had all shared in the general prosperity of the country. He had come to Warrington that thy:- by way of Manchester from his home at Cromer, on the coast, and he looked for Old Trafford Park. (Laughter) Thirty years ago he used to go out of Manchester to. play bowls at Urmston. There used to be a great place, there for bowls, but that day he saw erected works of the most massive and costly kind. Was that an evidence of the decay of a nation? (Applause). Wherever they went they saw chimney upon chimney,factory upon. factory,dock upon dock,canal after canal and there caght tobe more of them. (Applause). There was NO EVIOENCF: OF DECREASED PRODUCTION. So far as he could understand the only real degrease, was in pearl buttons. (Laughter.) They were short of their buttons inißirmingham,and then the jewellory. Oh, those precious Jewels; how Mr. Chamberlain loved them and counted them ! (Laughter.) But jeavellery was not everything. The world existed before. Birming- ham jewellery flooded the land. Then Mr. Chamberlain had referred to the watch trade. Mr. Chamberlain: marvelled at the large importation of Ameriennwatches into this country, and spoke of our declining export; trade. As a matter of fact it had since been proved that our exports of watches to America had increased from j£13,000 to £25.000. Then with regard to our imports of jewellery in 1892 a correspondent had since pointed out that that was the year when THE SULTAN OF MOROCCO disposed of his jewellery. The Sultan of Morocco had been o war, and he like us, discovered that war was costly. But there was this differ ence. The Sultan of Morocco had to sell his jewels to pay for his war. (Hear, hear.) Those who authorised the late war in South Africa taxed the workingman's sugar and tea in order to make him pay for it. They did things better in Morocco. (Laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain was making a great bid for popularity. At Cardiff he made a pathetie appeal to the Member for the Rhondda valley, Mr. Abraham, te assist him. It was only at the last eiection that Mr. Chamberlain called all the labour men in Parliament, fish out of water, and men who had never done anything whatever in Parliament. (Laughter.) He (Mr. Broadhurst) hoped that Mr. Abraham would not rise to such a shady fly as was thrown to him in that reference by Mr. Chamberlain. That states- man had spoken of the wonderful things he could do if he were given authority. He had suggested a tour round the colonies to arrange terms. He (Mr. Broad- hurst) would liken tour round the world at the public -expense. (Laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain told them that the Colonials were very good fellows and they could easily make terms with them. He added that he found that out in South Africa. If Mr. Chamberlain would come to Lancashire in the same spirit and with the same offers and inducements he would find them very easy people to make bargains. He had lent £30,000,000 or £40,000,000 to South Africa. and the British people had to guarantee these loans. No wonder he could get on very well. (Laughter.) Mr. Chamberlain had set himself a big task and his courage was amazing but he could not make fools of everybody. He made fools of them four years ago, and now we had to pay for it. (Hear, hear). We laughed and we danced at, the time; now came</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>RECKONING.</head>
        <p>THE RECKONING.</p>
        <p>• We laugh no more.(Hear,hear.)lt was unfortunate to see Mr. Chamberlain come down in the world as he had done. He was now chief undertakerat the interment of British industry and prosperity. (Laughter.) There was poor Jesse dollings condemned to • perpetual wailing and anguish to keep up the lamentations of Joseph at the graveside. (Laughter.) Yet all the time the corpse rose up to greater strength and prosperity and chased him from town to town. (Laughter.) Let them not hold this game too lightly. There were many people who were keenly interested in protection and who, in few years would make enormous sums of money under protection, as they did during the war. Their interest was an organised interest. The interest of the worker was not concentrated. It was anybody's business as well as it was everybody's business. The Liberal party was solid and united. It was standing with its back to the wall as a solid pkalanx against the advance of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MONSTER. O,F MONOPOLY</head>
        <p>MONSTER. O,F MONOPOLY</p>
        <p>called protection. (Applause.) Yes and they had many recruits from the enemy. (Hear, hear.) The Liberals were in agreement, and the constituencies must follow the lead of the members of the House of Commons for the Liberal party. They must make up their mindswhich candidate they were going t&amp; run—(hear, hear)—and when their minds were made up and their champion was selected they roust work as one man to secure his return. They could not afford experiments. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>SOLID CNANIMITY MUST RE THE POLICY of every constituency. The old Liberal party, the good old ship of State, the good old craft that had carried them through many and many a storm, was well found that day, as it was when it was equipped with capable men and a crew of which they were all proud, and upon whom they could rely. (Applause:) He appealed to that great meeting when it was separated that evening to feel that each individual had a great and sacred duty to discharge according to his light and' belief. By al/ means argue both sides, read' both sides,, think about both sides when they were in doubt go to those who were calculated to know, and whose advice they could rely upon. They must leave, nothing to chance, and if they made up their minds to support the cause of freedom of trade, and of' free access to the foodstuffs of the world, then let them by night and day work without ceasing, work as though the existence of the nation depended upon the result of their efforts. In that faith and in that spirit hehoped War- rington would return to the faith of its past history— (cheers)—so that when the time came they would be found at the gates with their lamps lighted, and they would go. forth to. the battle determined to suc- ceed beyond measure and expectation. The Liberal party would expect that of them, they would depend upon them dbing it, and if Warrington would so act they would be proud of the old borough, and whosoever was sent to Parliament in their name, Lord, Crewe and his collhagues of the Liberal party would bid him wel- come,anid' say "Well done, Warrrington." (Cheers.) The.resolhtion was then put to • the meeting and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SPEECH BY COUNCILLOR TINNION.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY COUNCILLOR TINNION.</p>
        <p>I In moving a vote sf thanks to the Early of Crewe and Mr. Broadhurst, Councillor Trs'NloN referred to the question of "dumping." He did not think any of the manufacturers who. objected to pay .C - for something that was worth £7: would have the least objection to raw material being " dumpcd" on theinfree of charge. The 7esults of Protection in this country would, no doubt, be somewhat similar to the state of affairs in Germany and other. European countries; where workmen had longer hours,,lbwer wages,and more to pay for cora- mcalities. He should be surprised if the working classes of England would be prepared to exchange their privi- leges of to-day tbr anything like that. He trusted all present would bear in miad the closing words of Mr. Broadhurst, and' that when the time came for them to exercise their votes they would vote for that which, in their opinion, was for the best interests not only of their own town but of EnglantP, and all' that appertained to the, prosperity of the natiOn. (Applause.) Councillor lA'Ams EN.'_Azzs seconded the motion which waslloartily agreed to. Lord CREWE said it had, been a double pleasure to attend the meeting, in the first place to receive their exceedingly kind and hearty north-country welcome, and in the second place to listen) to the speech of his. friend Mr. 'Broadhurst, because a speech by Mt. Broad- hurst was always a most vahmlife stimulant and unlike some stimulants, ono could not get too much of it. (Laughter and applause). ilq, moved avote•of thanks; to the Chairman. Councittor PE tCOCK seconded the proposition, which, was passed', and the proceedibgs concluded:</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN RVGLA.ND AND WALES.—According to. "-Kemp's -Al&amp;cantile Gn.zette," the total number of Failures recorded in England and Waes during the week ending Friday, Nov. 20th, was 179, viz.:—New Bank— ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 90.; deeds of arrangement registered,. 89, The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were :—Bankruptcies, ; deeds of arrangement, Era— total 16(1; being a net decrease in bankruptcies of 6, and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1903, to, ciMe, of 317,</p>
        <p>i</p>
