      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINEE.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE.</p>
        <p>NOVEMBRII 28th1903.</p>
        <p>WE fear that the THE DUKE'S LEAD. services of the Fite Food League to the. calm of Free Trade are not made very definite by Tuesday's speeches. The League is still halting, between that cause and the interests of the. Unionist party. Mr. BALFOUR has plainly stated that he is for a "deep and genuine change" in. our fiscal system, and the abandonment of the principle of taxation for revenue. It was this policy that drove the DUKE of DEvoNsurapont'of the Cabinet, and he spoke of it on Tuesday in accents of impressive warning. And yet the "Standard assures its readers that "the funda- mental proposition of the Ministerial programme can, indeed, be accepted by Free Traders so un- compromising as: the DUKE of DEVONSHIRE' and' Lord GOSCHEN." Where such conf.ision prevails,. what is the precise use of the League which foments it? When we examine this "fundamental pro- - position)!' which is accepted by the uncompromising Leaguers,. we find• nothing but funda- mental absurdity. The Queen's .Hall meet- ing adopted' a. resolution in favour of retaliation in. " special cases." The Duke, who maintained in one part of his speech that the pros- perity of the- cont.try was increasing, stultified himself by admitting in another part that " the excessive tariff § of other nations have inflicted,and do inflict a great iod,ury upon some of our indus- tries." FUrther,. the DUKE admitted that to reject the remedy of retaliation absolutely is " to pre - judice the cause. of Free Trade, and make it repugnant to every section of our people" This is the strange doctrine which Mr. BALFOUR, Mr. CHAMBERLLIN. and gir MICHAEL HICKS-BEACH have in con:it-floe,, and so far the DUKE is wit h them. He held that foreign countries, if suitably warned, would" think twice before they did any- thing to close our market against them." If this means anything at all,it means L LIN3D3WNE'S big revolver. "It would give great satisfaction,"says the 'DUKE, "to some of our most important in- dustries, and would satisfy the combative in- stincts:of a still larger proportion of our people.' Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, in his most warlike mood, has used no stronger language than this. But in the next breath the DUKE declares that the revolver must not beloaded.. "Wherever we inset with a protective tarff, and are not able to modify it by negotiations," we must not reply to that tariff, "by a corresponding tariff of our own." To do that, says the.DCKE, " would not be to extend the area of Free- Trade but to restrict its area." ' That is perfectly true, and is a sufficient reason why retaliation should not be employed at all. But if so, what is the sense or consistency of ' dangling the weapon before the eyes of the people as a possible gratification of their" cJ;nba-,ive instincts." But if. the DUKE will not assent to a "corres- ponding. tariff," he is certain that retaliation means war of some kind, and that, "while it will inflict injury-on our opponents, it is equally certain to inflict injury on ourselves." Here the puzzle grows more complicated. The injury to ourselves is certain) as every Free Trader knows ; but if it is not to be brought about by "a corresponding tariff,' what on earth is to bring it about P We must not,.says the DUKE, "restrict he importa- tion of those goods which, far our advantage and for our purposes, and not for the advantage and purposes of foreign nations, we desire to take from them." The essential principle of Free Trade could not be more lucidly stated. But how is it 'to be reconciled with retaliation, even in ".special cases" the blessed formula of the Free Food [League P "Only economists of the most doctrin-- wire type," says the " Standard," woull deny that there are occasions when the unrestricted entry of foreign imports may require:to be abridged." How is this a bridgmont to be effected without the 1" corresponding tariff" which the DUKE rejects It is only" a general Protective tariff," we are assured, to which the DUKE and Loan G3SCILEN are opposed. flow are you to prevent a tariff ad hoc fro n spreading? If Ithe " comb tti-v e instincts "of one-industry are to be satisfied in this fashion, who is going to refuse the gams con- solation, to other industries, especially the in- dustries of which we are told by the DUKE, upon what evidence we have no idea, that they are already suffering "great injury"? It is this maze of contradictions, and ambiguities, and fatal con— cessions, which makes the Free Food League about the feeblest contrivance for fighting Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN that could possibly be invented. We should say that in the long run the DUKE will find that he cannot sanction retaliation in any case,however " special "; but meanwhile, he is certainly not per- forming the function of putting the brake on the locomotive which is being driven at full speed against all the signals.</p>
        <p>Elections to fill the vacancy in Orford Ward,and one of the two vacancies in Bewsey Ward, will take place on Monday. Both contests promise to be closely fought, and they are by no means devoid of interesting features. Be wsey,as everybody with any knowiedge of local poli.ics wel! understands, is a Liberal stronghold, and therefore Mr. Owen Toole, the Liberal nominee, must be considered first favourite. He is opposed by Mr. S. Gough, who has previously sought to eater the Council under the Non-Trading banner, and, of course, flies the same colours still. It rather detracts from Mr. Gough's chances, we think, that Mr. Toole is equally sound on the general principle of trading between the. Corpor- ation and members of the Town Council. Which- ever candidate is elected, and we think it will be Mr. Tools, the Non-Traders are sure of a recruit to swell their ranks in the Town Council, hnd that, after all, is what they mainly desire—as a means to an end.</p>
        <p>In Orford WArd the fight is between Labour and Tory, and we have no hesitation in aavising the electors to plump for the progressive candi- date Mr. F. Stott. Here again, the Non-Traders stand to gain a seat, for Mr. Stott is an advocate of the principle, actuating the cause. His opponent, Mr. Rushworth, iswhat we have always considered the worst type of candidate, viz., a Tory and a publican. Personally we have nothing but regard for Mr. Rush worth, but we would ask the electors of Orford to seriously, consider whether it is to their interests to support a man who wilt increase the alreadypowerful and pernicious influ- ence of the liquor trade in the Town Council? Mr.. Stott, on the other han3, has strong claims upon the electors. He is enlightened, progresbive, and a nominee. of Labour, which is surely entitled to one representative in the coun- sels of the local governing body. He has already contested the ward,and fought a good fight,and we urge the voters in Orford to further their own direct interests by returning Mr. Stott this time at the head of the poll.</p>
        <p>Two letters in our correspondence column from the President of the Trades Council arid the Sccre- tary of the Labour Representation Committee, respe3tively, indicate that the Labour party in Warrington has got upon itsjhind legs as the result, of Mr. Edmund Osborne's recent criticism. Messrs. Podmore and Buckley both argue that the Labour. men are: not called up on to consult the Liberals before inviting a candidate themselves. Thie is a free country, and they are doubtless- right, but there is such a thing as courtesy, and one naturally expscts to see some display of it between parties whose aims are in many respects ide3tical. Perhaps the Liberals havejbeen:lacking in this respect—we do not know, but we are quite certain that our -..C.,abour friends have been both hasty in their actlen and incon- siderate to the Liberal party and the proepectivo candidate, Mr. A. p.. Crostield. The latter has been in the field so Ipng as to establish a prior right of opposition to the sitting member,. and for the. Labour leaders to coolly ignore him and in- vite an outsider was discourteous in the ealrerne. We are very greatly in sgrnpathy with the cause of Labour, and we should, very much regret to sea Liberal and Labour at loggerheads in Warrington as they have b2en, with iire results,. elsewhere The desire for harmony between the two parties has been frequently expreseed in these columns, and we are sure no advantage will a,ccruet o Labour bradopting the uncompromising attitude outlined by Messrs..Podmore and 113ekley. Mr. Crosfield has not, by any means,pressed his claims as a candidate, and the Labour party could have lost nothing, but on the contrary might have gained ranch,. by approaching him ere taking the decisive stsp they havedone. It is not too late for a bettort-inder- Standingpeven now, and we seriously counsel' the Labour party against alienating the Liberals, of the town by assuming a lofty independence,.</p>
        <p>number of anonymous Letter-write:s•in the press-who are,perhaps,not altogether disinterested seem very elisions that Councillor DAds chould resign. They are most assidwtes in pointing out that Councillor Dodds, being secretary of a, com- pany which does considerable- business with the Corporation,ie thus placed in an invidious position but they altogether ignore the fact that there was no uncertainty in the mind of the Town .U4' Ward electors when they elected Mr. Dodds, and they,are equally careful to avoid' mentioning that he is nut a shareholder in the company he repro sents.. These-are vital points, which the rcaf.criV of people who take any interest in the trading question are fully acquainted with. So are the letter writers- who use a cotiveniest nom-de-pleme and discover every month or two the same marers nest. They are begging the c:Testion entirety. What right-minded people desire to put a stop to is the trading between the Corporation and lirnitedi companies who are repre- sented on the Town Council by officials —secretaries and directors alike—who are also shareholders-in the particular companies. Theia "interest ". is direct and pecuniary, . while'l.Tc. Dodds's is purely imaginary. It is 1)0 1.1.5 e trying to impress this important distinction upon people who deliberately close their ears and eves tc itand who by persisting in their present attitude onlY contrive to bDing suspicion upon, and make them- selves, .ridiculous.</p>
        <p>Councillor Yohn Maginn is going, to try is " prentice-hand at solving the inluiry. problem. He will propose at Tuesday's Council meeting.that the inquiry be at once proceeded with and that . it be held with open doors so that.the ratepa7ars and the Press may be admi ted. hr. Maginnis asking for the impracticable and in our opinion merely wasting the time of the Coun-Al. Under such conditions there nould probably be no, inejairy at all, for very few witnesses- would care to give evidence in public on such questions as are- likely to arise. Then again it would be impossible for the Press- to report such an inquiry without being liable to actions for libel every week. Of couree,if Councillor Maginn will guarantee au,indernnity to the Presa of several thousand pounds this diffi- culty may be overcome. But, seriously, we calumet Mr. Maginn to do nothing so foolish as to taireper with the conditions governing the inquiry. in this way.. Me is-merely " ploughing the-sands,7 which is both unprofitable and useless.</p>
        <p>NO one could imagine that Liberalism waciead in Warrington after attending the demonst7ation in Parr Hall on Monday evening. There must have been over 2,000 people present and there was &amp; unanimity of thought and opinion which , augurs well for the progressive cause. her&amp; is no split among Liberals on the fiscal question—which was, of course, the topic of the hour—and in fact Mr. Chamberlain is to be thanked for having so solidly cemented together the scattertd forces which, only a very short time since,, threatened the future existence of th.,‘ Liberal party. could this :save been "Joe's ' object in springing the policy of Protection on the country' ? Many motives have been suggested, for his sudden plunge, but we have not seen this idea mooted before. Mr. Chamberlain was once-a Liberal as everybody knows, and it may be that some sudden revival of affection for his old love has led him to devise and take this drastic step for the unifying of the party whose interval dissensions he has often commiserated— Or at any rate, referred to. We won't say this hypothesis is worthy of serious examination, but we offer:it for what it is worth. .ft seems to us al- most as reasonable as some others which have been advanced And which nobody has taken the trouble to prove or disprove. Any of our readers who are sufficiently interested are cprite at liberty to ask Mr. Chamberlain himself, but we think they had , better first seek " protection."</p>
        <p>The excellence of the speeches was a feature of the meeting on Monday, and the speakers were rewarded with a very attentive and sympathetic hearing. The Chairman ( Mr. C. E. Parker), Mr. A. H. Crostield, the Earl of Crewe, and Mr. Henry Broadhurst M.P., all toothed upon different aspects of the political situation, and each in his own was, had something convincing to say. Mr. Parker and Mr. Crosfield, we have all heard before and special attention is therefore dinicted to the speeches of Lird Crewe and Mr. Broadhurst. Two more strikingly dissimilar orations it would have been difficult to have heard. The Earl spoke in a quiet, conversational style, which lacked nothing but emphasis, while Mr. Broadhurst was emphatic in the extreme. He hit srraight from the shoulder in the style an audience loves but withal his castigation was a good-humoured one Lord Crewe,though he had not the fire of the older politician, waA always making points in a most effective way. His speech was a model of close reasoning and clear insight into the principles which should govern the commercial policy of the Country. He took considerable pains to explode some of Mr. Chamberlain's statistical and dialec- tical fallacies, and they rapidly fell to pieces under the assaulc. The audience greatly appreciated both the principal speeches and, we believe, left the building not only enlightened but convinced that Mr. Chamberlain's proposals are, as Mr- Broadhurst said, nothing but a wild-cat scheme.</p>
      </div>
