      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>There has been a good deal said of late by advo- cates of protection on the necessity of tariffs for the benefit of the iron and steel trades, and it is interesting to know what the workers themselves think of the proposals. The manufacturers have put forward their case repeatedly but little has been heard from the workers, whom the proposed fiscal change will most vitally effect. On Saturday, Mr. James Cox, the secretary of the Associated Iron and Steel Workers, paid a • visit to Warrington and set forth with great clear- ness the case for the workers. He p )inted out • that tariffs did not prevent manufactured goods from entering a country and demonstrated this by the import returns of the United States which showed that despite their high tariffs they were importing iron and steel goods to the extent of a million tons per annum. In dealing -with the much abused question of "dumping," • Mr. Cox showed that foreign manufacturers were harder hit by it than ourselves. German manu- facturers are already crying out against it and contend that the" dumping" policy of the cartels enables English and other manufacturers to buy material at lower prices than themselves. The German wire-nail industry in particular com- plains that it has now to struggle against the competition of foreign manufacturers, who get steel billets and rolled wire from Germany at prices lower than which it is possible for the German manufacturers to obtain the same articles. It will -therefore be seen that whilst " dumping " is not .an unmixed blessing, still it has some advantages for English manufacturers of finished products in enabling them to beat the foreigner in the markets of the world.</p>
        <p>Whilst strongly advocating the maintenance of - the Free Trade policy as the best for this country Ur. Cox did not fail to point out some alternative policies, which if adopted, would he thought, im- prove our industrial position. He called attention to the necessity of dealing effectively with such important questions as freight, royalties and way- leaves. English manufacturers are fettered by these encumbrances to trade to a greater extent than any 91 their foreign competitors, and it is surprising that the subjects alluded to, do not receive greater attention. Sir John Brunner has asserted recently 'that the nationalisation of canals, with a through " system, would benefit the trade of the country to a far greater extent than any system of protection could possibly dq. Freights and royalties are subjects pressing for immediate attention and the fiscal discussion will have served a useful , purpose if it brings about a much needed reform in this direction.</p>
        <p>No one need be alarmed by the statement that the American iron producers have accepted a con- tract to ship 100,000 tons of iron to South Wales ports. To the German makers who have hitherto received these orders it is a very serious matter, inasmuch as it means the transference of the trade to the United States. But to British industry mile fact that the Americans are able to undersell the Germans mans that the galvanised sheet makers of South Wales will be in a position to enter the markets of the world, even those which are "protected "by a high tariff, and to oust all competitors. Last year this industry, which employs over 10,000 workpeople, used up some 250'000 tons of these German bars, the finished product undergoing during the process of manipu- lation in British works an enhanced value of 26 per ton, half of which represents labour. One result of our command of cheap German and American steel is, that instead of the foreigners developing the galvanisedsheet industry for them- selves they are actually by " dumping " at low prices giving a premium to the British industry. So little truth is there in the outcey that we are being ruined by foreign steel imports.</p>
        <p>While Mr. Chamberlain is assuring his audiences that the United States merely requires the appli- cation of a retaliatory duty in order to induce her to lower her tariff in our favour, the principal organs of American opinion adopt a very different tone. Thus the Nation, which occupies much the same position in the United States as the Spectato does in England, answers his assertion that "th American and not the British consumer will pay the duty" by bluntly warning us that "retaliation is a game two can play at ; if Great Britain places duties on our goods we shall raise our barrier against hers." That is precisely in accordance with the predictiors of Free Traders on this side of the Atlantic. Secondly, "what we buy of her is less essential than what she buys of us, as we have boundless resources of a very varied character while hers are limited in range and of diminishing productiveness." Such sentiments if uttered in public in England would _love protectionists to sing "Rule Britannia," but they are well worthy of consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. • In connection with the sorvic,--; at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow the Rev. John Gunnell will preach in the morning on " A plea for he drunkard's child," and Mr. John Upson will preach in Tthe evening on" Rests by the way." ,We would remind our readers that to-day Satur- day) the second annual Eisteddfod promoted by the Apollo Male Voice Choir will be held in the Parr Hall. 'There has been a capital entry for the various com- petitions. An open meeting in connection with Wycliffe P.S.A. Society will be held to-morrow (Sunday) afterneon, when kr. J. E. Balmer, of Eccles, will be the speaker. At Cairo-street Chapel, to-morrow (Sunday) the Rev. C. Harvey-Cook, the newly-appointed pastor, will preach. A:welcome meeting- to the Rev. C. 'Harvey-Cook and Mrs. Cook will be held in the schoolroom on Monday evening. The Rev. J.F.Afatthews will preach on" Compulsory military service " ,at Latchford Baptist Church, • t 0-mo rrow (Sunday) evening. The Rev. W. .T.priggs-Smith, vicar of Terrington 'St. John's, Wisbech, Norfolk, will deliver a lecture on " Chamberlainism " in Providence schoolroom, Chapel-lane, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday evening. * Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., will preside. The next of the series of -University Extension Lectures on "Dante " will he delivered' in the Co- (Verative Hall on Wednesday, December 9th and not -on Wednesday next, as previously advertised. • A sale of work in connection with St. John's Pres- -I)yterian Church vill be held in the Hall, John-street, cu Friday and Saturday next. Mr. W. Grant Paton, of Liverpool, will open the sale on Friday. The annual concert in connection with People's College will be held in Parr Hall, On Thursday, DETcember 17th. The programme will include the vantata " Britannia and her daughters."</p>
        <p>'Personal.- Air. William Pletcher, Grappenhall, has, through Mr. Porshaw, his Solicitor, forwarded twenty guineas lo the Warrington Infirmary. as a donation, and thus becomes a life governor. Mr. Georo.e ft Lamb, late manager of the Warring- On Liberal Club, was, at Altrincham Petty Sessions iltt Monday, granted temporary authority to sell at the 'Park Hotel, Altrincham. The list of successful candidates at. the recent final examination of the Incorporated Law Society contains the name of G. B. HoHord, of Arpley-street, Warring- Mr. Holford served his articles with Mr. Longland, _l':.gypt-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Dance in Parr Hall. The annual dance in connection with St. Mary's Church. Buttermarket-street, was 'held in Parr Hall (ln Monday evening and passed off successfully. :A.hout 60 couples were present and dancing was indulged in until the early hours Of .the morning to. the strains of Mr. Percy Randall's'band. Mr. G. N. Vernon discharged the duties of M.C. Excellent arrangements had been made, Mr. T. B. Tlongliton acting as'hon. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>Shopbreaking. On Monday morning it was discovered that the premises of Miss Dobson, who citrries on business as a tobacconist in Horsemarket-street, had bembroken into, and a number of pipes, pouches, and other articles stolen. The police have the matter in hand. A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALP TAYLOR'S Wonderful .Boots.—Advt. Accident to the Earl of Enniskillen. The Earl of Enniskillen, while returning from the North Cheshire Hunt on Monday, was run into by a carriage. Horse and rider were knocked down, and his lordship was run over and•sustained severe -bruises on legs and body. FOR CLASS AND CHINA. of every .description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishinn-Ironm eon,Ter, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Pleasant Saturday Evenings. The first•of a series of "Pleasant Saturday Even- ings" was held in Sharp-street Mission on Saturday. The chair was occupied by the District Minister, the Rev. J. G. Reid. Owing to the inclemency of the weather the audience was not a large one, but the con- cert proved to be thoroughly successful. During an interval 'refreshments - were served.</p>
        <p>GIVE TFIEM A TRIAL.—ALF. TAYLOR'S wonder- ful boots for everybody. Death of Mrs. Comber of Middleton Hall. _</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mrs. E. Comber, of Middleton Hall, who passed away on Sunday morn- ing in her 85th year. For the past few weeks Mrs. Comber had been suffering from bronchitis, to which she succumbed at about seven o'clock. Her loss is keenly felt, especially by the villagers of Winwick, Middleton, and Hough Gum, to whom she was_ very kind and generous. The funeral took place on Wed- nesday at Winwicli Church. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the er.ocK for BES</p>
        <p>• BOCT ; in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The Prop3sul Northwich to Warrington Light Railway. At the meeting of the N9rthwieli Urban Council 'on Tuesday night complaiac was made that nothing ' had been heard of the Norwich to Warrington light railway since the order waS granted by the Board of • Trade eight months ago. It was resolved to invite the solicitors to the promoters to state whether there is any likelihood of the schema being carried into effect. THE WELL-KNOWN GENT'S "W..A.UKEEZI AND "SG" Brands of Boots can be had from BOOTH &amp; Buttermarket-steet.—Sole agents.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Local Slander Action. At Manchester Assizes on Tuesday, before Mr. Justice Jelf and a jury, Thomas Howitt, farm bailiff, of Massey .Brook, Thelwall, Cheshire, brought an action for slander agamst James Gibson, farmer.— The slander consisted of a statement said to have been made by 'defendant in the presence of a • police ! sergeant that plaintiff had stolen, by: carting away in the night, a quantity of hay belonging to the defend- . ant—Verdict was given for the plaintiff with -,;:50 damages. FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. Gan- LAND Furnishing . Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warn ngion.—Advt. Warrington P.S.A. The usual meeting was held in the C 3-operative .Hall. Sankey-street, on Sunday, the chair being occu- pied by Mr. Balmford. Mr. •_Arthur Griffith? sang Glory: to Thee," with taste ; and Miss Griffiths and Mr. A. Griffiths were heard .to advantage in a duet. Two intrumental selections were rendered by Mr. A. Bates' party, and were well received. Mr. E. Wynne Jones delivered an address on " Parity, Sympathy, and Ambition." BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger,' 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Dance at the Conservative Club. The Warrington Harriers held their first dance in the Conservative Club assembly roam on Monday evening. About- 75 couples were present, and danc- ing was kept up until 2 a.m. to music supplied by Mr. George Anderton's band. In addition to a well- arranged programme of dances, an exhibition of the cake walk dance was given by Mr. Bert Browne, Sankey-street, and Miss Vanity,of Liverpool, who has Carried off Many first prizes in open competitions. The duties of M.C. were discharged by Mr. James A. Princep.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF T9Nips.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Order Against A Warrington Trustee. An application was made to Mr. Justice Wright sitting on Monday as Bankruptcy judge in the King's Bench Division for an order against Mr. NV. H. Drinkwater, Orford-street, Padgate-lane, Warrington, to file an account of his receipts and payments between 31st December, •1001, and 31st December 1902,. as trustee under a deed of arrangement entered into on the former date between Wm. White, milk seller, Poultoa-with- Fearnhead, near Warrington, and his creditors. Mr. Muir Mackenzie appeared for the Bard of Trade, and stated that the respondent was directed on June 21 last to file the account in question, and had not yet complied with the direction. The respondent, being neither present nor represented, his lordship granted the application with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOC K.—Advt.</head>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOC K.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A Bricklayer's Fatal Obstinacy. At.the " Dog and Dart " inn, Grappelli'All, on 'Monday afternoon, Coroner Yates conducted an in- quest touching the death of John Sanson, 23, brick layer, Arasonstreet, Warrington, who was drowned in the Bridgewater Canal on Saturday eveninv,.The evidence shoWed that on Saturday afternoon deceased and a companion named George Thornley of Howley Lane, went to the " Dog and Dart " .11)wling green. Thornley created a disturbance and was ejected and deceased followed ,him out. They went on I the canal towing path and deceased, who was excited and had had some drink,- walked into the water. Thornley plunged in and rescued him but deceased said" I am wet through ;ow, I am going to swim across."' Thornley tried to hold him back, but Sanson jumped into the canal and swam to the other side. After sitting on the bank for some time he again entered the water, despite the warning of his friend. He swam half way across, but sank before a farm labourer named David James, who made a creditable attempt at rescue, could reach him. The body was recovered later in the evening by the policy —A verdict of " Death frotn misadventure " was re- turned, the Coroner remarking that the coldness of the water probably caused a stoppage of the hetrt's action. •</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF rONICS.---63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.----Advt. Dante's " Purgatorio." Mr. Philip H. Wicksteed, M.A. continued his course of lectures on " D tnte " at the Co-operative Hall, on Wednesdayey:maing. The " rgatorio " was under discussion, and the lecturer pointed out that Purga- tory," in Rialto's opinion, was the state of penitent souls. llepentence was suonosod to be imoossi hie after death, and hence turning to God, even at the last moment or. life made the difference between for- giveness and eternal punishme-(4, because it made the difference between the love of goodness and eternal ill-desert. Dante pictured Purgatory as at the anti- podes of Jerusalem, in thesouthen hemisphere, and •!Va4 literally a getting bac': to the state of innocence which Adam and Eva forfeited by the fall. The sufferings of Purg it ary, in Dante's opinion, were a relief to the pettitents, as th e utterance of their love of the virtue they had neglected and hatred of the vice to which they had yieldeil. It was a state in which the penitent. was living himself out of the evil and living himself into the gool. Thera was no hint anywhere in Dante .that the prayers of the living could be of any avail to the souls m Purgx. tory if they were just mechanically repotted. There was no such idea that a-person could etnoloy another person t say prayers on behalf of a sonl. Tie declara- tion of Dante was that the prayers of those that pre- vailed were the prayers of those who loved the departed souls, aul D into taught that - the prayers of those who loved us on earth could hasten our passage through Purgatory. When the purification was complete, but not till then, was 'there any • desire to rise Into the nearer presence of God. The immediate goal was the earthly Paradise, which was represented at the summit of.the Mount of Purgatory, where the human will was no longer subject to delusion or deflection, and therefore needed no more discipline, and where nature was free from disturbance and evil. In an interesting way the lecturer described the meeting of Dante and Beatrice in Allis earthly Paradise, and showed how Dante for the first time fully realized what his wayward life had,been since her death. After dipping himself in the river's of Lethe and Ennoe, his purification was complete,and he proceeds with Beatrice through the spheres from the earthly to the heavenly paradise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday November 28, 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 28, 1903</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Potter. The death occurred with painful suEenness on Wednesday afternoon of Mrs. P.Ater, wife of Quarter- master and hon. Captain A F GPotter.Thedeceased lady became serionsly ill on Monday, and despite the 11 4 medical care and attention. she pAssEld away on Wed- nesday afternoon. She had lived in the Lovely-lane district for about 20 years and her genial disposition and.sympathetic nature had won for her many friends. She had spent all her lifetime in Warrington and .was exceedingly well known. She was a daughter of the late Mr. Massey, who carried on the business of a basket-maker in Market-street. The greatest sym- pathy is felt with Mr. Potter and his family in their bereavement. The funeral 4kes place at the Cemetery at noon-to-day. You CANNOT DO BETTER! Buy your boots and shoes at ALF. TAYLOR'S. Cheapest and best town.</p>
        <p>• Dallam Brewery Ambulance Class. Oa Tuesday evening, a very interesting meeting was held in connection with the St. John Ambulance Class connected with Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons Dallam Brewery. Presentations were made to Dr, G. Braide and Mr. J. E. Newnes, each recipient reeeiving a highly-finished umbrella, with their initials engraved thereon.—ln accepting the gift, Dr. Braide remarked that he was pleased with the progress made by the class. The members were becoming very efficient, and the firm were showing their interest in the class by providing the necessary appliances. He hoped • the members of the class would be as successful in the next examination as the first. After the Christmas holidays he would have pleasure in continuing the lectures. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASa WATERS.—Advt. A Successful Fancier. Mr. J. D. L?igh, the hoc. secretary of the Warring- ton and District Ornithological Society, has been a very successful exhibitor at the recent bird shows. The following is a list of the awards obtained by him :—Stratford-on!Avon, November 10th and 11th, . 3 birds exhibited, one Ist, ono 2nd, one 3rd ; Gates- head, November 13th and 14th, 2 birds exhibited, one Ist, one 2nd ; Keswick, November 13th and 14th, 2 birds exhibited, one 2nd, one v h a; Sheffield, Novem- ber 18th and 19th, 2 birds exhibited, One 2nd, one 4th; Wrexha,n, November 20th, 4 birds exhibited,one Ist, two specials; one 2nd, one 3rd, also ss. Challenge Cup for best bird in show: Ashbourne, November 25th, 3 birds exhibited, two lsts, one 2nd ; Barro7-in-Furness, November 25th and 26th, 2 birds, one Ist, one special, one 3rd. It will therefore be seen that with a total of eighteen entries Mr. Leigh has carried off one challenge cup, eight first prizes, five seconds, two thirds, and one fourth in addition to ' three special prizes. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. . Volunteer War Game. The officers of the Warrington Volunteer battalion played a very interesting war game on Monday evening • A special officer, Lieutenant-Colonel Marshall-West, came down from London to act as umpire-in-chief. The maps of the corps (known as kriegsspiel maps) deal with the country between Oxford and Maidenhead. The most important feature in -this district is of course the river Thames, and the imaginary operations con- sisted of the defence of the northern bank of the river between Goring and Henley and its attack from the south. The nature of the task and the shape of the river in that part of its course suggested at once the memorable struggle for the possession of the Tugela river. Nearly all the local officers took. part and much appreciated this form of tactical study, which is so much practiced in Germany as an essential feature of milit:try education. The Salvation Army. The work of the Salvation Army in Warrington has now been placed in charge of Adjutant and Mrs. Everness, who for the past fifteen years have been engaged as .leaders in the cause. Saturday and Sun- day were set apart as their welcome. At the tea given on Saturday for soldiers and friends about 100 persons were present, and it was followed by an address from Adjutant Everness upon the present position of the army in this town. This was followed by a procession and another meeting. The Army had engaged the Co-operative 'Hall, Winwick-street, for their revival services on Sunday and Adjutant and Mrs. Everness conducted three services. Instead of the usual open-air service at 5.20 the soldiers gathered at the hall in Academy-street, where the Adjutant conducted a "Love Feast," biscuits and 'water being distributed according to Army custom, followed by fervent prayer for an outpouring of the holy spirit. A very large procession was then formed and, headed by the brass band, marched direct to the Co-operative Hall, where it good congregation assembled, and a revival service was held: ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CL.ASS MINERA WATERS.—Advt. "The Migration of Mammals." This was the subject of a paper read at a meeting in connection with the Field Club by Mr. May, F.E.I.S. Mr. Charles White presided. The lecturer briefly dealt with the pro- cess oldiffusiOn by which certain orders of animals have multiplied and spread from.their primitive abode to far distant regions. He prefaced his lecture with a general sketch of the main areas which a study of geographical distribution reveals as tenanted by kindred genera and species of particular orders. He then enumerated the families of mammals characteris- tic of each of the six niain regions so defined, com- menting in passing on the light which these facts throw on past geological history, and particularly on the changes of land and sea areas in -Tertiary times. Mr. May had prepared a number of very useful dia- ()Tains to illustrate his subject, and was complimented on the care, accuracy and patience expended on them.' He came in for somewhat severe criticism in respect both of the lack of method and grasp betrayed in his paper and of the unsatisfactory nature of some of the theories which he enunciated. He replied to his ' critics with characteristic good humour and courtesy. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Ad.!t. St. John's Literary aad Social Society. The members of this Society held their usoal fort- nightly meeting in St. John's Church Schoolroom on ' Wednesday evening, the Rev. J. L. Milne, 8.D., Hon. President, in the chair. Mr. D. R. Hodge, Vice- President, gave a paper on a cycle tour which he had made in Scotland, from Carlisle to Ayr and Glasgow and back to Carlisle, illustrating the same in a highly; interesting manner by limelighti views, the ' lantern slides for which had been prepared from photo- graphs taken by himself. Mr. Hodge said that, given good roads, 'there was no better way of spending! a holiday than by cycling, which gave one opportum- ' ties of seeing the country. He desoribed the towns and districts passed through, referring to the great antiquity of some. of the fordier, such -as Dumfries, Annan, Ayr, &amp;c., and to the association some of the plaees had with the Covenanters and their struggles for religious freedom. His route also lay through. the land of Burns, and perhaps the most interesting references and views were those dealing with the principal memorials of the, poet, and places rendered memorable by -his' genius including his house at Dumfries, Burns' cottage, Ayr, the Twa Brigs, Alloway Kirk, &amp;c., &amp;c., all of which were referred to and described. There was an exceptionally large attendance of members of the society and their friends, and at the close a hearty vote .of thanks was accorded Mr. Hodge for his inter- esting and instructive paper. SEE BOOTH SC SON'S SMART WINTER MOTs.— 1 99, BITTTERMARKET-STREET.—Advt. N.S.P.C.G. Concert at Frodshem. A grand concert was given on Wednesday evening I last, at the Drill Hall, Frodsham,in aid of the National Society for the Prevention of . Cruelty to Children ; the performers being the members of the Warrington Entertainin4 Society. The programme which was a I long one, was heartily enjoyed by a large and ap- preciative audience and several of the performers were loudly encored. Miss Lucy Davies's songs, " Awake " and " Spring is here " were a musical treat, and Miss Eckersley's rich contralto voice was heard to splendid advantage in the songs " Angus Macdonald," and "Sing me to sleep," Mr. W. H. Ireland achieved great success in his tenor solos, "Star of Bethlehem "and " The sound of the Drum," • and the songs, (with "orchestral accompaniment) "Nazareth," . " Mv- Beloved Queen," and " Reign Edward Reign," were exceptioeally well rendered by Mr. F. Fieldlionse. Two whistling solos. "II Hairs and " Sing Sweet Bird," were well delivered by Mr. W. Whittaker, and Mr. G. B. Kendrick's banjo solo I " Romance in C Minor," was listened to with great attention. Mr. E. A. Sanders contributed two humorous recitations, " Liverwing Testinamial " and " Nomination Day" in his usual inimitable style. Mr. F. R. Bickford's Living Marionettes were a decided novelty, whilst Mr. J. Emson's comic songs had the effect of provoking roars of laughter. Mr. W. H. Kendrick was the conduct-1r of an able orchestra, which consisted of grit Whitfield ('cello' , lir. W. Ray (flute), Mr. j. Ray (violin), and Mr. .T. Scott (viola). • •</p>
        <p>New School Attendance Officers.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Attendance Committee last evening Mr. William Farrar and Mr. Thomas William Holbrook were appointed school attendance officers.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>SCOTCR SHORTBREAD The "_Crawford" Qtmlity.</p>
        <p>HIGHLAND. Thick Cakes. LOTMAN. Thin Sections, suzarerl on top. AYRSHIRE. Thin oblonz Cakes, sirel cm top. .Sold by Grocers and Baker everywhere.</p>
        <p>Ragged School Band of Hope. The first concert in connection wtth the above, was given in the upper school-room on Monday evening before an audience of 500. Mr. H. Osborne presided and gave an excellent address. An interesting programme was -submitted. A. most enjoyable even- ing was terminated by the singing of the " DOxology." New Salt Works Near Lymm. The Lymm District Council on Thursday night had under consideration plans for a new salt works to be erected near Heatley Station. Boring experiments have been in progress in the district for the last two years, and it is stated that at last a supply of brine has been tapped Which is likely to ,prove .remunera- tive. At present the construction of works which will employ some forty men is under contemplation, and it is not at all improbable that these works will be speedily developed, and that other , borings will be made nearer to Lvmm.</p>
        <p>Temperance Sunday. Temperance Sunday is being observed throughout the country by the Free Churches to-morrow. The Wesleyan 'Methodists of Warrington in harmony with the recommendation of Conference, and the expres- sions of the last quarterly meeting,are making special efforts to further the cause of temperance throughout the circuit The services at Bold-street on Sunday have been arranged with this in view, as will be seen from our advertisingcolumns,and the Sunday services are to be followed by a Circuit Temperance meeting on the following (Monday) evening when the Super- intendent Minister (Rey. G. EL Carson) will be sup- ported by a number of active workers including Miss Bessie Dutton, Mr. W. U. ,Sher burn Mr. Albert Perkins and other friends. We shall be glad to hear hat the cause in Warrington has received marked impetus as the result.</p>
        <p>Parish Church Tea Party. A successful and enjoyable function was the nuaual paroch:al tea party in connection with the Warrington Parish Church which took place in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. An excellent tea was served, lady members of the congregation presiding at the tables. Afterwards dancing was indulged in to music , supplied by Mr. ..Inderton's band and there were con- certs in the ante-rooms. One was provided by the pupils at Fairfield Schools, and the programme included pianoforte solos by Lena Duck- worth, and W. Harding, a violin solo by Elsie Wilkinson, song "On the banks of Allan Water" by W. Dennett, and action songs and a patriotic operetta by the whole of the scholars. All .the items were creditably presented, and were well received. An ither capital entertainment. was pro- vided by a party of ladies and gentlemen. The principal feature was Romberg's " Toy Symphony," which was well rendered with the usual variety of toy instruments. Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., con- ducted. Songs were contributed by Mrs. Jermin,gs Mr. Poole, and the Rev. G. W.. Dickinson, and Miss Thornton gave two violin solos. Daring the evening the parishioners and friends present numbered about 1,000.</p>
        <p>Marriage of Chief Inspector Flo3d. A. wedding in which much interest was evinced took place on Thursday at St. Paul's Church, the contract- ing parties being Mr. W. T. Flood, chief inspector of nuisances, and Miss Florence E. Taylor. Despite the unfavourabie state of the weather, a large congrega- tion assembled to witness the ceremony. Mr. Flood has been long associated with St. Paul's choir and as an evidence of their good-will on this auspici- ous occasion, his fellow members of the choir rendered a choral service; consisting of a processional, a hymn and a chant. Mr.- A.L.C.M., presided at the organ with his usual ability.. Representatives irom the several offices. of .the Cor- poration were also present, Mr. Flood being very p )pular amongst his fellow officials. The bride, who was given away by her brother, looked handsome in a dress of cream cashmere, and white hat with plumes. She carried a choice bouquet of white Bowers There were two bridesmaids in attendance namely, Miss Cissie Taylor (sister of the bride) and Miss Food (sister .of the bridegroom). They wore pretty d‘esses of grey material and black picture hats. Their bouquets were composed of yellow marguerites. The officiating clergymen were the Rev. W. Bracee imp, assisted by the Revs. T. 'Barber and C. J. Edwards. At the conclusion of the , ceremony, Mendelssohn's well-known " Wedding March" was played. A reception was afterwards held at the house of the bride's father in Wellington- street. The newly-married couple were the recipients of many useful and valuable presents, inclluding one from St. Paul's Sunday school teachers, one from the adult members of the choir, another fram the junior members of the choir. The Vicar and wardens also contributed a handsome present, as did also his fellow officials of the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>More Small-pox Cases.</head>
        <p>More Small-pox Cases.</p>
        <p>During the -past week four additional cases of small-pox have been discovered and ten patients are now being treated for the disease in the sanatorium at Great Sahkey. Th 3 cases are of a mild type and mostof them hav3 occurr3d in Orford Ward. The. medical officer of health (Dr. J. Guest Gornall) has issued notices advising the iahabitants t) take th 3 precaution of being vaccinated. Election Expenses.</p>
        <p>The followig is a return of candidates expenses at the November elections :—Orford Ward, Mr. A. Peacock, £2B 75., Mr. F. Stott, £l6 3s. sd. •, St. John's Ward, Mr. J. Ashley, jun., £l3 Os. 10d. ; Fair- field Ward, Mr. T. J. Hulse, £27. 4s. 10d. ; Howley Ward, Mr. John Evans; £29 Os. 10d. ; St. Austin's Ward, Mr. T. H. Ashcroft, £l3 i6s. 2d., Mr. J. Critchley, £2l 6s. 6d. ; Latchford Ward, Mr. S. B. Stringer, £l4 10s. Bd., Mr. W. J. Wilson, £2l 4s 11d. Cheshire Deputy-Lieutenants.</p>
        <p>The King has been pleased, on the recommendation of Earl Egerton of .Tatton, Lord-Lieutenant of Cheshire, to approve the appointment of the following Deputy-Lieutenants of Cheshire :—Sir Richard Marcus Brooke, Bart., Norton Priory ; Mr. James Grim') le Groves, Oldfield Hall, Altrincham ; Mr. Thomas Henry Lyon, Appleton Hall, Warrington ; Mr. George Littleton, Dewhurst, Beechwood, Lymm ; and Mr. Thomas Bland Royden, Franklyn Hall, near Birken- head.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>" Dr. Part" Receives Five Years Penal Servttnde.</head>
        <p>" Dr. Part" Receives Five Years Penal Servttnde.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, before Mr. Justice Ridley yesterday, Herbert Part (37), surgeon was charged with having committed bigamy by having at Prest- wich on December, lst, 1902, feloniously married Ellen Ford, his forZer wife, Margaret Ellen, to whom he Was previously married on October sth, 1898,being then alive. . Prisoner . pleaded . not guilty. Further particulars of the case will be found on page 7. In passing sentence His Lordship said he was convinced that prisoner was an impostor and a rogue. This was but one offence hi the deceit which he had prac- tised during the course of his life. It was impossible to pass the case over with a less sentence than five years' penal servitude, Glasgow Select Choir.</p>
        <p>WET would remind our readers of the Warrington Caledanian Association's Concert in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening next, as will. be seen from an adver- tisement. The popular humorist Mr. Nelson Jackson has been engaged in addition to the Glasgow Select choir who provided such a delightful musical treat last December to lovers of part music. This famous body of highly trained singers are absolutely unrivalled in treatment of the our national sthigs and ballads, their performance in this respect being a perfect revelation in part-singing. They have appeared repeatedly be- fore her late Majesty Queen Victoria, at .Balmoral. The programme arranged on this occasion embraces some of the finest lyric gems of England,lreland,Wales and Scotland, and- illustrates, the pathos and humour characteristic of the four countries. The choir may be trusted to present them in their usual faultless style, and we would strongly urge all lovers of music not to miss this opportunity of hearing these famous singers. The proceeds are to be given to the Warrington Infirmary and the Association Benevolent Fund. • Lecture on William Morris.</p>
        <p>"William Morris—poet, artist and communist " was the subject of an interesting wad instructive lecture delivered before the members of the Wycliffe Young People's Society on Monday evening by Mr. James Ball. The lecturer gave a biographical sketch of William Morris, and recited some of the most striking passages of his •poems. Mr. Ball laid special emphasis on Morris's great work in reviving decora- tive art in our home surroundings. Like Ruskin, Morris had, a hatred of the ugliness and lack of artistic taste in the furnishing and decorating of our homes, and set about to remedy it by wedding art to the crafts and trades. It was estimated that Morris completed from "000 to 800 designs for tapestries, carpets, chintzes, embroideries in cotton, wool and silk, wallpapers and high-class printing. By the • courtesy of Messrs. Mack, Hamilton and Com- pany of Oldham-street, Manchester, Mr. Ball was able to show twenty specimens of the work of the Morris Company, for .whom Messrs. Afack,Hamilton and Company are the Northern agents. The specimens included carpets, tapestries and chintzes, and the extreme beauty of design and perfect blending of colours were greatly admired.--Mr. Ball concluded an admirable lecture by a reference to Morris's Socialistic work.—At the close he was heartily thanked for his services, on the proposition of Mr. J. Gauld, seconded by Mr. A. Millar, and supported by the Chairman (Hr. J. Hawthorn).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Concert at Buckley-street Methodist Frae</head>
        <p>Concert at Buckley-street Methodist Frae</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>-A concert was held ia the schoolroom on Monday, in aid of the Renovation Fund, Councillor Ashcroft presiding. A very enjoyable programme was sub- mitted. Mr. L. Wood gave creditable renderings of the violin solos " Faust " and" Broken Melody," the latter receiving a • well-deserved encore. " The sea lath its pearls " and " God is a Spirit " were the selections given by the Lyric Quartette, Misses Fai r- htirst and Woodland, and Messrs. P. George and N. Cooke. Mr. P. George, with his deep baritone voice, was heard to advantage;.in " The Curfew." " The " Wishing gate " was tastefully sung by Miss A. Woodland, who possesses a rich soprano voice. Miss L. Fairhurst gave " Alas ! those chimes " and " Abide with me," the former song calling forth a well-merited encore. Mr. J. Hannan was loudly applauded tor his renderings of" Mona "and" Alice, where art thou ?" Mr. W. Whittaker gave two con- certina solos, recTiving a well-deserved encore, to which he responded with a ventriloquial sketch. The duet" The moon hath raised," by Messrs. J. Hannan and P. George, was a great success. Mr. Albert E. Prow was the accompanist. On the motion of Mr. A. Jennings, seconded by Mr. J. J. Fairhurst, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. P. George, who was responsible for the, evening's entertainment. Thanks were also given to the Chairman and the artistes:</p>
        <p>I Hospital Sunday. The Rev. H. V. l'igot presidei at a meeting of the Hospital Sunday Committee, held at the Infirmary yesterday. There were also present: Revs. john .Yonge, B. Percival, W. Bracecatup, Rev. Father Whittle, Rev. Father Wilson, Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., Councillor Barton, Mr. Tanner, Captain Everness of the Salvation Army. Mr. A. J. Jolley (hon. secretary to the Committee). and Mr. J. H. G. Hampson (secretary of tire Intirmary).—ln his annual report, Mr. Jolley stated that 1.2 places of worship which contributed to the Hospital Sunday fund in 1902 ailed to do so in 1903 Five cot= enced or renewed their contributions. On balance there was a net loss of £ll Bs. 6d. under these heads. The total receipts were £2Ol ss• 6d. a decrease on last year of £22 12s. Bd. Of these amounts .£2Ol ss. Id. was collected within and £lOO Os. sd. outside- the Borough. The expenses were £1.4 9s. as compared with £l6 10s. last year.--The report was adopted on the proposition of the Rev. John Yonge.—Mr. A. J. Jolley was unanimously re-elected hon secretary to the committee, and thanked for his past services. In acknowledging the compliment, Mr. ,Tolley said if by any means of putting pressure on the representa- tives of the varions.charities he could increase the .contributions, without becoming a nuisance, he Nvord d not fail to do so.—lt was decided that the third Sunday in March--March 20 —be Hospital Sunday.—t Acting on a suggestion of the Rev. John ,Yonge, i was agreed that a circular setting - forth the work and needs of the institution, be placed in the pews of the churches and chapels about the time of Hospital •Sunday.----The meeting concluded• with an ack-now- fedgment of the Chairman's. 'services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>THE INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR MAGINN'S NEW</p>
        <p>PROPOSITION.</p>
        <p>Town Council Bu§iness.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Town Council to be held on Tuesday, Councillor Maginn will propose :—(1) That the proposed Inquiry into the alleged trading by mem- bers of the Council with the Corporation be proceede d with, with all speed, and that the Commissioners be re- quested forthwith to proceed with the Inquiry and to complete the same without delay. (2) That so much of the resolution relating to the said Inquiry passed by the Council on the 11th August, 1903, be varied so as to enable the Inquiry to be held with open doors, and that the ratepayers and the Press be admitted to each sitting of the Commissioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NEW STREET-MORE RESOLUTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW STREET-MORE RESOLUTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Street improvement and Lightine: Committee has resolved that the new street behind Bridge-street be continued through premises belonging to William Hodgkinson, Ltd., the Cornorition giving a plot of land in the Eagle and Child yard equal in area to the plot required for t nw stre3t, Caroarttion to repay to Hodgkinson's. Ltd., the cost of all works and other expenses incurred or necessitated by thealteration of their buildings and plans. Councillor PEMBERTON will move the rescinding of the following resolution of the General Purposes Com- mittee, passed on October 6th," Resolved that the pro- posal contained in the following resolution of the Street Improvement Committee be approved. A pro- posal from Mr. R. E. Garnett to add 20 feet in width co-extensive with his premises to the proposed passage behind the new premises in Bridge street, and,!to give the scheduled property belonging to him, containing an area of 316 square yards, in exchange for land in the Eagle and Child yard, containing 531 square yards." Education Committee. Vacancies.</p>
        <p>The Council will appoint representative members of the Education Committee in place of the late Councillor Brophy and of Mr. 3. Charlton Parr, resigned, and a co-optative member on the recommendation of the Association of Roman CatholiciSchools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MONDAY'S ELECTIONS. The Contests in Orford and Bewsey</head>
        <p>MONDAY'S ELECTIONS. The Contests in Orford and Bewsey</p>
        <p>Wards.</p>
        <p>• There will be two bye-elections at Warrington on Monday next to fill the vacancies occasioned in Orford and Bewsey Wards by the retirement of Councillor Unson and the elevation of Councillor Pierpoint to an Aldermanic seat. Mr. Fired Stott, the nominee of the Independent Labour Fiartv.will again seek the suffrages of the electors in Orford Ward and he has been actively prosecuting the campaign for some time past. He will be opposed by Mr. J. A. Rushworth, licensee of the "Horse and Jockey "Inn. who contests the seat in the Conservative interest. In Bewsey Ward Mr. Owen Toole comes forward as the Liberal candidate and his workers have been busily engaged in canvassing the electors. Mr. Samuel Gough is again making an effort to win the seat, as the official nominee of the Non- trading committee. Mr. Peter Peacock was nominated but withdrew on Tuesday. The polling stations will he,as follows IIEwsEY WARD: The Mission room, Hoyle street, St, Ann's school, Dannett street. ORFORD WARD : Silver street schools. MR. STOTTS CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>Last evening a public meeting in support of the candidature of Mr. Fred Stott was held in the Co-oper- ative HalWinwick-street, when speeches were delivered by the candidate, Councillor Jackson, of Manchester, Mr. William Speakman, secretary of Earlestown Trades Council. and local trade unionists. Mr. James Ball presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ANOTHM ELECTION IN BEWSEY WARD.</head>
        <p>ANOTHM ELECTION IN BEWSEY WARD.</p>
        <p>The. election to fill the casual vacancy occasioned by the death of Mr. John Brophy will take place on Mon- day the 7th December. Nomination papers most be delivered at the Town Clerk's office at or before five 'clock on Nfonday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>COMPULSORY SERVICE QUESTION. Mecting at Latchfori. ENGLAND'S STATE OF DEFENCE.</head>
        <p>THE COMPULSORY SERVICE QUESTION. Mecting at Latchfori. ENGLAND'S STATE OF DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>A wall-attended meeting promoted by the Warrington branch of the National Service League was held in the Parish Room, Latchford,on Wednesday evening, Captain F. M. Appleton presiding. There were• also present on the platform:—Major F. M. Roome, Captain G. R. Crosfield (hon. secretary of the branch), and Lieutenant Broadbent.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said tha members of the League realized that the country was not in the state of security that they would wish it to be in. He believed the invasion of England was not only a possibility but that within the last few years we had been within a measurable distance of such a great event in our national history. (Hear, hear.). Captain Appleton quoted the opinions of Lord-Wolsley and the War Commission on the sub- ject, and, continuing, said that there was scheduled in the war office of every military state in Europe a dis- tinct plan of invasion of England. They believed no Government would put the country..in a proper state of defence unless they were forced to do so by the man- hood of the nation. It was therefore necessary,through the means of that League, to let the people know the truth. Captain Appleton read the following telegram received from Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield : "Hearty good wishes for enthusiastic and successful meeting. to-night to promote the great and urgent question of securing an efficient auxiliary force, po.ver- ful in numbers, discipline, and equipment. for defence of the country. Very much regret preiious engage- ment prevents me corning." (Applause). Captain G. R. Crosfteld then moved "That this meet- ing is fully in favour of the scheme proposed by the National Service League.' National security, he con- tended, was of more importance than National pros- perity and that was why compulsory service was being. brough,t to the fore at present when the fiscal question was occupying so much attention. National prosperity said the promoters of the League, depended much more upon the character and the brains of the nation than upon any fiscal policy. Then again, the longer the con- sideration of the matter was deferred, the more they would forget about the War Commisajon. The pro- moters of the league were not acting aggreasively, but were putting forward the proposal that a man should give up 100 days of his life to military training in order that he might be of use in case the country should be invaded, on the grounds of defence and not offence. They did not want to promote bloodshed, but to avoid it. He agreed with the principle of arbitrating as far as ever they could, but there was a point when arbitra- tion would not succeed, and they must prepare for that. It was a very significant fact that every arbitral agree- ment that had so far been made between nations had started with the formal declaration against arbitrating about anything which would effect the honour, the vital interets, or the independence of those nations. There was a certain class of people of flabby intellects who said Europe would not be se unkind as to attack us, because the nations loved us and thecause of humanity too much. • .He considered, however,that a perfectly reasonable contingency would be the coalition of two powers against England. Our navy, continued the speaker, was equal in ships approximately to any two powers, but in men it was appallingly short. To every three men in the fiehting line we had only one man in reserve, whereas France and Russia had three men in reserve for everyone in the fighting line. Then again it must be borne in mind that our squadrons had, owing to the vastness of the e upire, to be scattered about very much more than the ntvies of other nationsl He did not. wish to ditract from the necessity of have ing a powerful navy. He knew that if the navy were- absolutely smashed up it would probably be the end of us. He conteaded, however,that they would strengthen the navy enormously—far More than by building battle ships, if they spent the equivalent amount of money in raising up a sound home army. Captain Crosfield pro- ceeded to point our the necessity for a strong army to guard the shores in cue the navy was led away by a ruse or temporarily "smashed up." We did not want to have the navy spread aboet around the coasts as a sort of protecting ring, The navy should find out where the enemy's fleet was and swoop down with telling effect upon its weak spots. How could it do that if the coasts of Englandevere not to be left un- protected? During the Boar War there were at one period only about seven battalions of soldiers left in the country and we were only fighting a force of about 80,009 men. Considering the extent of the empire and the many duties the army had to fulfil, it was absulutely impossible to make it large enough to uphold ourpower abroad and defend out shores in case of invasion. We had to rely for the second line of defence upon the anxiliarys forces would, under the present system and with the present training, he absolutely inadequate to repel a serious invasion or even a mere raid on the country. Captain Crosfield then detailed the proposals of the League which have already been 4iven in ful.:l in the "Examiner." In case of invasion, he pointed out, the home army would have to face the long-trained troops of the continent, and it would benecessary to radically change the present system of the auxiliary forces. One great advantage of the new scheme was that the training would be continuous. If they paid the Volunteers :ClO or more he doubted whether they would be willing to undergo two months' training. So long as it was entirely voluntary they would constantly be having to make the training more pleasant than useful. One object raised was that it was wrong to make a man fight for a cause when his con- science was against it. (Hear, hear.; He quite agreed with that in regard to a war outside this country, but if England were attacked, no matter how much a man might think his country was in the wrong it would be his duty to defend his hearth and home. (Applause.) He did not care a fig for a man's religion if it was going to stop him froet doing that. Dealing with the contention that militarism would be rife throughout the land, the speaker said he could quite understand that objection if the training proposed was the same as that undergone in Russia, Germany or Franco where men served for several years. Here, however, there would only be 100 days' training. The editor of one newspaper said that as soon as the War Office authorities found that 100 days were not sufficient they would increase the period to 200 days. That showed a most appalling ignorance of how the laws of the land were managed as any such change would have to be sanctioned by Parliament. In regard to the employment difficulty, if the proposals became law they would find that after a time the employers—as they did on the Continent— would come to recognize that the improvement of the men in physique, mental qualities and character in consequence of the military training, would far out- weigh any temporary disadvantage odcasioned by the men being obliged to go to camp. There were 100,000 men in the army to-day who were not fit to take the field and this awful wastage would be put an end to if the League proposals were adopted. • The saving thus effected would far more than compensate for the expense incurred in raising up this great na,tianal militia. Captainerosfield argued that other advantages of the system would be improvement in the physique of time nation, a stimulation of the education of the , upper classes in particular, and the binding together of the rich and poorer classes who would have to serve side by side. (Applause). Major Roeme seconded and discussed the question from a soldier's point of view. Many people, he said, did not realize that England had frequently been invaded and that Sheerness was burnt by the Dutch in about 1670. He referred to the state of preparedness in which we found the Boors when we entered upon the Transvaal campaign and emphasised the value of military discipline. 'Me South African struggle would be child's play compared with a, war on English soil; which would mean the loss of ten's of thousands of men in a few hours. Although he disliked compulsion or compulsory service for a soldier, he believed the League proposals were the only basis on which we could obtain an efficient defence of the country. England was in a state of unpreparedness for war, and if it were not remedied other nations would be taking advantage of it. He thought that in the heart of hearts of most Englishme.. the question of national defence came a long way before any fiacal question. (Applause). The resolution waa carried with one dissentient. Votes of thanks were accorded to the Chairman and speakers and the meeting concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL</p>
        <p>THE CIITCKET Crxtt.—The general meeting was held in the large room on Wednesday the 18th November, The Rector, the Rev. R. Green:Al, OcCdpied the chair. The treasurer's report showed a deficit of £,4 us. 81d. A ne-‘, pavilion was erected at the beginning, of the season, the cost being almost defrayed by sub- scription. The above item WRS mainly responsible for the adverse balance. The .report of the Secretary stated that bad weather had interfered with the number of matches played, but out of the sixteen carried through, nine were won, five lost, and two drawn. The most successful batsmen were :—W. Carter with an average of 11.8, W. Banks 11.4 and F. Williams 2:3.9. The latter only played five innings. W. Cartercarne out first in the bowling with an average of four runs per wicket, while R. Hallows came second with 4.06. The 2nd eieven played sixteen games, wen eight, lost five and drew three. R. Gifford and C. Smaile headed the batting list with averages of 2:3 (for five innings) and 8 respectively. Gifford took 20 wickets for 20 runs, E.Winter 4 for 15. and H. Carter 31 for 1 17. After the election of officers, it was proposed to hold a concert on the 16th of December in order to reduce the club's debt. The proceedings terminated with the presentation of a handsome clock to the vice- captain, W. Carter who recently entered the bon&amp; of matrimony.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS AND NEW YEAR PRESENTS., Our readers are reminded that Messrs. Gorge Henry Lee and Company' Ltd.,Basnett-street, Liverpool, have opened their annual exhibition of useful artibles and novelties suitable for Christmas and New Year pre- sents. There is a choice and admirably-varied stock ranging from silver and eleotro plate goods and jewellery to all kinds of toys and games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>! THE MAYORALI "AT HOME." 1 •</head>
        <p>! THE MAYORALI "AT HOME." 1 •</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs.Willians Bolton) held their first "At Home" at the Town Hall on Thursday. A large number of guests attended to pay their respects and convey their congratulations to the new Mayor and Mayoress. Mr. and Mrs. Bolton were accomp tale d by their two sons and two daughters. The guests were recaived in the Council Chamber which had been specially furnished as a reception 'room. Alderman Bolton wore his chain of office and Mrs. Bolton had a marmificent bouquet of choice and rare orchids, set round' with asparagus plumons. Light re- freshments were served in the adjoining rooms, but the limited accommodation was taxed to the utmost, and at times, the rooms were inconveniently crowded. Mr. R. H. Pearson's orchestra efficiently played a selection of music. The following was the programme :- 1 Overture. .."Tancredi," Rossini, "Dance of the Elves," Solomon. " Valse Enchantee,". Berger. Cello Solo. Incidental Music S:c., "Three Musketeers," Berceuse "Petite Reine," Valse. "Osterreichs Klange," "Minuet," Cello Solo. "Egyptian March," " Valse Bleue," . Cornelius March," . "God Save the King." The entrance hall had been tastefully decorated with a collection of palms and plants, introduced from the Park conservatories, and arranged with effect by Mr. Parker. The catering was again very satisfactorily attended to by Mr. and Mrs. Powys. The furnishing and decorating had been undertaken by Mr. Ralph Johnson.</p>
        <p>3....</p>
        <p>...Rosse.</p>
        <p>Warm. Cutler.</p>
        <p>1 9</p>
        <p>Straups. Margis. .Mendelssohn</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The ant:cipated two points accrued to Warrington from their engagement with Huddersfield on Saturday, though, as expected, the Fartowners made a bold bid for success. They were greatly encouraged by a recent triumph over Leigh, and by the absence of Fish from the Warrington side, but they found the home- sters in strong form. The score, 7 points to 3 points, hardlY represents the extent of Warrington's superiority, as they were on the attack durint, three parts of the game, and Huddersfield had scarcely any chance over and above the one they turned to account.</p>
        <p>Saturday was about the w wst football Saturday of the season, and clever play was militated against by a. driving wind and heavy showers of rain. Such atmos- pheric conditions as these naturally had an effect on , the" gate," - but a crowd of from three to four I thousand assembled, and they were rewarded by an interesting game. The first half was positively I brilliant, both teams playing fast, open hotball and attacking alternately. Warrington had the gale against them, but this did not appear to effect then' play, and they 'surprised. the Huddersfield men with a vigotous attack. Hockenhall, had he Fish's speed, would have scored in the first five minutes from a fine opening made by Isherwood, but he was overhauled by Deere. Then the visitors descended upon the Warrington citadel and it took Hallam, Isherwood, and Hock-en- hail all their time to keep the speedy right wing out. The home forwards changed the venue, and smart passing by Davies, Mahoney, and Dickenson resulted in a perfect piece of centre play by the latter. He served Harris at the right moment, the Huddersfield men were left standing, and a try behind the posts recorded for Warrington. Harris crossed the line much nearer touch, and adopted the,risky plan of dodging his way round the goal though twice tackled. However, nothing succeeds like success and Dicken- son was enabled to kick an easy goal.</p>
        <p>Five points against the wind was splendid from a Warrington point of view, but it did not suit Deere and Co. They were soon at the other end and smart .work near the line resulted in the speedy Welshman. getting _a try at the corner. No goal resulted, and a fine opportunity of drawing level by means of a pinaltv goal was missed soon after. Half-time arrived with Warrington leading by 5 points to 3 points.</p>
        <p>It was generally expected that on resumption War- rington would over-rim their opponents, and for a time they did so, bat Harris was too well watched to repeat his feat of the first half. The visiting forwards changed their tactics, and by screwing the scrums they contrived to make the game close and spoil Warrington's combination. By this tne-ans they kept the score down, but with a smart and speedy set of backs it was hardly the game to play even from a Huddersfield point of view, unless they already admitted themselves beaten. Such tactics naturally robbed the game of its variety and attractiveness and Warrington did well to get another couple of points through a penalty goal from the foot of Dickenson. Huddersfield only crossed the half way line twice in the second half and were a soundly beaten team by the close of play.</p>
        <p>In point of 'style there was not a great deal to choose between the teams but Warrington struck me. as being the more effective side. Huddersfield started well but seemed afraid to play out for points in the second half, while Warrington were always on the go. At half-back there was some hesitation in passing the ball which needs to be avoided if the threequarters are to do their best work. The latter were in good form,and I was particularly pleased to see Dickenson opening out the game for Harris in the most approved style. Hallam was again in rfflre trim and quite over-shadowed Walton, whose kicking was very poor. Larard played well in the centre for the visitors and saw that Deere got plenty of work, while Holroyd and Muxworthy put in some effective play.</p>
        <p>Brong,hton Rangers successfully accounted for Hull Kingston Rovers. who now tie With Salforci for second place, Oldham leOing the way with a two points margin. At the other end of the table are Huddersfield, Swinton, Keighley and Widnes, all boasting of fi noints and fighting hard to get away from the wooden spoon.</p>
        <p>The league table up-to-date is as follows : For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Oldham V) 9 9 1..14 24-100 5 5-25...19 Salford 11... 8... 2..: 1...21 29-109... 5 9-37...17 Hull K't'n R 11... 8... 2... 1...10 16— 68... 5 9-37...17 Hunslet l2 S. 3. 1..17 20— 94...11 12-58...17 Bradford Lends 11... 7...,3... 1... 7 18— 68... 7 12-50...15 Warrington it... 6... 3... 2...11 12— 58... 8 8-40...14 pleghton R. 11... 7... 4... 0... 9 2.5 93...13 10-55...14 Halifax 11... 5... 5... 1... 7 34.... 8 8-40...11 Runcorn Hull Wigan Leigh Batley Huddersfild 12... 3... 9... 0... 6 20— 72...15 25-105... G Swinton Keighley Widnes l2 ° 8. 2...10 9 47...17 31-127.. 6</p>
        <p>There should be a 'fine struggle at Bradford to-day where Warrington furnish the opposition. Both team have done well so far, and on r -suits may be considered still in the running for the championship As a rule the meeting between'the two furnishes an excellent game. Last season Warrington drew at Bradford in a rapid scoring game---eleven points each , —and the year before they won outright, so that the auguries for to-day.are not unfavourable. Warring- ton are deterinmed on making a big effort, and it is gratifying to find the strongest pssible team selected. ish is once more in harness, and if he signalises his e'urn by one of those electrifying displays he Is rcanable of, no one will be better pleased than Ovnlist."</p>
        <p>-- The -Warrington side is :--Hallam; Fish, Isher- Boardman, Morrison wood, Dickenson, and Harris Davies and Afahoney ; Cook. Thomas, Lunt, Harmer,Edmundsou, and Naylor. It is not decided yet which forward will stand 'down. .</p>
        <p>J. Swift is suffering from an injured ankle but will, it is expected, soon be "fit."</p>
        <p>The " A." team is expected to wipe the greensward with Plank-lane Wanderers to-dal, and they have a. good team for the purpose with Knight fullback, Kenyon, Hockenball, and Savage, three-quarters; Bate and l?,dmnnds at half. Savage is it well-built young soldier from the barracks, and he gets his first trial on the wing to-day. •</p>
        <p>TN SEARCH OF .GAME AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>John Topping, of 11, Tickle-street, Parr, St. Helens, was fined 10s. and costs at Newton Police Court on Thursday, for being on land at Ilaydock belonging to Lord Newton, on October 21, in search of game. Defendant, who is a collier, gave an address at Haydock, which was found to be incorrect. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted.</p>
      </div>
