        <p>2. What does it contain</p>
        <p>S. What is its action</p>
        <p>4. What Diseases is it specially recommended for? SHOCKING TRAMCAR ACCIDENT. Influenza, and the after effects attack of Nathaniel Fairhurst, a robust little chap of five, met Influenza, Indigestion, Chest Affect ns n 1 Diseases of with a terrible accident on Monday at St. Helens, when l the Liver in all their various forms. Neuralgia and all he was knocked down by an electric car proceeding up Nervous Disorders, Depression of Spirits, General Park-road, from Haydock to St. Helens. The bqy is the I only child of William Fairhurst, a collier, living at 5, Weakness, Loss of Appetite, Sleeplessness. Critchley-street, of Park-road. He was in the roadway about half-past three in the afternoon, when he was caught by the car. The driver, Fred Potter, pulled up at once, but the lad was carried somedistance, and when the car stopped, he was found practically under the wheels. Both legs were almost cut off at the thigh, the bones being smashed, and he received a compound fracture of one arm. The child subsequently died, and at St, Helens Town Hall, on Wednesday, Mr. Brighouse held the inquest—George Collins, grocer, of Hard- lane, said he saw the accident happen shortly after half-past three on Monday afternoon. He was riding on his motor bicycle, on the tram track, and, seeing a car coming up, he went off on to the macadam. He then saw that a lurry was standing on the macadam. Just as be got alongside of the lurry the little boy dashed from behind it. On hearing the car he then paused, and was going to turn back, but the car struck him, and knocked him forwards. He then went under the car, and was dragged forward in front of the wheels. The Coroner: In your opinion could the driver have done any more than he did ?—Witness I don't think so.—Fred Potter, the driver, of 40, Brynn-street gave evidence.—The jury returned a verdict of "Accident, and said that no blame attached to the driver.</p>
        <p>Must patients confine themselves within doors when using it 1</p>
        <p>; the working man need not abstain from his labour; the mother from her household duties, or the child be kept from school while using it.</p>
        <p>6. Is there any difficuity in procuring it</p>
        <p>There need be none, as it can be obtained of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or will be forwarded direct by the Proprietors, carriage free, on receipt of stamps. It is sold in bottles 2.5. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each. Beware of Imitations. See the name " Gwilym Evans" on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
