      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A LONDONER'S WARRINGTON ADVENTURES.— SINGULAR CASE. A singular tale was unfolded to the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday when Mary Ann M'Carthy and Edward M'Carthy, last from Wigan, were charged with stealing from the person of Robert Clare, in the Queen's Hotel yard, Sankey-street, at eight o'clock on Monday night,a silver lever watch and rolled gold chain, value .22.—The Chief Constable asked for a remand for a week to inquire into the antecedents of the prisoners, and said there was still some property to be recovered. The complainant was a young man of about twenty and he came on Monday from London to Warrington for the purpose of taking a situation. On arriving at the Central Station at 2.30 in the afternoon he acted very peculiarly. As soon as he got into the street he saw the female prisoner, who was a complete stranger to him. She accosted him, and offered to find him lodgings, and asked him to treat her. He told her the story of his coming from London,and she then took charge of his portmanteau containing his effects. Clare foolishly, gave her a sovereign for some purpose or other. He saw her no more just then, but she after- wards rejoined him and introduced him to the male prisoner and another man, who, she said, were her brothers. They spent a good deal of their time to- gether, and eventually, at eight o'clock in the evening, they went to the Queen's Hotel. The female prisoner persuaded complainant to go into the yard, and the other two men followed. They knocked the com- plainant about in a. semi-jocose manner, and when he got into the public-house again he found his watch and chain and, a sum of money missing. The prisoners had decamped, but complainant followedthe femaleprisoner and demanded his watch and chain. She replied: "I have not got it. Don't get-me pinched, and I will get it for you." They returned to the hotel and the woman handed prisoner the watch, the back having been wrenched off. Information was given to the police,and Detective-Superintendent Monaghan arrested the prisoners' who, when charged, said, "Is that all ?" The money had not yet been recovered. He (the Chief Con- stable) could scarcely have believed that any young man of twenty years could have been guilty of such folly, but the statement just given had come from com- plainant's own lips.—Prisoners were remanded for a 'week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Sniethurst, Councillor Thornton, Messrs. F. Monks, _</p>
        <p>, Birchall. Hayward, and Wright. CHILDREN NEGLECTED.—EIiza Appleton, 21, Lord Nelson-street, was charged with neglecting her six children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary -suffering and injury to health —Mr. Browne, who prose- cuted on behalf of the National Society for the Pre- vention of Cruelty to Children, stated that the present case depended upon the evidence of the husband, who complained that his wife gave way to drink. The matter had been under the supervision of Inspector Hadkett since last January. From time to time improvements had taken place, but only temporarily. Last week the inspector and Dr. Peacocke visited the house, and the doctor stated that the house was unfit for habitation; and the children appeared badly fed. and were verminous.—Evidence was given by the hus- band, Inspector Hackett and Dr. Peacocke, who said the house was in a very bad condition. The husband said his wife was continually pawning the clothing. The ease was adjourned for four weeks to give the defend- ant a further chance of amendment. AN UNFORTUNATE PURCHASE.—Arthur Dawson, 23 Wood-street, Earlestown, was charged with cruelty to a pony ou November 14. He pleaded guilty. inspector Gibson, Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals, said that at ten o'clock on the above date he was in Knutsford-road and saw the defendant driving a pony attached to a coster's cart laden with vegetables. The pony was very emaciated and suffered from spavin ' and strained tendons. Defendant said he bought the animal a fortnight ago and had been "done." A fine of 10s. including costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Mr. C. White.(in the chair) and Alderman ._Fairclough _ . .</p>
        <p>YOUTHFUL TirtEvEs'.--William Ashton, jun., 20, Litton's-row, ;John Clare, jun., 18, Litton's-row, Henry</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>cepriliVitgookfeAVlANAFlNGetira S 1 4 ~.‘ .; 5,- ....-• , ... ~... ~ .</head>
        <p>cepriliVitgookfeAVlANAFlNGetira S 1 4 ~.‘ .; 5,- ....-• , ... ~... ~ .</p>
        <p>THE WORRIES OF THE RICH.</p>
        <p>One of the most significant facts of modern life is the rapid yet steady increase in the death rate from nervous diseases. The feverish anxiety of the rich to amass still greater wealth, the strain and worry attending the con- stantly increasing competition and diminishing profits of the tradesmen, the unhealthy surroundings and bitter struggle of the poor to make ends meet. all tend to produce an enfeebled and depressed condition of the nervous system, which, reacting on the' digestive organs lays the foundation of many distressing and serious diseases, both mental and physical indeed, in the opinion of some of the most eminent medical authorities, cancer, that dreaded disease which claims more and more victims each year, is dependent for its hold on the human body almost entirely on depressed vital conditions such as inevitably result from a breakdown of the digestive and nervous systems. It is no exaggeration to assert that fully one half of the diseases which cripple and torture mankind have their origin in some simple derangement of the stoma, liver or kidneys, and might be avoided altogether by a timely resort to the digestive tonic. Mother Seigel's Syrup. A record of the cases of Chronic indigestion, gastritis, anremia, nervous collapse, etc., within the knowledge of the writer, that have been completely and permanently cured by Mother Seigel's Syrup, when all the ordinary methods of treatment and medicines known to the Medical Faculty have utterly failed, would more than fill every column of this paper. One instance only can be recorded here. In a solemn declaration made at Rochdale before Mr. H. Chadwick, a commissioner for oaths, on April 16th 1903, Mrs. Mary Jane Rylancls, of 125, Oldham road, Rochdale, states that following an attack of influenza she was for two vears always ailing. "Mv appetite failed." she says, "and whatever I ate caused such pain that T became afraid to eat at all. Sometimes the pain would be in my stomach, at other times it would grip me in the hack or between the shoulders. I also suffered acutely from headache and frequently was so dizzy that I had to hold on to the furniture for support. Mv heirt palpitated to such an extent that I came to think it was diseased. After two years of this misery T had a most violent attack, and for six weeks lay in bed. The doctor told my husband that I was suffering from ulceration of the stomach and heart disease, and added that T might go off at any moment. Believing this my husband sat up with me night after night for three weeks. My stomach at this time felt as if it was burning, and I was so constipated that it almost amounted to complete stoppage of the bowels. Nothing but a little brandy and water would stay on my stomach. "However, when all seemed hopeless, I heard of Seigel's Syrup and commenced taking it. Gradually it restored me to health and I have never suffered since. T am now as well as ever before in my life and am abso- lutely certain that it is to Seigel's Syrup I owe my life." Such is the voluntary and solemn declaration of a woman who, having herself been restored to health is desirous that others should benefit by her experience and testimony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>HORSE-HIRING DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>A HORSE-HIRING DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>Horse Over-Worked and Injured. CLAIM AT THE WARRINGTON COUNTY</p>
        <p>COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, a claim was made by Thomas Dolan, contractor. of Glrven-place for £lO 10s., damages for a horse, against J. Berng.rd, of the Wilderspool Steam Laundry. Warrington.—Mr. Cromp- ton (Messrs. Browne and. Crompton) represented the plaintiff. and Mr. Unsworth defended. Mr. Crompton for the plaintiff, explained that there had been several transactions between the plaintiff and defendant. On different occasions he had 'hired horses for the defendant's use. On the 28th September, the defendant sent his man to the plaintiff's premises tohire a horse for the purpose of doing a round in Warrington and 'afterwards in Earlestown and Newton. He asked for a horse named Daisy which the defendant had had on previous occasions and which the plaintiff lent to him. The plaintiff askeA what time he would return and he said he would be back at nine o'clock. The plaintiff waited for his return until that hour and then went away, leaving his man to wait. The horse was not returned until eleven o'clock and it was noticed that the skin was broken on the knees. The knees were both broken and the animal was in a very bad condition. The plaintiff went to see the manager of the laundry, Mr. Shenton. and complained to him about the condi- tion of the horse. Mr. Shenton called his driver who said in his presence that the horse was in a bad condition, through being overworked. She had been all round the Newton and Earlestown district, and after he got back to Warrington be had to go to Preston Brook. When he rea.ched Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath. the horse fell down through sheer exhaustion. The plaintiff asked for compensation, but nothing was done at; the time. The plaintiff afterwards received a letter from Mr. Shenton, who said he had referred the matter to the defendant., who declined to entertain any claim in respect of the animal, as he said it was con- tinually falling down. The defendant kired the horse out, supposing it to be fit for a day's work, but no one could say that the horse in question was fit to do a day's work. He therefore declined to entertain any question for damages. as the horse was as had when it came into his possession as when it left it. The plaintiff bore out Mr. Crompton's statement. He contended that the horse was overworked. the distance covered in the day being 42 mile's. The defendant sent , the engine driver with the horse and he was not a com- petent man. Witness had to sell the mare for £2. Peter Maginn gave evidence that he sold the horse some time ago for £5. It had met with an accident and had broken its knees. He estimated that the horse travelled 30 miles on the day in question. That was a fair day's work for the animal.—Re-examined: It was working too long on the day in question. • John F. Dixon, veterinary surgeon, said he saw the mare and came to the conclusion that she had been driven too quickly or too long. He advised the plaintiff to sell the mare. Mr. Unsworth said the defence was that there had been no negligence on the defendant's part, the affair 'being purely accidental. The horse was a very poor one and had previously fallen down, breaking its knees. John Smith, who was in charge of the horse, gave evidence and said he was not the regular driver. His duties were to drive the engine at the laundry. Mr. Shenton. manacer of the laundry, said Smith received no instructions to ask for a particular horse. His Honour considered it was an act of negligence to allow Smith to drive the animal if he was only the engine driver. After further evidence, the judgment was given for , the plaintiff for £7 7s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
