      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE v. YORKSHIRE. This match was played at Runcorn on Wednesday,be- fore a thousmd spectators. Cheshire lost the toss, and Walker kicked off against a strong wind. After Mosby and Price hatl shown up well for their respective sides, Molyneux started a passing movement. Price passed to Jones, who had a clear run in. Molyneux failed at goal. Yorkshire attacked, but Hough defended well. West received, outdistanced Davies, and scored at the corner. Little missed the goal. Play continued in the Cheshire half, but the Cheshire backs continued to play splendidly, and Price and Richardson made good runs, the ball going into touch at the Yorkshire corner flag. The Yorkshire pack broke back to centre, and Hough was called upon. Piice put in a smart run, but Mosby collared him, and play was still in the Cheshire half. The home forwards gained possession continually in the scrummages, but the backs were unable to get clear. West made efforts to get over. The Cheshire three-quarter line passed well, andl Davies being surrounded, Price took a bad pass over the line for Molyneux to kick a goal. Yorkshire again rushed to the Cheshire quarters, when Little placed a penalty goal. Morley then scored at the corner, Little failing. Laidlaw scored another try, Little kicking a goal. Half-time :—Yorkshire, two goals three tries (13 points) ; Cheshire, one goal two tries (8 points). On resuming Hough, the Cheshire full back, retired hurt for a short time, and the Yorkshire forwards over- running their opponents tries were quickly forthcoming from Jenkins and West, neither of which were improved upon; and though Cheshire played up pluckily they were outplayed. Yorkshire, however, failed to take advantage ot their opportunities until the closin,-, stages, when Morley ran over under the posts and Mosby placed a goal. Final: Yorkshire, three goals six tries (24 points); Cheshire, one goal two tries (eight points). reams : _ _</p>
        <p>Cheshire: R. Hough (Runcorn), back; D. Jones and H. Price (Runcorn), and J. E. Davies (Birkenhead), three-quarter backs; J. Richardson (Runcorn) and .1. Molyneux (Leigh), half hacks; S. Walker, A. Moulton, J. Tomlinson, and A. Richardson (Runcorn), J. Belton and J. W. Davies (Birkenhead). forwards. Yorkshire: W. B. Little (Halifax), hack; G.H. West (Hull Kingston Rovers), E. P. Mosby (Bradford), and J. P. Jenkins (Leeds), three-quarter backs; J. Morley (Halifax) and R. Ward (Leeds), half backs; A. Starks (Hull Kingston Rovers), J. Riley and J. Swinbank (Halifax), J. G. Moffatt (Leeds), A. Laidlaw (Bradford) and G. F. Fletcher (Hull Kingston Rovers). forwards. Referee, J. H. Smith (Widnes); touch judges, W. Jones (Cheshire) and F. Lister (Yorkshire). DURHAM AND NORTHUMBERLAND v. CU MBERL AND.</p>
        <p>This match was played at South Shields on Wednes- day. Cumberland had one change, Titnney (Maryport) coming into the three-quarters line vice Spottiswoode (Oldham). The visiting county at once pressed, Crane scoring. Sleep was injured, and had to retire. Crane got over with another try, and soon afterwards Timney followed with another, but Stephenson failed to con- vert in both cases. Durham and Northumberland im- proved, but could not score. Interval :—Cumberland, three tries(nine points); Durham and Northumberland, nothing. The visitors resurr_ed in promising style, but Moralee saved splendidly. The home forwards dribbled, well down, Hector scoring from a penalty. The twin coun- ties cOntiuned attacking strongly, and Gordon experi- enced hard lines. The home three-quarters showed better combination, but the Cumberland defence was reliable. Kitson beat Stephenson near the posts and 'scored, Hector landing a goal. Shortly afterwards Sinclair got over. Hector being again successful with the goal kick. Result :—Durham and Northumberland three goals and two tries (twelve points) ; Cumberland three tries (nine points:) DURHAM AND NORTHUMBERLAND v. LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Lancashire County Union Com- mittee was held at the Gros-Senor Hotel, Manchester, on Tuesday evening, Mr. Robert Walmsley (Salford) presiding. The following team was selected to play for Lanca- shire against Durham and Northumberland at South Shields on December 9 :—D. Smith (Salford), fullback; J. Fish (Warrington), R. Wilson (captain), and A. Hogg (Broughton Rangers), three-quarter backs; J. Lomas (Salford) and J. Baxter (Rochdale Hornets) halfbacks; P. Tnnney, J. Rhapps, G. Heath (Salford), J. Ferguson and G. Frater (Oldham), and G. Boardman (Leigh), forwards. . _ .</p>
        <p>Reserves—J. Hallam (Warrington), full back; P. Thomas (Oldham), centre, J. Leytham (Lancaster), wing three-quarter back; S. James (Broughton Rangers), half hack; A. S. Boardman (Warriagton), H. Buckler (Salford), and J. R. Hilton (Wigan), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>At Warrington, before 4,000 spectators. Warrington played against wind and rain during the first half, and clever play ISy iheir forwards enabled them to attack, despite some fine work by Larard and Brady, and the Huddersfield goal had a narroW escape. A round of passing between Davies, Mahoney, 'lsherwood and Hockenhall was successfully met by Deere, and event- ually Huddersfield got the advantage, Bragy opened out the game in smart manner,andan opening was made for Deere, but Hallam stopped him. Moseley then kicked across to Larard, who passed badly to Deere and the Warrington line again had a narrow escape. The home forwards dribbled to the "25," where Dickenson passed to Harris, who raced over Dickenson landing the extra points. Huddersfield rallied in fine style after this, and Deere eventually got In at the corner, but the goal kick failed, and at half-time Warrington led by 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), to Huddersfield 1 try (3 points). On resuming Warrington had the elements in their favour, but the Huddersfield forwards played a close game. A capital effort by Moseley was negatived by Dickenson, and for a time the visitors were engaged in defensive work. Harris was twice pulled up by Mux- worthy and Moseley near the line whilst Dickenson failed to place a penalty goal. The defence of the Huddersfield men was very fine. Near the close Dicken- son landed a penalty goal, aud the result was :—War- rington 2 goals and 1 tity (7 points), Huddersfield 1 try (3 paints). Teams:— Warrington—Hallam, hack: Harris, Dickenson, Isher- wood, and Hockenhall, three-quarter backs; Davies and Mahoney, half backs: Morrison, Cook, Thomas, Lunt Jolley, Boardman, Naylor, flo:1 Edmondson, forwards. I Huddersfield—Walton, back; Deere, Larard, Max- worthy. and Moseley, three-quarter backs; Brady and I Holroyd, half backs; Charleqworth, Chambers, Driver, I Littlewood, Nlicklethwaite, Osborne, Pool and Sutcliffe, forwards. I Referee. Mr. F. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judges, I Messrs. T. H. Robinson, Runcorn, and A. Critchley, ; Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUNCORN v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Runcorn were nnable to put their full strength into the field. The wind blew half a gale and ra in fall, so that the conditions were not favourable to g ood play. With the wind behind them in the first half, Runcorn failed to score, and subsequently were kept on the de- fensive. Jenkins got in,and Smithson added a second try, both the kicks failing, and Leeds won by two tries (six points) to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>HULL v. WIDNES</head>
        <p>HULL v. WIDNES</p>
        <p>At Hull. The match was played in a strong wind,and was a one-sided affair, Hull having all the best of it, and winning by 21 points to fivo. Early in the game Thompson scored for Hull near the posts, but the place kick failed. Carvill added two tries in quick succession, and Harrison kicked two penalty goals, while Carvill scored another try. After changing ends Thompson kicked a goal from a try by Rhodes, and Widnes gained a try and placed a goal. Result :—Hull, three goals and five tries (21 points) ; Widnes, one goal and one try (five points).</p>
        <p>SALFORD v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>A well-contested game was played by these clubs on the Salford ground, and although Salford managed to obtain a victory by five points there was very little to choose between the teams. LEIGH v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, in stormy weather, before 2,000 spectators. The game was divided into four twenties, owing to the great force of the wind. Bradford played with the wind in the first quarter and kept Leigh defending.— Bradford, three goals and a try (nine points), Leigh, one goal and two tries (eight points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LEADING FOOD BEVERAGE.</head>
        <p>THE LEADING FOOD BEVERAGE.</p>
        <p>Don't overrate your powers of endurance. Take due precautions, and face the world with the comforting assurance that you will be able to stand the strain. What you really need,to do this effectually is a beverage at once refreshing, pleasant to the palate, and strengthening to both mind and body. Such a food beverage is Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. It has made happy many a home which otherwise to-day would have been wretched, and the ultimate effects of which it is impos sible to foresee. For breakfast, dinner, supper—indeed at an' hour of the twenty-four—Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa will be found light and refreshing ; it leads the way among all beverages, and nothing can equal it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
