      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>. . . ' • . , "....-,.....--- ...it -Ado ' ' , .... • . , – – _ . . ', ;,40. ll', . ' • , • . . , ... _ , I .., ... ________ . – . • . . , . , . ____.....- ______ .. . .. ~.. ..</head>
        <p>. . . ' • . , "....-,.....--- ...it -Ado ' ' , .... • . , - - _ . . ', ;,40. ll', . ' • , • . . , ... _ , I .., ... ________ . - . • . . , . , . ____.....- ______ .. . .. ~.. ..</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 28, 1903</p>
        <p>KEIGHLEV v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Oldham gained a victory over the home club at Keighley by two goals and two tries (ten points)to one goal (two points). Facing a strong wind Oldham did well in the first half. Spottiswoode scoring a fine try, which Ferguson improved. Changing ends', Keighley played up in better style, but they could not penetrate the Oldham. defence. P. Thomas added a try to the visitors score and Ferguson again proved successful at the place kick. Walker landed a penalty goal for Keighley, and Oldhaia won by ton points to two.</p>
        <p>BATLEY v. HALIFAX. At Batley. The home side won this game by two goals and a try (seveti points) to two goals(four points). A high wind interfered a good deal with the play. Two goals were kicked for Halifax in the earlier portion of the match, this being the extent of the scoring up to half time. After change of ends Batley continually pressed, but lacked finish. W. Davies placed a goal for them, following which Spurr gained a try, which Davies improved, Batley thus winning by seven points to four.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>HUNSLET V. SWINTON.</head>
        <p>HUNSLET V. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Hunslet, but owing to the wretched weather there was a poor attendance of spec- tators. Hunslet turned out as selected, but Swinton were without Lewis and Hampson, whose places were taken by Barnett and Greenwood. At half time Hunslet led by seven points to nothing. On changing ends it was expected that Swinton would quickly reduce this lead,but battling againSt the wind and rain in the first half began to tell on them, and Hunslet again assumed the upper hand. Their forwards carried all before them, and through the efforts of J. Whiteley and Ramage eight more points were added to their half-time score. Wal- work, from a pass by Chorley, scored a try for Swinton, which Evans converted, and the game ended :—Hunslet three goals and three tries (15 points) ; Swinton, one goal and one try (five,points). BROUGHTON RANGERS V. miLL KINGSTON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ROVERS.</head>
        <p>ROVERS.</p>
        <p>The first encounter between these clubs took place on the Rangers' ground; and ended in the defeat of the Rovers for the second time this season. The Rovers won the toss, and took advAtage of a strong wind in the first half of the game. The Rangers, however, quickly got to work, and a try ought to have been recorded in the first few minutes, but Harry failed to take a pass from Wilson on the visitors' line. The Rangers continued to attqck, and Sinclair just managed to fall on the ball after tke home forwards had dribbled it over the line. Sleep at last made a fine opening for Medley, who had a clear field before him, but he failed to elude Poole, whilst a few minutes later Gordon missed an easy try through failing to take a pass from Stevenson. A splendid dashing run by Widdeson scored a try for the Rangers, but no goal resulted. Then the Rovers played tic; desperately, and the game became rather rough, and the referee had to order one of the. visiting forwards off the field. At half-time the Rangers led by three points to nothing. Continuous rain eventually made the ball heavy and greasy, and play in the second half was of a scrambling nature. Harry scored a try, and the Rangers won by two tries (six points) to Hull Kingston Rovers nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>LANCAS HIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCAS HIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>ROCHDALE HORNETS WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>This match was pl,iyed on the Athletic ground,Roch- dale, on Saturday. In the first half the game was of an even nature throughout, and the nearest approach ta a score was an effort by Knight to place a penalty goal In the second half the Warringtonians were always the superior side, and a magnificent kick by Knight opened the score for the visitors. The " Wirepullers " con- tinued their aggressive work, and after Edmunds and Jordan had been pulled up on the line, a mark to Edmunds was successfully negotiated by Knight. The visitors excelled in the finer arts of the game, and throughout this half the Hornets were rarely dangerous. Final: Warrington, two goals (four points) ; Rochdale Hornets, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>INTER-WORKS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTER-WORKS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS BROS. V. WALKER'S.—PIayed on Saturday and resulted in a win for the former by 5 points to nil. NAYLOR'S V. LONDON&amp; NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>_ Played on the ground of the former at Howley. Owing to the strong gale of wind four quarters werede- cided upon. The visitors losing the toss started opera- tions, and Naylor's having the advantage of the ele- ments were early aggressive, but could score nothing tangible. The railway men playing an effective game gradually gained a hold in Navlor's half, and Lloyd opening out the play passed and repassed with Jackson who got clear away, but unfortunately stumbled near the line and missed a certain score. This was a narrow squeak for the hornesters. who signified the let off by rushing to neutral ground where the ftrstquarter ended. The railway now having the wind behind them forced the pace, and several outbursts were nipped in the bud by safe tackling. AW length,however,the visitors' backs combined welLand Hewitt served Jackson for that player to score an unimproved try.—Half-time: L.and N.-W.R., 1 try; Naylor's,nil. After the interval Naylor's forwards headed by Hassall played up strongly, but the defence was sound, play raged at centre, the Locos were penalized and Morgan dropped a splendid goal. This reverse roused the visitors and after passing Timmins tricked his opponents and gave smartly to Jackson, who again rounded his men and planted the ball behind the posts, Taylor placing a goo!. The last quarter was all in favour of the North-Western the strong wind spoiling many good efforts but towards the close Lloyd broke away and shipping through the defence scored a capital try. Taylor converted from a difficult angle. Final score: L. and N.-W. 2 goals 3 tries (13 points), to Naylor's 1 goal (2 points.) CROSFIELD V. CABLE.—Cancelled.</p>
      </div>
