      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TERRIBLE CHARGE AGAINST A FATHER.</p>
        <p>Peter Doward, of 45, Havelock-street, was charged,at St. Helens on Monday with feloniously and violently, assaulting his daughter, Harriet Doward. Mr. H. L. Riley prosecuted for the National Society for the Pre- i ventiou of Cruelty to Children, and said the girl was born on May 12, 1889, and was therefore fourteen and a half years old on the 12th of this month. He asked for Doward to be committed to the assizes on three t charges—namely, rape in the month of June, also on the 7th of September, and in connection with the second date with an offence under the Criminal Law Amendment Act. On the first occasion the girl told her mother what had occurred, and she taxed her husband with it, whereupon he said he would cut both the mother and ; the girl up if ever he heard a whisper of the matter I again. In the early part of this month Mrs. Doward, who already had seven children living, was very ill, and about to be confined. She then told the nurse what had occurred, and after the girl had been questioned, the nurse communicated with a clergyman, and he with the society. Ten days after her confinement the girl's mother died, and the husband was arrested after the funeral. In answer to the charge he said it was false. —The girl gave evidence. Doward was dommitted 3 the assizes for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN BANKRUPTCY CA 1:</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Bankruptcy Court, on Friday, on the application of Messrs. H. B. White and Sons, solicitors, Mr. Registrer Ridgway made a receiving order in bankruptcy against Messrs. W. and N. Stephens, of 24, Sankey-street, Earlestown, builders. The application was made on the petition of the debtors themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MORE BOOKMAKERS FINED.</head>
        <p>MORE BOOKMAKERS FINED.</p>
        <p>At Newton-le-Willows Police Court on Thursday four well-known Earlestown bookmakers were brought up for street betting. Matthew Seton, on the 18th inst., was proved to have taken sixteen bets between 1.30 and 2.30 in the afternoon. Defendant, who did not appear was fined £5 and costs.—Frank Dale, who was stated to have taken two bets in Earle-street was also fined £5 and costs.—ln the case of James Lee, Mr. J. W. Knowles, who prosecuted, stated that it was not necessary to prove that a man made a bet under thecountry by-laws; if he used the streets for the purpose of carrying on the business of betting it was sufficient. Police officers deposed that Lee took his clients into a back street after perusing a newspaper, and that Lee subsequently made entries in a book. Defendant denied it, but was fined £5 and costs.—Walter Waterman denied having made bets on the evening of the same day, but a dis- guised sergeant swore that he was close to the defendant when he received two coins from a man who made a bet, whilst another constable, also in disguise, proved seeing defendant making use of the street for betting purposes. Defendant, who had been up six times before was fined £5 and costs. IMPORTANT PROPERTY SALE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening at the Wellington Hotel Earlestown, before a large company, Mr. Leach, of St Helens, brought under his hammer, several lots of property, all of which was keenly competed for, and sold at good prices. The sale was rendered necessary by the death of Mr. R. Kilshall, at one time a farmer in a large way of business at Burtonwood. The first lot offered was a cottage at Green-Jones-brow, Burtonwood, let at a rental of £7 16s. per annum. This was pur- chased for £lOO by Mr. Dutton, Burtonwood.--Lot 2 was a semi-detached residence, No. 71, Cross-lane, Earlestown, the annual rental being £24. The bidding for this was exceptionally spirited, and was ultimately sold for £440 to Mr. Moon.—Lot 3 was a similar house, 73, Cross-lane, but it only brought £320, the purchaser being Mr. T. Lythgoe. —Lot 4 was four dwelling houses known as "The Beeches," in Park-road, Newton, and these were knocked down at £1,200 to Mr. Moon for Mr. Jordan. Lot 5, a dwelling house, known as "Bradley View," at a rental of .£23 10s. per annum, was sold to Mr. Rigby for £250. Lot 6, two semi-detached residences situate lin Park-road, Newton, were sold at £525 to Mr. G. F. Clarke. Lot 7, the shop and dwelling house No. 16, Cross-lane, was knocked down for £315 to Mr. White. Lot 8, seven dwelling houses in St. John-street Earlestown, with a total rental of al per annum were sold to Mr. Rudd, for £942. Twenty-one shares in Farr's Bank were disposed of for £B5 ner share, and 28 shares in Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Company, were sold, for -£5O 5s each for 7 of them and £49 15s. each for the remainder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>CHAUFEUR FINED AT FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at Frodsham,Raoul Ananell,a chauffeur employed by a gentleman residing at the Midland Hotel, Manchester, was summoned for furiously driving a motor car in Frodsham on the sth inst. t vidence showed there was a dense fog, and the defendant was driving at twenty to twenty five miles per hour. He collided with a cart, and reported the accident to the police, urging them to summon the man with the horse. One witness described the speed of the car as terrific, and said it would have taken a clever chap to shoot defendant as he passed (laughter). He was fined ,C 5 and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS._</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS._</p>
        <p>On Monday, before Messrs. J. Ockleston and S. Taylor, Joseph Bushell. Nelson-street, was fined 15s. or in default 14 days for using obscenr language in Church- street and Nelson-street, on the 14th inst. P.C. Bmtt proved the case. Prisoner read the language alleged to have been used and observed that it was very good. There were 20 previous convictions. Inspector Eger- ton said he was always there and they did not know what to make of him. It would be a good thing if he could be put into some home.—Rose Ann Byrne, Hughes's-court, was fined 10s. for drunkenness and dis- orderly conduct in the Court on November 16th. There were three previous convictions. The woman appeared with an eight months' old baby and said she wculd have to take it to prison with her. The magistrates gave her three weeks in which to pay the fine. "AT HOME" AT RUNCORN TOWN HALL. Oo Friday evening at the Town Hall Mr. S. Taylor, chairman of the Urban District Council. held an "at home" to 150 invited guests who were received by Mr. Taylor and his daughter, Mrs. J. M. Frith. The recep- tion rooms had been very prettily decorated, and the proceee.ings were considerably enlivened by the special engagement of Mr. W. P. Bailey's band which rendered the following items :—March, "Le Regiment Favori," Ertl; valse lente, "Pondree," Berger; overture, "La Sirene," Auber ; entracte, "Shepherd Girl's Dream.' Labitski ; selection, "Country Girl," Moneton ; entracte "Cyardas," Michaele ; ballet music from " Faust,' Gonnod ; fantasia. "Reminiscences of the Plantation" entracte, "Saint d'Amour," Elgar ; selection, " Torea- dor," Moneton ; fantasia, "Down South," Middleton, and march, "Hands across the Sea," Sousa. A special feature of the reception were the musical sketches, etc., of Mr. Leslie Harris whose ability as a drawing-room entertainer was never seen to better advantage. At nine o'clock the company retired for supper which was served up in excellent style by Messrs. Galt and Capper. the following being the menu : Grosses Pieces: Boar's head, game pie, turkey a la royal, roast chichen aux cressons, capons a la bechamel, ox tongues, beef a la chasseur, roast pheasants,Cumber- land ham, lobster salad, trifle, Charlotte russe, creamss. la celestine, jellies ala reine, pastries a la Franeq in</p>
        <p>Pick your Porringer! They are of several sizes, and their cost is just— NOTIIING</p>
        <p>It works ouL like this : On every packet of 'Provost' Oavi</p>
        <p>there's a coupon, and if you saw. :hese coupons and send them to • . the proprietors they will send you, carriage paid, a 2-pint Porringer for 30 coupons, 3-pint Porringer for 50 coupons, 5-pint Porringer for 75 coupons, 6-pint Porringer for 100 coupons. Or, if you don't want to wait until you have accumulated the coupons, you -can get the Por- ringers in advance by paying 25., 2S. 6d., 3s. or 3s. 6d., according to size, and when you send the coupons the cash you have paid will be refunded.</p>
