      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK,</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK,</p>
        <p>A FAMOUS RUSSIAN SPOF:TSIVIAM</p>
        <p>All British sportsmen share in the regret at the death of Prince Soltykoff, the Russia a who has for so many years been a patron of the. British turf. He was born in Russia on December 23, 1828, and was educated at St. Petersburg University. He served in the Russian Diplo- matic Corps, and subsequently en, tered the army as a private. H e passed through the Crimean war, during which he ,obtained promo- tion, becoming A.D.C. to Genera) Paskievitch. "Upon the con- clusion of the war Prince Solty- koff came to England on leave for three months. He never re- turned to Russia. KOFF. In 1867 His High-</p>
        <p>4 ~------:. I 4/4 ..lit.k \- • )1r -41 . ‘‘... li - \ 'll ‘. 1 tip, • , , ' q, .I, -----,, \\ - ...• 6,‘, ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>LATE PRINCE SOLTY-</head>
        <p>THE LATE PRINCE SOLTY-</p>
        <p>ness was elected a member of the Jockey Club, andin 1889 became asteward. For the past 30 years the deceased has had a palatial residence at Newmarket, known as the Kremlin. The Prince, it is worthy 'of note, was the first Russian ever elected a member of the Jockey Club. Nobody worked harder than he to purify the atmosphere of the turf. He was immensely popular among all. classes of racing men, and he was a close friend of King Edward VII. One of the first horses to carry the pink jacket and black cap forward was a mare named Douche, who carried off the Queen's Vase at Plymouth, as long ago as 1864, and among the most prominent suc- cesses in the Prince's racing career were the victories of Duke of Parma in the Cesarewitch in 1867, Thurio in the Grand Prix de Paris in 1870, Lucetta in the Cambridgeshire in 1880, Gold in the Ascot Gold Cup in 1890, Sheen (carrying the record weight) in the Cesarewitch in the same year, and Lord George in the Lincolnshire Handicap, in 18'91.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>TWO CYCLE AND MOTOR SHOWS.</head>
        <p>THE TWO CYCLE AND MOTOR SHOWS.</p>
        <p>The two great cycle and motor shows oi the Year are now open., the Stanley at. the Agricultural Hall, and; the National at the Crystal Palace. • The Stanley of course iF the bigger of the two, but a, wider selection of motor exhibits is to be found at the National, for the reason that the terms under which the Agricultural Hall is leased to the Stanley Club preclude the display of more than twenty-five complete cars. This year's Stanley is the twenty-seventh of the series. There are several, notable absentees among</p>
        <p>the exhibitors, but certain leading ilrms which have previously exhibited at the National have now gone_ovcT to the Stanley. It is next to impossible, however, to point to any striking novelties where ordinary cycles are concerned. The element of newness is confined almost entirely to. whatever conaei under the head of autobilism in one form or another. The perfect mob= bicycle deserves mention as having 'four cylinders, an arrangement which should give great smooth- ness of running as regards the motor itself, but will be more liable to interuption on account of the increased tendency to break of the sparking plugs, particularly certain varie- ties of this year's patteras. Among other interesting exhibits is a Humber motor cycle engine which is shown in section, and worked by a pulley to show the operation of the piston, valves, and commutator in a way that will appeal to the tyro in motor matters.</p>
