      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BULGARIA AT THE COURT OF ST. JAMES'S.</head>
        <p>BULGARIA AT THE COURT OF ST. JAMES'S.</p>
        <p>For the first time in its history, Bulgaria is now represented at t 1 Court cf St. James's by an accredited representative. is, how- ever, not an Ambassador or even a Minister, but, rather, a diplomatiz agent: His name is Count Dimitri Tzokm ;. and, though still a</p>
        <p>young man of under forty, he has had very wide experience and hasa big record behind him. Since his birth in 1864, his training has been thoroughly cosmo politan,' and no one could be better fitted for the arts hind wiles of diplomacy. His first school was Robert Col- lege at Constan- tino pl e, and. then Count Di- mitri Tzokow went to study law at the Uni-</p>
        <p>versity of Paris and at the Eeole des Sciences Politiques in the French capital. At the age of twenty- three, when Prince Ferdinand was proclaimed Prince of Bulgaria in 1887,, he began to serve his country and entered the public service of Bulgaria. Count Tzokow has been first sec- retary and, at times, charge d'affaires at the Bulgarian Legations in Belgrade, Bucharest, Constantinople, and St. Petersburg, so he is an authority upon the Eastarn question. In 1900, he was appointed charge d'affaires at Athens, but a year later he was recalled and despatched to Paris with M. Sarafow, the Btilgarian Minister of the• Interior, in order to negotiate a loan of five million pounds with a private financial syndicate. He was reward- ed with promotion, and only last year he was appointed to the Bulgarian Foreign Office as Under-Secretary of ,State for the department. Now lie has been sent to England, and will be very well received at the Court of St. James's.</p>
