        <p>COUNT DIMIT.RI TZOKOW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CITY'S HOMAGE.</head>
        <p>THE CITY'S HOMAGE.</p>
        <p>The visit of the King and Queen of Italy was in every way a complete. success, and it is only to be hoped that they took away such golden opinions of us as they left behind of themselves. Not that much was seen of</p>
        <p>ADbREsS.</p>
        <p>them outside the sacred precincts of the Court, as all their time , was spent at Windsor, except the one day spent in town, last Thugs:lay. T Nn was very gaily deGOrate4</p>
        <p>in their honour, and the streets were crowded to see the King and Queen go by. First their Majesties drove to the Italian Embassy in Grosvenor Square and received the leading Italians in the metropolis, and then they proceeded to the Guildhall. There it was</p>
        <p>that the public had the best opportunity of forming their own impression of our royal Visitors. Before the dejeuner an address of welcome was presented to King Victor in a most gorgeous casket. After lunch came the toasts, and th 3 King made a very neat and cordial speech of thanks, which created a most favourable impression. King Victor is small, but natty and spruce and gives one is very wide-awake; while</p>
        <p>4e idca that he</p>
        <p>- his Queen, Shdugh stately and dignified and suggestive of considerable character, is very gracious and always. rippling over into a smile upon the least excuse. The greeting given to them was equally cordial upon their return journey and obviously delighted them. As arranged, they left Windsor on Saturday morning, returning direct to Portsmouth, which they left in the Victoria and Albert to the accompaniment of a royal salute. Whether or not the visit has any definite diplomatic result, it cannot fail to bind the two countries morn rlosely together.</p>
        <p>3111* 3:1 •;;;;.4. U:37 sX.r r 'O% b• • THE GOLD CASKET WIC CONTAINED T H.FL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PECKHAM'S PRIDE.</head>
        <p>PECKHAM'S PRIDE.</p>
        <p>One great topic of conversation at the moment is the phenom.enally fat boy at Peck- ham. His name is John Trundley, and he puts the fat boy in " Pickwick " entirely in the shade. He even threatens to rival the celebrated Daniel Lambert before he has fini.hed. He is just five years</p>
        <p>of age and weighs ten stone four pounds. He appears very short, though abnormally tall for his age,as his chest measure- ment of forty- four inches dwarfs his mere four feet 01 height. Round the waist —or where the waist ought to be— he is the same as round the chest. His thigh is 26 in his calf 6} in., his upper arm 15 in., his neck in., and his head 22,- in. 'With all these extraordinary measurements, Master John Trundiey is • extremely well and active, and mentally he is a biii.tht intelligent child of five, above the average in ability. His phy- sical strength is abnormal, as already he can lift an adult or a half hundredweight; while,. on the other hand, it requires two or three adultS to carry him. If he is naughty, it is found best to leave him alone till he is good again, as the usual parental reproof, adminis- tered across the knee in the time-honoured fashion, is in his case out of the question. He is too big for school, and a special exemp- tion has been made in his case. He is very pleased to be fat, and not a little proud of it. Altogether, Master John Trandley is a very happy and contented child.</p>
