      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MR. CEAMEERLAIN'S ECONOMICAL BXPERT.</head>
        <p>MR. CEAMEERLAIN'S ECONOMICAL BXPERT.</p>
        <p>Professor A. S. Hewins, who is resign- ing his post as the Principal of the London School of Economics to take up work con, aiected with Mr. Chamberlain's fiscal proposals, has been known for some time past as one of the chief economic authorities who favoured Mr. Chamber lain's view. What is the nature of the work which Professor Hew- ins is about tc undertake is at present a mat ter that must be regarded af. confidential, un- less Mr. Cham- berlain should himself see fit to disclose it ; but it is nc secret that Pro- fessor HewinP has for some time past writ- ten a great deal. both anony-</p>
        <p>mously and over his signature' at support of Mr'. Chamberlain's proposals' rhe details of Professor Hewin's public careel are tolerably well known. Educated at Wolver- hampton and Pembroke College, Oxford, Ile began his public career as an Economist 10 888 • as a University Extension Lecturer Seven years afterwards he was invited tc organize the proposed 'London School ol Economics. This school has now become part of London University; and its sphere usefulness has been very widely extended since its foundation. He is thirty-eight yea:ll of 'age, is a member of the London Universit, Senate, a Professor at King's College, and Fellow of various scientific and learned so' cietics. .</p>
        <p>• / , ;r _ / PROFRSSOR W. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PRINCE OF SWINDLERS.</head>
        <p>A PRINCE OF SWINDLERS.</p>
        <p>It will be a long time before Harry Woods who describes himself as an engineer, 26 yeaft of age, enjoys freedom. On Saturday last lig was sentenced at the Old Bailey to sever years' penal servitude, "and," added counSel- " there are other and more serious matters t 4 be laid against him." Woods has, in fact, for some time past been carrying, on an extraor- dinary career of fraud, going about from place to place posing as a very wealthy and titled army offi- cer, and largely supporting him- self by forgeries. One of the cheques he forged was for ‘19,400. De- tective-sercre'eant Palfrey, of New Scotland Yard, said that Woods had been living ter by frauds of the most impudent chalaccia. since he was 14 years of age. At 21 behalf frauded an old couple of Zroo by telling %old a fictitious story of money coming to srPari. He had succeeded in duping persons in try : ons parts of the metropolis and the cotlliotiler Clacton, Portsmouth. Plymouth, and ;,uten- places. At Southend he posed as "1-'-itriot ant Edwards, son ot Earl Leitrim, of Castle, Ireland," and in that name rnarrl: ri.. respectable young lady. When she ta%e' Fat. soner with regard to his real means he trirseaid cued her with a revolver. Prisoner, Sergeant Palfrey, had been discharged 111115 the Marines after 51 day's service in Or of medically unfit, and at the latter ell btit 1901 he joined the Imperial YeoinanrYi the only active service that he saw N.vas _O6l a military train awl in the hospl ital, for this he was enti:led to a meda _rire'rt three clasps—C:lp • Colony, Orange years o f rP f aaysci and the Transvaal. For said the officer, in addition to acts stenl prisoner had been carrying on a -.3trions by blackmailing gentlemen in high Pt)!1 .threatening to accuse them of a cronv be had long Practise</p>
