      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>COLBORNE PERSONATION CASE.</head>
        <p>THE COLBORNE PERSONATION CASE.</p>
        <p>Remarkable Developments.</p>
        <p>There is a remarkable development in the case of the man who, giving the name of Dr. Part, stands com- mitted to the Liverpool Assizes under the remarkable circumstances recently reported.—Two charges brought against him by the police failed, including one of bigamy, but if information which has just come to light be true the police may, after all, prove in a position to proceed with that part of the case.— A woman named Margaret Ellen Hankey has come forward to swear that Part married her at the Parish Chyrch, Dalton-in-Furness, on the sth October, 1898, under the name of John Cubbin. Mrs. Cubbin is now residing with her father, and has recognized the por- traits of the sham Dr. Part as being those of her hus- band. She states that after the marriage he practiced as a doctor at the Sheffield Medical Institute, and in June 1899, went on a voyage to the West Coast of Africa as a ship's surgeon. He returned in October of the same year, and shortly afterwards went to Preesall, near Poulton-le-Fylde. Sub- sequently he disappeared, and was lost to his wife until the attention;of,her friends was aroused by the recent dis- closures. Mrs. Cabbin says her husband used to corres- pond with a gentleman named Dr. Herbert Part. The woman's declaration was mentioned in the Manchester Police Court on Monday and Superintendent Mackenzie obtained from the Stipendiary an authorisation to forward to the Home Office with a view to proceedings being taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PILES.</head>
        <p>PILES.</p>
        <p>Piles aye the outcome of venous blood settling in the veins of the rectum. Imperfect blood circulation, brought on by an inactive liver, weak heart or stomach troubles,will often cause this. Sometimes large masses of fceces lodge in the bowels. These press upon the veins, retard the circulation,and thus cause Piles. Bleeding Piles are the worst. If left alone they often result in Cancer of the rectum. Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills end Piles. Mrs. M. Harrison, 201, Aston-lane, Birmingham, has suffered from an aggravated form of Piles for years. Constipation added to her troubles. "Day after day," she says, 4‘ my burdens increased. I constantly had to take an injection beforel could find relief. But Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills have changed all this. Now lam free from pain. The troubles that have so long tormented me have left. They have cured me and in them I have the greatest faith." Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills are a perfect blood purifier, and cure liver and kidney co mplaints,b iliousness, indigestion,constipa tion,piles, dyspepsia, female ailments, eczema, pimples, and blotches. Sold by leading chemists and stores, price Is. 11d. per bottle, or on receipt of price from The W. H. Comstock Co., Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C. FREE SAMPLE. Mention this paper and enclose id. stamp for postage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FUNERAL OF THE LATE COUNCILLOR</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE COUNCILLOR</p>
        <p>BROUGH.</p>
        <p>The 0-rave closed over the remains of the late Councillor Btophy on Monday afternoon, when the interment took place in the Cemetery. The members of the. Town Council met at the Town Hall, and joined the funeral procession at the deceased's late house, the Farmer's Arms, in Dallam-lane: A. posse of police under the superintendence of Chief Inspector Phillips, and the members of the Fire Brigade with Deputy- Superintendent Turner at their head walked in front of the hearse. The deceased, prior to becoming a licensed victualler, was an engine-driver, and secre- tary of the local branch of the A.S.R.S., and he was borne to his grave by four railway men and two of the oldest customers of the house. -The mourners and friends present were : Mrs. Brophy and family (two sons and six daughters)' Mrs. Murdock, of Garston (sister of the deceased), Mr. Lamb and Mr. Healey, of Garston, Mr. Waldron (Grappenhall), Mr. Eddowes (Southport), Mr. Marshall (foreman of the locomotive department at Warrington), Mr. Campbell(Bolton), Mr. George Wright,Mr.Joseph Potter,Afr. David Houghton, Mr. Owen Toole, Mr. Charles Brookes and Mr. George Brookes. The members of the Town Council present were: Aldermen Smethurst, Bennett, Councillors Burton, Barton, Magian, Duncan, Brough, James Evans, U.pson, Wilkinson, Peacock, Rogers, Ashcroft, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr. F. Taylor (gas secretary), Mr. W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), and Mr. F. Timperley (assistant collector.) The Beer and Wine Trade Association was represented by Mr. John Wrench (president), Mr. Kenyon and Mr. Barlow, (vice-presidents). Mr. W. J. Shaw (secretary), Mr. A. Whittaker (treasurer), and Messrs. McClinton, Lake, Cook, Lonr,rimore, Bonnell, Ritchies, Corker, Roberts. Jones, Roughley, Hackney and Walker. Mr. T. Walsh, and Mr. S. Wakefield represented the licensed victuallers. A deputation, numbering 25 railway servants, employed by the London and North Western Railway Company, attended. The Amalga- mated Society of railway servants was represented by Mr. E. Malone, and the Associated Society of Engine- men and firemen by Mr. J. E. Plinston. The Irish National Foresters was represented by Mr. Charles. Roddy, and Mr. Thomas Hughes.— The funeral service was conducted by the Rev. Father Whittle assisted by th e Rev. Father S warbrick. A large number of floral tributes were forwarded. A large artificial one, with a glass shade, bore the inscription "A token of respect and esteem from his fellow members 'of the A.S.R.S. Warrington Branch." Amongst the organisations who sent floral tributes were the mem- bers of the Warrington Liberal Club, the Irish National Forresters, and Messrs Eddowes and Com- Pany with which the deceased was connected.</p>
        <p>lI.H.S.,SZIL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>COLONIAL DOOR CLOSED.</head>
        <p>THE COLONIAL DOOR CLOSED.</p>
        <p>Giving and • Receiving. MR. CHAMBERLAIN evidently believes "it is more blessed to give than to receive." He is greatly troubled because foreign countries appear to be sending us more than we appear to be sending them. His aim seems to be to try and stop this, not only in appearance but in reality. His ambition is for us to send away more than we receive. By some extra- ordinary line of reasoning he seems to think that the man, or nation, who sends away more than he receives grows wealthy by the process, while the man who receives more than he sends back is made poor. It is strange that any sane man should be deluded by such an argu- ment. What is the difference between a rich man and a poor man? , 1. A rich man receives every year more than he has to pay out again. 2. A poor man receives little, and, in order to live, has to pay back every year all, or nearly all, he receives. 3. A successful man of business puts by every year the surplus of goods he receives. In time he retires and lives upon the surplus he has saved. 4. The unsuccessful man of business gives back all, or more, than he receives, and in time becomes a bankrupt. What applies to men applies to nations.</p>
        <p>Food Taxes. THE basis of Mr. Chamberlain's scheme of Preference is a tax upon our food and goods. He says he is going to tax:</p>
        <p>Corn. Meat. Dairy produce. Manufactures. Our food, our clothes' cur houses our goods everything we buy is to be made dearer. The extra money which we shall have to pay for all these things is to be divided between a few Colonists, British landowners, and ruanufac- tur3rs. It is important to note that the food- producers in four Colonies only, out of our numerous cossessions, are to benefit by Mr. Chamberlain's scheme. India, Ceylon, Straits Settlements; and other possessions, with a population of 345,000,000 British subjects, are to be treated like foreigners! Mr. Cham- berlain's scheme is to benefit the -food pro- ducers of Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa only. The total population of those Colonies is only 16,104,000, and a very small proportion of these will benefit by the scheme. On the other hand, 42,300,000 British subjects of the United Kingdom are to pay more for all they buy.</p>
        <p>Free Trade and Protection Prices. UNDER Mr. Chamberlain's scheme the prices of our goods are to be increased by 2s. in the i. The prices of our food are to go up in a similar manner. What dear food means to our poor, thou Sands of old folks %still living can remember. When we last had Protection in England, Protection against cheap food, the prices went up to an alarming extent. Here is a grocer's bill showing the difference in prices between 1820 and 1903. It was made out by Mr. L. H. Longman of Bruton, who took the figures from his own ledger: PRICES UNDER PROTECTION (1820) AND FREE TRADE (1903). 1820. 1903. s. d. s. d. 21b. Lump Sugar ... 2 0 5 31b. Moist „ ... 2 3 6 _</p>
        <p>lb. Tea ... ... 4 0 10 11b. Yellow Soap ... 10 3 11b. Currants ... 1 1 4 11b. Raisins ... ... 10 5 141 b. Salt ... ... 4 9 4 31b. Candles ... ... 2 3 1 Coffee... 1 8 9 11b. Starch , 11. 4 *lb. Pepper ... 1 0 5</p>
        <p>£1 7 0 5 8i None of us want to go back to the high prices of the 01d Protection days. Mr. Chamberlain will probably tell us that his Preference taxes will not be so high. But that is always the excuse Protectionists make when they want to introduce these taxes. The experience of France, Germany, and other countries, who have adopted Protection, is that they had a small tax to commence with, and as this did not answer the taxes were increased. France commenced, in 1880, with a tax of is. on wheat; it is now 12s. Germany commenced, in 1879, with a duty of 2s. ; it is now 7s. 6d., and it will soon be 12s. Last May Mr. Chamberlain would have been content with a Is. duty on corn; he now asks for 2s. If the Colonies demanded more, would Mr. Cham- berlain say them "nay " Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman quoted an American saying the other day : "You can't stop half- way down a waterfall." If we once give way to Protection we shall not be able to stop until we have brought back the bad old days of 1820.</p>
        <p>The Colonies and British Trade. MR. CHAMBERLAIN has made various vague allusions to the "requests," "promises," and "offers " which have passed between the Colonies and himself. These allusions are delusions. The Colonies do not mean to let in British goods free, even if we were to tax our food to give them more trade. The following interesting extract from the Westminster Gazette is conclusive proof: Mr. H. Gee writes to draw our attention to the following discussion in the Australian Common- w?alth House of Representatives on October 9th last: Mr. McDonald (Q), referring to Mr. Chamber- lain's speech, inquired if any offer, and if so what offer, had been made to the Home Govern- ment. Mr. Deakin (Federal Premier) thought Mr. Chamberlain had referred to Canada and, South Africa. If the allusion was meant to refer to anything else, it must have referred to the resolutions passed at the Premiers' Conference in London in 1900 in favour of Preferential trade. r Mr. Reid: That conference did not represent the views of Australia, but of seven gentlemen. Mr. King O'Malley (Tasmania) : Is there any danger of the tariff being reduced in favour of the home country? Mr. Deakin: If there is any such danger it will come from my right hon. friend the Leader of the Opposition.</p>
        <p>But, it may be urged, has not Canada at least given British goods a preference' and will they not give us still further advantages ? Well, this is what Mr. Chamberlain told the Colonial Premiers about the Canadian pre- ference:</p>
        <p>In spite of the preference which Canada has given us, their tariff has pressed, and still presses, with the greatest severity upon its beat customer, and has favoured the foreigner, who is constantly doing his best to shut out her goods (Colonial Conference, 1902). -</p>
      </div>
