      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 28, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ODDS AND ENDS. 3</head>
        <p>ODDS AND ENDS. 3</p>
        <p>acc soma trunfzs money won-t ouv— morels and manners, for instance.</p>
        <p>No philosophy will keep a man from falling in love; but love will interfere with a good many kinds of philosophy.</p>
        <p>A NATURAL MAN.—" Then he did not fall on his knees when he proposed ?" "No ; he stood on one foot."'</p>
        <p>STRANGER : "Sandy, are you cold with the kilt?" Sandy: "Na, mon; but I'm nigh kilt wi the cauld."</p>
        <p>JUDGE:• "Haven't 1 seen you before some- where ?" Prisoner: "I shouldn't wonder. I have been in some queer places in my time." WHY can a man's nose never be longer than 11 inches.—Because if it were 12 it would be a foot.</p>
        <p>THE compositor who made the line " Sveet are the uses of adversity" read "Sweet are the uses of advertising" certainly knew what he was about.</p>
        <p>A.B.—" Do you suppose that kings and queens are like ordinary mortals?" B—" Certainly. 1 have no doubt that a queen asks liar king if her hat is on straight." YOUNG DOCTOR—" I'm doing very well. I was c‘lled up three nights last week." Old Doctor— " That's good! I hope you never forget to appear annoyed on such occasions?" BEASSURING.—Patient : " Look here, doctor, do you think you will ever be able to tell exactly what is the matter with me?" Doctor: "Oh, yes; Iwill find that out at the autopsy." BOBBY—" Dad, what is a mutual friend ? " Mr. Fog—" He is generally one who makes it his busi- ness to see that you don't miss hearing the nasty things your friends say about you." MRS. GIBBS "Your wife is such a sympathetic woman, Mr. Tibbs! It takes very little to move her." Mr. Tibbs: "You're right, madam. Eleven times in four years, and she's looking for a new house now."</p>
        <p>SHEi: "I could have married either Barr or Scott f I'd wanted to, and both of them have grown quite rich, while you're as poor as a church mouse." He: "Of course. I've had to keep you all these years. They haven't." "SAY, Maud," said Minnie, "did you see Mrs. Jinkle's new vase?" "Yes, isn't it perfectly horrid !" "1 dont' know yet. I haven't found out whether it is modern and perfectly horrid, or antique and perfectly lovely." PRACTICAL PHILOSOPHY—" Ah, well, Mrs. Jen- kins, them as lives longest sees most; but as I often says to my old man, says I, a kind word's an easy obligation, and goes a good deal further than a hobnailed boat or a quart pot, says I." EXCITED INDIVIDUAL "Guard, I've been robbed. I left a paper parcel containing five poems on that seat, and now it's gone. Guard— " Well, you can write 'em over again, can't you ?" Excited Individual—" Yes; but there was a sand- wich in the parcel, too. THE postman left a letter at the house of Mrs. Binks-Jones, whose son is at college. Mr. B. J. : "Here is a letter from Charles." Mrs. B. J. : "Read it." Mr. B. J. (reading) : "My dearest darlingest mother " Mr. 8.-J. : "Good gracious! The young scoundrel needs more money again." FIRST NEWSBOY: "There goes a gent. Chase him." Second Newsboy: "No use. Just saw him come out of a barber's shop. He'd heard all the news there."</p>
        <p>LITTLE GIRL—" I'm afraid to go to sleep in the dark." Mamma—" Nonsense, dear. Remember that little angels are with you." Little Girl (10 minutes later)—" I can't go to sleep, mamma ; one of the little angels is biting me." : "Darling, will you miss me when lam far away in a foreign land ; " She: "Yes, dear • but you will write to me often, won't you? *hat a chance it will be for me to increase my collection of foreign stamps !" "You see, my dear," said Mr. Younghusband to his wife, triumphantly, at 3 a.m. the other morning, "the moment I begin to sing to baby she is quite quiet." "Yes," said his wife; "she is easily frightened, poor little thing."</p>
        <p>AN American girl once got Paderewski to give her three of his autographs. "One," she ex- plained, "to frame and hang up in my bedroom, one to carry with me always, and one to paste inside our piano to irnprove the tone." PROMOTING _PEACE.—" I have here" said the caller, "a poem advocating peace." "I suppose," asked the editor," that you honestly and sincerely desire peace?" "Yes, sir." "Then barn the poem." BatnEsarArn : "You poor, frightened darling! You looked scared to death at the altar." Bride: "Yes. George trembled so I was dreadfully afraid he'd loose courage and run away."</p>
        <p>"I TOLD you you couldn't keep a secret, Ethel." "Well, I can." "You've told everybody what I confided to you the other day." "I know that: but I've got the secret just the same. They haven't taken it away from me." THE DEPTH OF HIS MEANNESS.—He Ind ex- pended every epithet abusing the parsimony of his enemy, and he had at last to pause for lack of breath and say, "You—you—you are mean enough to steal the acorns from a blind pig." THEY had had a little party of guests at the house, and he remarked to his wife, as he was getting ready for bed, that he flattered himself he had acted the part of host in rather a bri:liant manner. "I can only recall one brilliant action of yours," she said. "What was that?" "Lighting the gas." _ _</p>
        <p>NOT EASILY FRIGHTNNED.—One must admire the firmness of an absentee Irish landlord. "Stop where you are," he wrote to his agent, "and if my tenants think they will alarm me by shooting you they little know the man they have to deal with."</p>
        <p>DANGEROUS SPORT.—An EnoliAman shooting small game in Germany remarked to his host that there was a spice of danger in shooting in America. " Ah," said the host, " you like danger mit your sport? Den you go out shooting mit me. De last time I shoot mine bruder-in-law in de schtomaek."</p>
        <p>SHE was standing alcne on the beach, at an American watering-place, gazing peabively on the ocean. A youth approached, then paused. "No," he murmured, "I shall not disturb the current of her thoughts. She is communing with the spirit of this beautiful world." Then she saw aim, and, turning, said: "I say, mister, how fur does this here millpond go, an' whar'bouts does it stop at?" A FEW years ago a London member sat down, after his maiden speech, on a new silk hat which he had provided in honour of the auspicious oc- casion, and as he was ruefully surveying his bat- tered headgear, to the amusement of the unfeeling spectators, an Irish representative rose and gravely said, "Mr. Speaker, permit me to con- gratulate the honourable member on the happy circumstance that when he sat on his hat his head was not in it." The call of "Order, order l" from the Speaker was drowned in mats of laughter. A WONDERFUL SAUSAGE MACHINE. Some Americans were boasting about their inventions and the wonderful machines to be found in the States. One of them told of the well-known minc- ing machine which, a live pig being introduced at one end, turns out the animal'in sausages at the other. An Irishman, who was not going to have the Yankees riding roughbhod over every other nation, turned on them and said :—" Bedad, we've got the same machine in Ireland, only ours is more perfect, sure ; for it you don't like the sausages you can put them back into the machine, and, by reversing the action, they'll come out a live pig agin where he went in." AT a big junction. Ticket Collector (examining passenger's ticket) : "This won't do, sir; you'll have to get out and—" Passenger : "I shall not get out. I refuse to be bullied by you; here is my card ; you have your remedy" (etc., etc.) Ticket Collector: "I only wanted to say, sir —" Passenger: "Don't talk to me; you're only a menial; fetch the station-master." Ticket Col- lector : " It's all right, sir." (Train proceeds, and passenger relates tales of his various triumphs over railway officials.) Scene lI.—A dark and lonely railway platform. One melancholy figure, brooding. Passenger (thoughtfully) : "I wonder if that collector only meant to tell me I was in the wrong train.</p>
