      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must mall cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not latep than Thursday.</p>
        <p>[SPECTATOR.—The main point regarding Councillor Dodds raised in your letter has been so often dealt with that we did not deem it worthy of re-publication. You will find a reference to it in our editorial columns.—ED. "Ex."]</p>
        <p>DiR. H. P. HUGHES.—The status of the Cobden Club and its Secretary has already been sufficiently well defined in our columns and elsewhere,—ED. "Ex."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Mr. Justice Grantham and the Publicans.</head>
        <p>Mr. Justice Grantham and the Publicans.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the conclusion of the Durham Assizes, Mr. 'justice Grantham made some remarks, which will probably cause the liquor men, to call down "tire and brimstone" on his head. The "Daily Mirror" of to- day reports him to have said that "it was time some- thing was done to stop the sale of drink, to persons who had had ehough, and time some of those publicans who sold it were strung up by the neck." No doubt the learned judge spoke under strong feel- ing. I have no ground for supposing that his Lordship is a teetotaler, or that he has any pronounced opinions on6he general questions connected with the social evils produced by the liquor trade. However, his.utterance is strong enough, and coming from a judge will have the desired effect in strengthening the present attitude of the licensing justices,—especially at the next Brewster Sessions. What will Mr. Balfour say when he hears of Mr. Justice Grantham's remark? But of course he won't see it, since he never reads the papers, &amp;c. May I respectfully commend the altogether too- searching remarks of his Lordship to the magistrates who dismissed the Roebuck Inn case with costs only.— I am Scc.</p>
        <p>JOHN VRXNCIS MATTHEWS. Latchford, Nor. '26th, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Warrington and Labour Representation.</head>
        <p>Warrington and Labour Representation.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Will you !:indly grant me a small open space in your paper, to reply to a certain article that appeared in your last week's issue written by Mr. Osborne.</p>
        <p>The portion of Mr. Osborne's article that deals witla Labour representation is what I would specially re- fer to. The writer seems rather annoyed that we, the Labour Party should have had such presumption, as to think of nominating a Labour Candidate and to do this without consulting the political organisation with which he is connected. Why i we should consult this special organisation, any more than any other, I fail to see. Perhaps Mr. Osborne, thinks that his party have some claim or right to be consulted 7on this matter. Evidently • the Labour Party does not think so. The Labour Party (and when I say the Labour Party I mean the majority of Trade Unionists in Warrington,and not a few leaders whom Mr. Osborne terms his socialist friends) think it is quite time that they should have arepresentative of their 'own, and be allowed to choose their own. in the past they have usually left this sort of business to a few Seeing that the working class are about 7-10ths of the electorate and a large proportion are Trade Unionists, they 'tare justified in thinking they have a right to choose their own candidate, and in choosing their candidate they quite see the absolute necessity that he should not be attached to any other political organiza- tion, for this reason, if for no other, that the Trade Unionists in the past have been divided into two political parties. They have seen the folly of this, and will be as firm and united now as they would be in the case of a trade dispute. If Labour was to attach itself to one or other of the political organisations, a section would go back to therr old party and Labour would be again disunited. Mr. Osborne appears to forget that the workingmen do not all belong to oneparty. We are aware of this and recognise our movement as being the centre point, to which all workingmen may flock, whether they be Liberals, Tories, or Socialists,to work under one banner that ofilLabour, without any "isms"attached. If Mr. Osboine will only look at this matter in a broad ligheand not through party spectacles he will also see the absolute necessity of independence, and will not try to further the claims of a certain individual of his own party to be a representative of Labour.</p>
        <p>If Mr. Osborne really desires to be the friend of Labour, then let him and his party stand on one side and give Labour a straight and fair fight. if there is a three-cornered contest at the next election,it will show at once that the Liberal Party is as much opposed to Labour Representation as are the other Party. In conclusion, I might tell Mr. Osborne that the Con- ference that unanimously decided to invite Mr. Wilkie was composed of nearly 100 delegates representing over 2,000 Trade Unionists. If this is not a strong enough opinion, perhaps he will tell us what is.—Thanking you, sir, in anticipation of your favour, I remain yours respectfully</p>
        <p>0. BUCKLEY. Secretary Labour Representation Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Mr. Crosfielti in Lighter Vein.</head>
        <p>Mr. Crosfielti in Lighter Vein.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln Mr. Frederick Harrison's fascinating volume entitled The Choice of Books" he quotes a delightful tale of Voltaire's, describing how the King of Babylon cured of 'excessive self-esteem a great satrap called Irax. The moment the satrap awoke in the morning the master of the royal music entered the favourite's chamber with a full chorus and orchestra, and per- formed in his honour a cantata which lasted two hours; and every third minute there came a refrain to this effect : " Que son merit est. extreme ! Que de graces ! qua de grandeur! An ! combien Monseigneur Doit etre de lui-meme ! The cantata over, a royal chamberlain advanced and pro- nounced a harangue that lasted three-quarters of an hour in which he extolled him for possessing all the good qualities which he had not got. At dinner, which lasted three hours, the same ceremonial was continued. If he opened hts mouth to speak, the first Chamberlain said, " Hark, we shall hear wisdom!" And before be had uttered; four words, the second Chamberlain said, "What wisdom do wehear ! " Then the third and the fourth Chamberlains broke into shouts of laughter over the good things which Trax had said, or rather ought to have said ; and after dinner the same cantata was again sung in his honour. On the first day Irax was delighted; the second he found less pleasant ; on the third he was bored; on the fourth he said he could bear it no longer; and on the fifth he was cured. - Now the "sovereign people " of 'Warrington, if they may be so described, have also their satrap, who like this man of old time is in need of a cure. Moreover, as there is no deficiency of musical talent in Warring- ton I have been wondering whether, with so worthy art object in view, the services of one of our local brass bands might be 'available to serenade ouraugust patient morning and evening. And though we have no royal chamberlains at 'disposal,l cannot doubt that one or two of our local orators might be willing to extol his great merit and virtue in contrast-to this 7 sad short-comings and delinquencies of the rest of his fellow townsmen in general and the Liberal leaders in particular. If our musicians and orators are willing to undertake this philanthropic mission, T. would only suggest to them that the patient will need to be handled with care and forbearance. True he is a man of peace who would quarrel with no one ; yet in his pacific character is capable of making statements and insinuations as unjust as they are inaccur- ate, without the common courtesy to withdraw either when proved to be so. Indeed in his capacity as peace- , maker he has poured forth at times what sounds to some ' of us very like vitriolic abuse on other people. To give one other illustration of the consistency of this remarkable man, he has signalised his devotion to the Liberal cause by offering to support as candidate for Warrington, the gentleman who is now opposine one of the ablest and finest Liberals in the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>...... I shall not again trespass on your space by discussin either the complicated symptoms of our unfortunate patient or by any further argument concerning his case, for which I trust a speedy cure may happily be effected. Yours, &amp;c., A. H. CROSFiELD. Hoylake, November 26th, 1903.</p>
        <p>{*The following free translation of Mr. Crosfield's quotation from Voltaire may be of assistance to our readers : "How great is his merit ! What gracefulness ! what splendour ! • Oh! what% lord His Lordship must be !" El). " E</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Liberal and Labour. Mr. Wilkie's Candidature.</head>
        <p>Liberal and Labour. Mr. Wilkie's Candidature.</p>
        <p>Sir.—lf Mr. Osborne will.write to the Press offering criticism, be must expect to be criticised in return. He cannot have all the game to himself and he must not cry when he gets "tackled." He has not replied to my remarks made at our last meeting. My position is this :—Mr. Osborne in his first articla in your paper, gave your readers the im- pression, that it was customary for the Trades Council to hold "revolutionary debates." That a society had wit hdrawn from membership becauss of the same, and he insinuated the Council was not properly conducted. I protested on the following grounds :--That during my continuous membership of the Council for a period of between nine and ten years. I knew of no " revolu- tionary debate" being held. That the Council was con- ducted equally as well as any other organisation in Warrington. That during my time on the Council, no branch of Trade Unionists had withdrawn through "revolutionary debates." That if something did occur ten years ago, it is not applicable for present day criticism. Mr. Osborne refers me to a sentence, in a letter ligritten to the public Press, by a gentleman in answer</p>
        <p>to Mr.Stott. Now I do not think any gentleman's opinion, given in the Press, is sufficient to condemn any organisation. No judge could convict on such evidence. If Mr. Osborne will point out any occasion, during the last ten years, when a "revolutionary debate " has been held in a loosely conducted Trades Council meeting, then there will be a case/to criticise. I repeat again there is no justifiable reason, why all the Trades Union branches should not join the Trades Council. If any branch has a grievance, there is the fullest opportunity for ventilating, and discussing the same. _ _</p>
        <p>As to the rivalry and jealous Mr. Osborne mentions in his article last week, this is only imagination on his part. I know of nothing of the]sort, and as Mr. Osborne is not, I understand, a member of any trade union, he. must have been imposed upon by some mischief maker As to my egotism as he calls it,l shall, while I have the honour and pleasure to hold my office as president, endeavour to withstand and repel any attack made upon us.</p>
        <p>And now a few remarks on the remainder of Mr. Osborne's contribution. I take it that the very repre- sentative conference of Trade Unionists,which invited Mr Wilkie to contest Warrington in the Labour interest at the next Parliamentary election, know their own busi- ness. They do not feel it necessary to acquaint every irresponsible individual with the reasons for their actions. Mr. Osborne may depend upon it, that Mr. Wilkie fully satisfied them, when he met them face to face, on his position,politically socially and financially. And it was a unanimous conference.</p>
        <p>Mr. Osborne's article reads to me as an attempt to prejudice the public of Warrington against Mr. Wilkie, before they have had an opportunity of seeing and hearin,, him. This is not fair play. At least judgment should be withheld until he has had a chance of publicly statinghis, and our reasons, for his candi- dature.</p>
        <p>As to choice of a candidate, who has ft better right,to choose than the organised Trades of Warrington? Why should the choice be restricted to two political parties, whose actual paying members can be easily numbered? The Trade Unionists of Warrington are a very con- siderable section of the male population, and far out- number any other organisation which laims to take political action. H the Trade Unionists nominate, it is for them to bring forward their candidate before their fellow workers and the public, and allow such to judge of his fitness for Parliament.</p>
        <p>In Mr. Wilkie, there is a candidate who can justly claim to be a Labour Statesman. He began his labour career probably before Mr. Osborne was born. He is an ideal candidate for an industrial constituency like Warrington, and his position in Parliament, along with the Trade group—which will be there after the next General election and act directly for Labour interests— will enhance the cause of Labour to a considerable degree. His understanding and experience in Labour affairs will be most useful and advantageous in Parlia- ment, and it only needs unity among the workers of Warrington to ensure his triumphant return. Yours &amp;c. JOSEPH PODMORE Jun., President. Trades and Labour Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>BAZAAR AT HILL CLIFFE.</head>
        <p>BAZAAR AT HILL CLIFFE.</p>
        <p>The Abolition of Pew Rents at the Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The congregation of Hill Cliffe Baptist Church are engaged in a worthy effort by means of a three days' bazaar to wipe off a deficiency in the finances of the church incurred by the abolition of pew rents, and vari- ous calls upon the funds. The project has been entered into with enthusiasm and the strong committee, with the Rev. J. S. Hughes as president, Mr. E. England as hon. treasurer, and Mr. R. Lloyd Jones as hon. secretary have left no stone unturned to ensure the complete success which is so ardently desired. When the bazaar was opened on Thursday afternoon the little schoolroom presented a bright and inviting appearance, and the stalls were laden with a variety of wares. The follow- ing acted as stall-holders:— LADIES'. SEWING CLASS STALL Hughes, Mrs. Hallas, Mrs. Williams, Mrs. Moss. FANCY STALL:—Miss lianas, Miss Moss; Miss Worrall, Mrs. Jos. Moss' jun PLANT AND FRUIT STALL :—Chairman, Mr. John Moss, Messrs. Joseph Moss, sen. John Moores, W. Wilde, E. Wilde, George Moss, William Moss, An.slow, T. F. Moss, Sept. Moss. . REFRESHMENT STALL :—Mesdames Dodd, Jones, Leigh; Misses Hall, Bouchier, England, M. England, and Jones.</p>
        <p>YOUNG PEOPLE'S STALL (ADVERTISEMENT) :—Chair- man and Hon. Treasurer, Mr. T. Deakin ; hon. secre- tary, Miss Dodd; Misses Worrall, Jones, Marion England, Wheeler, Bell, E. Anslow, B. Holland, More- ton, Broadhurst, V. Jones, Wilkinson ; Messrs. H. V. Hughes, H. Moores, P. Beswick, P. Moss, and J. J. Worrall.</p>
        <p>There was the usual " bran pie" in charge of Miss Dodd, and a side attraction was a museum of curios- ities, Messrs. A. Arnold, .11. V. Hughes, B. Moss, W. Gould, and W. Arnold being responsible for the arrange- ments.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony on Thursday was performed by Mrs. Baxter, of Dutton Hall. _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. S. Hughes, pastor, facetiously observed that the reason why they were holding the bazaar was to be found in the question that was agitating the country from end to end—the fiscal question—for they had found that as a church they were likely to suffer to some extent from the new disease of "fiscalitis." In plain words lack of money was responsible for the bazaar. He trusted that that attempt at free trade by fair traders in all kinds of useful and ornamental . articles would land their church in a state of protection and presperity Three years ago they were confronted with a threatened serious decline in their income and a somewhat increased expenditure. Under the circumstances it was then thought that a said of work would have to be under- taken but their friends rallied to their help nobly. In a very short time their weekly offerings doubled and then trebled and by various other means they had been able to keep a fairly even balance until just lately, not- withstanding the fact that the erection of a new organ and the purchase of new books had involved them man extra expense of £6O. This year the spots on the sun had effected them and owing to the unfavourable weather during the summer they had only been able to hold one outdoor service instead of twelve or thirteen. This, of course, meant a decrease in the amount of the offerings, although their regular congregations had not declined. They had also been bold enough to dispense with pew rents and all seats in the Church were now absolutely free. That had been his ideal of a ' place of worship, but the increase in the weekly offerings had not exactly compensated for the loss occasioned by the abolition of pew rents. In order to meet the demands upon them and to relieve then, of the strain during the coming year they hoped to raise from £7O to £BO by that undertaking. Mr. Hughes then announced that donations had been received totalling about £l3. On looking back into the history of the church he found they had not had a sale of work for nearly ten years, but this was the fourth or fifth pro- ject of its kind. Members of the Baxter family had taken some part in each sale and he was very pleased that Mrs. Baxter was with them that day. (Applause.) Mr. Baxter was unable to be present but had forwarded a donation of two guineas. in opening the sale Mrs. Baxter said she could re- mer.-ber attending a sale of work at Hill Cliffe some fifteen years ago. She wished the sale every success. (Anplause.) On the motion of Mr, England, seconded by Mr.• Barker, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs Baxter and the business of the sale commenced. in the evening two enjoyable concerts were given by Miss Bouchier's party and were highly appreciated. The programme included songs by Miss Longdin and Miss Ball, pianofo rte solos by Miss Bateman and Miss Longdin, recitations by Miss Beswick, violin solos by Miss Bouchier, and xylophone solos by Miss Pigot. Other interesting features were selections on Mr. T. gramaphone, ;conjuring by Professor White, and humorous sketches by Mr. Summers. YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The sale was re-opened yesterday afternoon by Mrs. Armitage. • The Rev. J. S. Hughes announced that notwithstand- ing the unfavourable weather they did exceedingly well on Thursday, the proceeds amounting to £57. In wel- coming Mrs. Armitage he referred to the interest which Mr. and Mrs. Armitage took in the church and the zenerous help they rendered. Mr. Hughes also paid a tribute to the excellent work of the lady helpers. Mrs. Armitage said she was glad to be present because Hill Cliffe Chapel was doing such a very good work in the neighbourhood, and it also gave her an opportunity of showing an interest in another denomin- ation. If they could all show more interest in each other's chapels and churches it would be a very good thing. (Hear, hear). The Rev. H. Dunnico proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Armitage. He referred to the principles of Non- conformity and pointed out that the Free Church idea had so far survived that in no English-speaking nation outside our own iwas the idea of a State Church tdlerated. He hoped that as a result of the sale the hands of the Hill Cliffe congrega- tion would be strengthened and that their finances would be placed on such a footing that they would be able to go forward in their work, undeterred by any dread of debt, to carry on tho principles for which they stood.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews seconded and the vote was heartily accorded. • - _</p>
        <p>Last evening another successful concert was given. The Misses Pooley and Warren rendered an instrumental quartette, Mr. Edgar Balmford contributed cello solos, and Mr. Whittaker played concertina solos, all being well received. Miss E. Dunn and Mr. J. Hannan were heard to advantage in songs. The proceeds for the day amounted to £3l 10s., mak- ing a total for the two days of £BB 10s.</p>
        <p>THE CANDAJE AND 'ME GUNPOWDER. While an apprentice named Mac Master was weighin out some gunpowder by the aid of a lighted candle a Boyle on Thursday evening a terrible explosion occurred The apprentice was badly injured about the face, and the house was wrecked, all the windows within a hun- dred yards radius being broken,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>REVIVAL IN KAFFIRS.</head>
        <p>THE REVIVAL IN KAFFIRS.</p>
        <p>Prominent among the low-priced shares, says " Money " in the South African Market, North Shebas are attracting attention owing to the Company's excel- lent prospects. It appears that the developments on the Company's property, which are being vigorously prosecuted, are of a very satisfactory character. On an adjoining property, owned by a well-known Johannes- burg Company, a large battery is being erected, which it is expected will begin to crush in the New Year. This should have a beneficial effect on the price "f North Shebas which are quoted on the Stock Exchange at is. 9d. to 2s, 3d. for the £1 share 19s. paid. They should not remain long at this low level.</p>
        <p>The memory of those gallant men of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment who ,laid down their lives in tieir country's service in the South African campaign will be kept green in the hearts of their surviving comrades and of Warrington people generally. On Saturday afternoon the fay-or (Alderman Bolton), unveiled a fitting memorial, in the form of a screen and tablet which have been ereeted in the Boteler Chapel at the ParishliChurch by the officers and men of the regiment, and add much to the beauty of the venerable building. The screen (late perpendicular in style)' is of English oak, throughout, .and is divided into seven bays. A large central bay, forming the entrance into the Boteler Chapel, is flanked by three small ones on either side, the upper portions of which are.filled- with wrought-iron grilles, and the lower with carved "linen' panels The bays are richly traceried, and have over- hanging canopies springing from small octagonal shafts with caps and bases; surmounting these is a carved cornice divided up by crocketed pinnacles, each bearing a shield with the cyphers "1.H.5." and "St.I.E." alternately. On the east side the shields are carved with the emblems of the Passion. Crowning thecentral canopy is a figure of an angel representing "Victory," carrying in the right hand an uplifted sword, and in the left a wreath of bay ; on either side of this are smaller figures of angels, also carrying wreaths, and below the centre figure is a shield bearing the arms of the 3rd Batt. South Lancashire Regiment. The tablet bears an inscription and the names of the following officers and men who fell :</p>
        <p>Major E. K. Heath. Lieutenants E. M. Hanbury and C. H. Walker. Corporal T. Murphy. Lance-Corporal F. Dodd. . .</p>
        <p>Privates J: Brown, T. Cantello, J. Cassidy, J. Cassons, J. Dolan T. Egan, A. Elliott, D. Flynn, A. Follett, R. Forber, 1. Fowler, E. Harvey, J. Hilton, J. Jones, S. Knowles, R. Lister, M. Mahon, J. MaHinson, J. Matthews, J. McCabe, P. Monoghan, G. Murther, J. Naughton,A. W. Rayson, J. Rigby, A. Schofield, T. Sparkes G. Travis, A. Waterworth and W. Wood. The Bishop of Liverpool had consented to unveil the memorial, but to the regret of all, he was unable to attend through illness. Fortunately the Mayor stepped into the breach and performed the ceremony,which was witnessed by a representative gathering of members of the corporation. The service was particularly impressive and there was a large congregation. A contingent of the 3rd South Lancashire and King's Liverpool Regiments attended, accompanied by the band from Orford Barracks. The soldiers were met at the west door by the wardens, sidesmen, clergy and choir and during the singing of the hymn "Onward, Christian soldiers" to Sullivan's well-known setting walked in procession to the seats allotted to them, the clergy and officers moving on to the Boteler Chapel. The officers of the South Lanc- ashire Regiment present!were:—LientenantColonel M. H. D. 5.0., (commanding officer) Colonel R. Ireland Blackburne(honorary colonel of the battalion,who accom- panied the men to South Africa), Major Charles Marson, Captain and Adjutant A. Willoughby Osborne, Captains Eckersley, Vaughan, Greg, and Lloyd, Lieutenant Blyth, 2nd Lieutenants Bell and Price, and Quartermaster Huxford. A number of officers of the Bth and 40th Regimental Districts and the Warrington Volunteers (including Major Edelsfen, commanding officer) were also in attendance. Lieutenant-Colonel HALL, on behalf of the sub- scribers, requested the Mayor to unveil the screen and tablet, and the Rector to accept them on behalf of the church officers, and to preserve them in years to come. The MAYou then unveiled the memorial, and after- wards delivered a short speech, in which he expressed extreme regret at the cause of the absence of the Bishop of Liverpool. He was glad the men who fought for their country and gave up their lives in the service of the Empire had not been forgotten by their comrades, and that such a beautiful memorial had been erected.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR said he accepted the memorial on behalf of the church officers with the profoundest appreciation. It was a beautiful and costly gift, and he felt sure the church officers would regard it as a sacred deposit and would preserve it with the greatest care. It wouldadd immensely to the many touching associations connected with that old Lady Chapel erected more than live hundred years ago by the great Boteler DEAN DARBY, of Chester, then offered .a dedicatory prayer, followediby the collect for All Saints' Day. Spohr's beautiful anthem " Blessed are the departed" was rendered by the choir, and afterwards a bugler sounded" The Last Post."</p>
        <p>An address was then delivered by Dean Darby who took for his text the lwords " Ye shall hear of wars and rumours of wars ; see that ye be not troubled,"Matthew xxiv. 6. He said the first note which must be struck from that pulpit was one of profound disappointment. Everyone within those sacred walls must deeply regret charge fact that the right reverend prelate who had crge of the diocese was not present, and they still more re- gretted the cause. They knew that he lay in suffering and that he would have been with them, had it not been for that. Expounding the text, the preacher said the [rejection of Christ must bring disaster. God could use ' the armies of this world to work His will of destruction upon those who would not shield themselves •in His faith and fear. Wars and rumours of wars followed as a necessity from the conflict between right and wrong. The justification as the cause of war was the eternal opposition between God and the prince of this world—the usurper who had claimed to rule over the hearts of men when he could win them from the one and only God. Patriotism—the love of the nation in which God in His providence had placed us—was not and could not be contrary to the love of God. Let them set Him before them always and stand on the side of right, and though they might be called by God Himself to unsheath the sword they would be doing right, because they were doing battle for the God of Justice. Questions of right and justice must arise between nations. God condemned motives which led tb war—such as ambition, lust of power and riches, tyranny, the grinding down of those beneath us,wishing 'to put down all beneath our feet,but He did not condemn the effort to redress wrong by force The only justification for war was the endeavour to re- dress wrong. The wrong wrought by one authority was the justification of the force used by the other. They of the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment were called to resist tyranny, hypocrisy of the deepest dye,selash and reck- less defiance of right, and they responded to the call. They could justly lay claim to have gone out not merely as patriots in defence of a nation's right, but as soldiers of God to put down wrong in this world which belonged to Him. They now gathered in memory of those Rho laid down their lives in that cause. Many of those pre- sent endured much hardness as good soldiers,—much privation, constant danger, anxious watching,—as they kept the lines of communication open. That day they joined in dedicating a memorial, that in generations to come their comeides might still live inn nation's grati- tude, and as recognized in the Church of the living God as men who laid down their lives to serve His 'cause. Let them, asi :they dedicated the memorial to those who had gone from amongst them, con- secrate themselves to the glory of God, that they might live in Him to His honour, serving their generation for the time that He gave them here, and hoping through the merits of the one Saviour of the world and His mercy to stand before the Throne and rejoin those who had passed hence in glory that was eternal.</p>
        <p>The hymn "The Sln of God goes forth to war was then sung., and Canon Penrhyn pronounced the Benedic- tion. The singing of the National Anthem concluded the service, and the procession returned to the west door. Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., who ably presided at the organ, playing a voluntary. Afterwards Lieutenant-Colonel Hall held a reception at the Lion Hotel, and light refreshments were served.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>At the Winmarleigh Cafe on Thursday evening, the annual meeting of the Warrington and District Band of Hope Union was held. Mr. T. Dale (chairman of the executive committee) presided. Mr. F. Gandy (the hon. secretary) read the report of the Executive Committee, which was very encouraging, several of the affiliated societies returning an increased membership. The auditors' report was read, showing the affairs of the Union to be in a very sound financial condition. At this point, the meeting took the form of an open conference of Band of Hope workers and the Rev. J. F. Matthews presided. An interesting address was de- livered by Mr. Harold Holgate, of Withington, entitled "The three stages of temperance belief." The speaker revieWed the temperance question from the p•int of view of "self," the nation" and "humanity." He quoted statistics showing that the quantity of drink consumed per head was very much greater than it was 20 or 30 years ago and the need for temperance work was accordingly very much more urgent. The drinking habits of the people were injuring the manhood of the nation. The physique- was deteriorating, especially among the working-classes, and there was scarcely a family that had not some relation or friend who was a victim to alcohol.</p>
        <p>IDuring the evening, songs were rendered by Mr. Corrin and Mr. Spruce. Mr. F.Podmore was the accom- panist. Refreshments were partaken of, after whidh the annual meeting was resumed, *hen the officers were elected for the ensuidg year, as follows :—President, Mr. R. A. Naylor; vice-presidents, Revs. J. S. Hughes„ J. Yonge, H. Dininico, J. Gardham-Reed, G. H.Carnson, I and R. Wilkins Rees, Miss Naylor, Miss Bessie Dutton Miss Whitfield, Councillor W. Brough, 111essrs. J. A. Hutchin, E. Lloyd Jones, H. Taylor. A. 111ounfield, and A. Scriven; treasurer, Mr. NV. H. Sherburn; hon. secretary, Mr. F. Gandy; hon. assistant secretaries, Messrs. H. Woolley and R. E. Jones; auditors, Messrs. R. Pierce Williams and G. Whitfield; representatives to the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union, Messrs. R. Pierce Williams and Joseph Owen; con- ductor, Mr. A. Spruce; accompanist, Mr. F. Podmore ; organising and visiting committee, Messrs. R. E. Jones, Joseph Owen and J. W. Roberts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MR. COX AND TARIFFS</head>
        <p>MR. COX AND TARIFFS</p>
        <p>Mr. Cox, who had a hearty reception, said he had specially selected the iron and steel trade for discussion in relation to Mr. Chamberlain's proposals, because in the first place he knew something about the trade and in the next place, because his Wearers knew something of the trade and could follow better arguments based upon theirltrade. (Hear, hear,) in the third place he had chosen to speak upon it because it was a peculiar trade, specially selected by the advocates of protection in this country to illustrate what the— termed the evileof "dumping." He was only going to -deal with the proposals of Mr. Chamberlain, because he recognized that they were really the only proposals before the country. Mr. Chamberlain ad- mitted that we must suffer a tax upon food and pro- posed a 2s. duty on corn, a five per cent duty on meat, and a ten per cent, duty on manufactured goods. He promised—so far as any man could promise—that that 2s. tax on food should not be exceeded. But when once they began taxation of a protective character no one knew where it would end. They knew how the small tax in France and Germany had grown, and no one could guarantee that a tax on food would not grow here. Mr. Chamberlain promised them remissions which would counter-balance the tax on food, but they were duties which they ought to have taken off without any bargain as they were put on to pay the expenses of the South African war. It was absolutely impossible for any statesman in this -country to find a system of preferential tariffs for the Colonies with- out taxing a great deal of raw material. He wanted to reason this matter out. He did not believe in appealing to the passions and prejudices of the working men, but in appealing to their reason. Mr. Chamberlain had stated that if they would only agree to his proposals he would guarantee that there would be such an increase in their wages as would more than compensate them for the extra cost of living. A VOICE; He means the Irishman's rise. (Laugh- ter,) _</p>
        <p>Mr. Cox (continuing) said he-knew something about wages, and he could not think that any employer of labour would listen to him for a moment if he went to the office and claimed an increase of wages because the price of bread and meat had gone up. (Laughter.) The wages question was not settled on sentimental lines. or from itay motives of nhilanthropy. The cost of living would not enter fora moment in the consideration of wages by the master. Instead of high prices on com- modities raising wages, they found it did just the reverse. Mr. Cox proceeded to quote from the figures issued by the Inland Revenue officers, showing the profits in the iron and steel trades. This return showed that while the profits in these trades were £1,840,350 in 1896-7, they had risen to £3,211,984 in 1900. The figures for last year showed that the profits were £6,600,263. A great deal had been said about iron and steel imports. Our imports of pig iron in 1902 were as follows :—From Sweden 56,842 tons at £5 6s. per ton; United States, 10,701 tons it £5 lls, per ton; Canada, 51,041 tons at £2 ss. ; other countries 155,595 tons at '£2 13s. The iron from Sweden and America was a speciality and could not be produced in this country. Our exports, on the other hand of pig iron alone were 1,102,8:35 tons at an average price of £3 4s. 9d. Orton. (Hear,hear.) With regard to unwrought steel, whilst .we imported 281,013 tons at £5 Os. Bd. per ton we exported 301,331 tons at £9 us. 4d. per ton. Taking the statistics for bars in the same period they found that whilst the im- ports were 171,915 tons at a 6s. 2d. the exports were 152,694 tons (steel) at £ll us. 4d. and 125,063 tons (iron) at £8 4s. 3d. (Applause.) The transition from iron to steel was still going on in this country and iron was gradually going down whilst steel was coming into more general use. Tariffs could not pre- vent that transition from taking place and as a matter of fact the transition had been slower in this country than in any other country in the world. Do not let them suppose that the iron itrade was absolutely dead. Iron' was becoming more and more a speciality—a speciality dependent on its quality—and iron would continue to he used as long as there was a puddler to puddle it. The argument that tariffs would prevent im- ports was utterly fallacious. Despite the high tariffs in America and Germany, the imports of manufactured goods were increasing. Mr. Cox proceeded to demon- strate that the "dumping" of raw material or semi- raw materiathad benefited our manufacturers by en- abling theta to beat their competitors in the open market with their finished products. The position of affairs in Germany had become so serious that a com- mission of inquiry had been instituted. The German wire-nail industry complained that it had now to struggle against the competition of foreign manu- facturers,who got steel billets and rolled wire'from Ger- many at prices lower than it was possible for the German manufacturers to obtain the same articles. One very striking result of the "dumping" policy of the German Kartells was that by supplying manufacturers abroad with material at low prices the German Syndicates made it possible for these foreigners to compete on very favourable terms with their rivals in Germany in regard to the sale of finished products. Mr. Chamberlain ''had stated that'under protection'wages would increase,but the experience of Germany did not bear this out. German manufacturers being obliged to pay more for their materials than the foreigner covered themselves by cutting wages. With regard to American competition, he was not afraid of that great bubble—the American Billion Dollar Steel Trust.' A Corporation whose 100 dollar shares were being hawked about the market and offered for 10 dollars was not going to hurt us very much. Dealing with the tinplate industry, Mr. Cox admitted that the Dingly tariff had ii temporary effect upon our exports, but the tinplate trade had more than recovered itself. Last year our tinplate exports were 71,175,980 boxes, the highest point, he believed, yet touched. That did not represent the whole of our tin- plate trade, because whilst our home consumption in 1888 was about two million boxes, last year it was over four million boxes. Since 1896 there was an increase of 79 tinplate mills, the number having increased from 318 mills to 397. if that was an evidence of what had been called a decaying industry, he would like to see similar signs in regard to every other trade in this country. (Laughter.) A great deal of nonsense was taiked about percentages and he was surprised at sensible men using bogies of this kind. Did they expect. an old established business to grow in the same ratio as a little business which had just been started? If a business doing a turnover o f £20,000 a year increased its output by £2,000, they had an increase of ten per cent. but when a small business with a turnover of Zl,OOO increased it by another £l,OOO, they had an increase of 100 per cent. Was there anyone foolish enough to suppose that this one hundred per cent. was better than the ten per cent. he had mentioned? To illustrating the subject of imports and exports, Mr. Cox asked his hearers to consider the result of their work as exports and their wages as imports. He had never heard a man grumble about his imports, or wages being too high. (Laughter.) A tax on imports must of necessity mean a tax on food. At present many of them had a difficulty in making both ends meet, but what were they going to do if they put a tax on im- ports? Thousands of honeist, hard-headed people were working from morning to night to earn a bare liveli- hood, and a few weeks "play" or illness kit them in poverty. They were now asked to tax their food for the benefit of Colonials who were richer than ourselves. •</p>
        <p>J.</p>
        <p>By paying almost the entire cost of the army and navy, the British people were already making sacrifices for the Colonies and he did not think they were prepared to go further by a tax on food. Amongst those who were supporting Mr. Chamberlain's policy were some manufacturers who thOught they saw in it a means of creating, a vested interest and a monopoly for their trade. If they could succeed in that these manu- facturers did not care who else sank or swam. (Hear, hear). America was crying out for a revision of the tariff laws, but during the past 13 years the vested interests and monopolists had grown so strong and powerful that the Government dare not touch the question. President Rooseveldt had been. trying hard to grapple with the question of the trusts, but the Republicans, who brought the tariffs into existence were bound hand and foot to the monopolists. What happened in Germany and America would happen in this country under Protection. What Britain wanted was not protection, but cheaper freights, and revised royalties and way-leaves. (Hear, hear.) In conclusion, Mr. Cox asked his hearers not to take a retrograde step, such as that proposed by Mr. Chamberlain, but to remove the obstructions in tke path of Free Trade by insisting upon free trade in the minerals and transport of this country. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS. Mr. 3. 'ELWELT„ jun., proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Cox and remarked that Mr.. Cox had put them in possession of a good deal of information that they did not hitherto possess. Mr. 11GE, in seconding, said the time had gone by for them to be the manufacturers for the whole world. Other countries were now manufactuAng for themselves and the figures given showed that we were doing very well. (Hear, hear.) If ironworkers found that the iron trade was diminishing, they must turn their attention to the manufacturers of steel. (Hear, hear.) The vote was carried unanimously. Mr. Cox acknowledged the compli nent, and proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. Mr. Blum{ seconded and said those who remembered the bad times under protection did not want to go back to :them. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>s 7 Alderman PIERPOINT acknowledged the compliment and said there was something about those new fiscal pro- posals which seemed ingenuine. He hoped at the general election they would give the Protectionists such a dressing that they would bury this agitation for ever, never to revive it again. He had travelled a little in highly protected countries, and had a fair knowledge of French peasant life and German workman life, and had had conversations with the people. They envied the working man his condition in England. (Appla'use.) We had prospered under Free Trade still he would like to see the English working man do better, and he would do better if he had more sense--(laughter)—and exercised more discretion in spending his money. But if they did not mind, their condition would go from bad to worse, and they would find V- ey had less to eat and less to drink and it would serve them right. Let them take to heart what Mr. Cox had said and see if they could not bring about a better condition of things in Warrington. (Applause).</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. MEETING OF THE PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. A meeting of this committee was held in the National Schools on Thursday evening under the presidency of Mr. G. Pilling. There were also present :—Messrs. Clarke, Whitley, Pemberton, Mounfield, Fairhurst, Pearson, G. F. Ashton (clerk), J. Swinton (surveyor), and W. Farrington (inspector of nuisances.) PLANS FOR NEW SHOPS REJECTED. Mr. Thomas Williams submitted plans for the erec- tion of twenty-four shops in the field opposite the Post Office in Old London-road. The Surveyor stated that the plans were not in accordance with the byelaws in several respects. The Clerk said the matter had been considered at a special meeting of the Building Committee when it was resolved that the byelaws bEi adhered to in regard to the secondary means of access and the rear air space. ' Mr. Clarke said the committee looked upon that part of Stockton Heath as the most important building plot in the village and it was imperative that the byelaws should be adhered to. Considering that the road was likely to be congested in years to come it was impor- tant that all the air space possible should be allowed. Mr. Williams was informed that it was no use coming before the Parochial Committee unless the plans com- plied with the byelaws in every respect. The present plans did not, in many respects comply with the bye- laws, and he did not think the committee were being treated fairly. He proposed that the plans be not entertained.</p>
        <p>Mr. Williams and Mr. Warburton, architect, were - called into the room, and after hearing Mr.Warburton's explanations the committee decided to reject the plans.. VILLAGE IMPROVEMENTS. The Local Government, Board wrote sanctioning a loan of .£650 for the making of Dundonald Avenue and Kimberley Drive and the clerk reported that the contract had been signed. The Clerk stated that at the list meeting of the Rural District C macil ii recommendition of the com- mittee that the footpaths in Chapel-lane be paved with concrete slabs was under consideration. An estimate was submittedlby the surveyor but as it was rather high the Council decided to refer the matter back to the committee to se3 if they still recommended that the work be carried out.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor said the cost of paving with concrete slabs would be £llB whereas blue tiles would only cost £75. •</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Whitley seconded by Mr. Clarke it was decided to recommend that the footpaths be paved with concrete slabs.</p>
        <p>Good Templary. The first public tea meeting in c Inflection with the - Tower of Refuge Good Templars locig, was held in the Temperance Hall, Latchford on Thursday evening when about 60 members and friends partook of an excellent tea the t:ibles being presided over by the fol- lowing ladies :—Mrs. B. Few, Mrs. W. H. Ford, Mrs. Joseph Massey, and Miss Forster. A concert was afterwards 'held at which Mr. B. Few pre- sided The programme was contributed to by. Mr. Millwood, Mr. Birchall, Mr. Isherwood- Mr. Yates, Misses Jackson, Jones, Hough, B. Jones, Forster, Brown, Mr. R. Gifford, Mr. T. Buckley.&gt; Miss Browne was the accompanist. Councillor Ellison&gt; proposed a vote of thanks to all who had taken part and this was heartily agreed to</p>
        <p>GREAT ENGLISHMEN OF THE 16TH CESTURY.—Mr. Sydney Lee's course of Lowell Lectures on "Great Englishmen of the 16th Century," which he delivered at Boston a few months ago, are, it is now arranged, to be published next spring by Messrs. Archibald Constable and Co., In England, and by Messrs. Scribner in America. The date of publication has been postponed owing to Mr. Lee's wish to revise the text of his Lec- tures, and to embody some additional research. Besides a general introduction, the subjects to be treated in the forthcoming volume are Sir Thomas Moore. Sir Philip 'Sydney, Sir Walter Raleigh, Edmund Spenser, Francis Bacon, and Shakespeare. To Shakespeare, Mr. Lee devoted two lectures, which attracted very wide attention in_ America.</p>
