        <p>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE AriardaSTEEL FEDERATION. for Ladies.l PILLS The Rev. J. L. Milne and the Drink A FRENCH RRMHDYi PERSEDING di V TER APPLE. PIL COCHIAC, PENNYROYXL, &amp;c. Price Question. 48.6 d. post free. Obtainable only from The third public meeting of the above was held under MARTIN, CHEMIST, SOUTHAMPTON the auspices of the Bonehill Branch Lodge of the 1.0.G.T. in the Temperance Hall, Knatsford-road, Latchford, on Monday. There was a good attendance. FANCY MEETING YOU Th e chair was occupiedby Councillor A. Horrocks,who Just in time to tell you that HOLDROYD‘S Gravel Pills wa s supported by the Rev. J. L. Milne. The CITA I ItMA in his opening remarks said it was are a guaranteed and positive cure for Lumbago, Back- theN right and bounden duty of every man and woman ache, Wind, Dropsy, Gout,Sciatica, Kidney Diseases. etc. who was a member of a church of whatever denomina- Try one box, is. lid., of all Chemists. Your money tion to uphold the Temperance question. Drink was promptly returned *if not satisfled.—HOLDßOYD'S the root of all evil, and it behoved everyone who pro- Medical Hall. Cleckheaton, Yorks. Post free, 12 stamps. fessed Christianity to stand by the side of temperance. In his experience, it made his heart bleed when he thought of children, whose parents were drunkards, who were starved, ill-clad, beaten and otherwise neglected. He had begun to look with despair on the Government, magistrates, and local rulers united with the temper- ance workers. The drink trade was supported by so many members of the Government and the bench that it was nearly useless to try and get a license abolished. Even when they had a magistrate to stand on the temperance question, he was boycotted and called a madman. He thought the only way to solve the problem of the drink curse was for people to be sensible and close their mouths to intoxicating liquor. That day had not come yet, but it would come if people would be firm. (Applause.) The Rev. J. L. MILNE, who was heartily received, said he was practically new to temperance work. When he left college, he was sent to China, and there was little er no temperance work needed there. (Hear, hear.) Some memories were aroused when he read the bill announcing the meeting. On it, he saw the letters 1.0.G.T., and remembered that some 20 years ago, he ' was a member of a Good Templar Lodge. (Applause.) For sometime he filled the onerous duties of Chaplain. At the time he was in hopes of joining the army and not the ministry. He did not think there was much hope of rescuing the drundard, although the Good Templars took this as one of their chief aims. He thought the best way was to get hold of those who did not drink in- toxicating liquor and keep them out of the way of temptation. In all his experience, he had not come across a town so bad as Warrington for drink. He sometimes wished there was a body in the town like the women of America who went about " smashing " the saloons. The drink question was more important than any fiscal policy. If the nation were sober, there would be no need for free trade agitations as English- men would be better able to compete with foreigners. A few days ago, a Liverpool brewery paid to their shareholders a dividend of 20 per cent. He was sure a curse was on that money, tainted as it was, with the blood of innocent children. While in China, he had seen, on the waters of the rivers, the bodies of babies who had been thrown there by their fathers simply be- cause they were too poor to support them. He had more respect for these men than for the man who starved his children to satisfy his craving for strong drink. (Applause). Let temperance- workers take a hint from the drink trade, and organise their forces. If all classes of temperance workers were to combine, and work hand in hand, the drink traffic would in time be curbed. (Applause). During the evening Miss E. Littlewood very effec- tively sang" Charity." A hearty vote of thanks to the chairman, speaker and soloist was moved by Mr. H. Blease, seconded by Councillor Ellison, which was unanimously carried. KILLED BY THE WIND. A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned at the inquest at Heysham, Morecambe, on Monday, upon William Lucas, a workman employed at the Midland Railway Company Harbour works, Heysham, who met his death in the act of starting a wagon. Whilst in a stoopingposition with his head against the buffer, the wind caused another wagon behind him no start. Before he realised his danger his head was jammed between...the two buffers, crushing it to a pulp. LANCASHIRE'S NEW MAGISTRATES. The following gentlemen were on Monday placed upon the commission of the peace for the County Pala- tine of Lancaster by the County Sealkeeper at Preston, acting under instructions from the Chancellor of the Duchy:—John Greville Earle, Childwall Lodge, Waver- tree, Liverpool, merchant seed crusher; Robert Glad- stow, Woolton Vale, Liverpool, esquire ; Richard Darning Holt, Field House, Prince Alfred-road, Waver- tree, Liverpool, shipowner; Washington Champion Rawling Calgarth, 6, Victoria-road, Waterloo,Liverpool, ' esquire; George Rolls, The Park, Waterloo, Liverpool, shipbuilder and engineer; and Harvey Cecil Woodwara, Northway House, Seaforth, esquire. TRUSS,—New washable watc,p.-aot is the most'suitable in tropical climates ; it is light, clean, cool. comfnrtable, and durable ; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITE &amp; WRIGHT surgical Instru-a ent tilakers.93.Renshaw at,Liverpool—Adv t For the past quarter of a c,Qntury there has barn continuou flow of letters bearing testimony to the truly wondorful curet effected by CLARKE'S WORLD .FAMED BLOOD MIXTURE, "the finest Blood Purifier that science and medical skill have brought to light." Sufferers from Scrofula Scurvy. Eczema, Bad Legs. Skin and Blood Diseases. Pimples and Sores of any kind. are solicited to give it a trial to try its value. Sold everywere at 2s 9d per bottle, beware of worthless imitations andsub